      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>AN ODD GENIUS—A genius who is not odd. Tint neighbourly feeling of which we hear so much is usually one of curiosity and envy. Jr is said that "Love laughs at locksmiths," but it shows the most profound respect for goldsmiths. Iv is a wise child who goes out of the room to laugh when the father hits himself a thump on finger with the hammer. " Buommer, I hear you are going to start house- keeping?" "Yes, Darlinggor. "What have you got towards it ?" "A wife." "THE brightest thins in a conversation," says a philosopher, "are the things one doesn't think of saying until it is too late to say them." QUEER TASTE. —Farmer Way back: "Well, of all the fools, that artist feller takes the cake." Mrs. Wayback : "What's he doin' ?" Farmer Wayback : "He's down yonder paintin' a picture of that old tumble-down barn, and there's a brand- mew barn right behind him." ALWAYS STUDYING SOMETHING.—Wife: "Do you believe that Edison has discovered a way to do without sleep r Husband : "Might be. Perhaps he has been investigating a baby.' BY ALL MnaNs.—Hazel : "Sandstone has asked me tc lend him five dollars, and I don't know whether to do it or not. Would you ?" Nutte -(earnestly): "I would, old man. He invited me to dine with him this evening." THE sermon of the best preacher in the ivorld -will not make so much impression upon a congre- gation as- the sudden pattering of rain on the windowpanes of a church containing 200 new summer bonnets. AN INFERENCE ONLY.—" Do you believe in the transmigration of souls'!' asked Mizer. "Yes. Don't you?" said Hicks. "Sometimes. What do you suppose I was before I became a man'!" "Oh, I don't know. A sponge,'l guess." WHAT WE ARE COMING To.—First Citizen: "So you think there is no danger to be appre- hended from foreign powers ?" Second Citizen: "None at all. We have let so many Anarchists and -dynamite cranks into the country that not even the Czar of Russia would care to own us."</p>
        <p>LITTLE DOT: "Oh, mamma, there's a sign— 'Puppies for sale.' Won't you buy me one?" Mamma " Wait till you are s. little older, dear." Little Dot: "But they'll all be dogs then !" ETHEL : "Have you noticed how, much merrier Blanche has grown lately? She used to be so very quiet and reserved—hardly open her mouth. She's quite a different girl." Maud : " Yes, dear, she's got a new set of teeth, you know." "SEE that man?" said one of the two people who were talking about success in life. "Yes." "He has left behind lots of people who struggled to overtake him." "Who is hel" "Conductor on an omnibus."</p>
        <p>FIRST Deaf Mute (speaking on his fingers): "What chance is there for you and me in the business world, I'd like to know." Second Deaf Mu.e : "Plenty, plenty. Let's start .a barber's shop." WHY THEY SETTLE. —Wife : "Who can doubt the power of woman's love ! Think of the thou- sands of wild youths who have settled down into staid and respectable citizens as soon as they mar- ried." Husband : "Goodness! they couldn't afford to be anything else after they got married."</p>
        <p>How IT WORKS. —Primus : Your theory about •moral suasion wi:h children is pretty enough, but have you ever known it to work ? ' Secundus: " W y, ye ; I've never had to strike a child of mine sitv in self-defence."</p>
        <p>--Too-Nltatn.—He : "Why, this dog knows as much a Ido !" She : " ILn't you think a sove- reign was a big price to pay for him ?" EXPLAINING A MYSTERY. MPS. Nell Wed : "Charley, love, why was a )alain gold ring adopted as the symbol of marriage ? ' Charley: "Because by the time a fellow pays all the expenses incident to an average courtship he is not able to buy any- thing more elaborate." A LITTLE fellow had been seriously lectured by his mother, and finally sent into the garden to find a switch with which he was to he punished. He returned soon, and said : "I coutdn't find a switch, mamma; but here's a stone you can throw at me." _</p>
        <p>...„„ MRS. BICKFORD (who has secreted several pills in the jelly) : "Now, let mamma's :ittle girl run here, and she shall have something nice." A moment later little Ethel cried, triumphantly : "Here, mamma, is the seeds I Wasn't I a good girl not to swallow them ?" IT is a very lazy man who will not take the trouble to reverse his cigar when he finds that he has put the lighted end of it by mi.:take into his mouth.</p>
        <p>"TIIESE eggs are quite fresh, I suppose?" said a lady to an old negro who came to her kitchen door with eggs to sell. "Fraish, lady?" was the reply. "'Deed dey is, ma'am. De fact is, lady, my hens nebbah lays none but fraish eggs." YJUNG WIFE: "Bridget, you have been in the labit of boiling the potatoes peeled? Bridget: Yis, mum." Young wife: Well, when the cold weather come's you'd better boil them with the jackets on?" PAT (doing look-out duty for the first time) : "Hubo !" Officer (on the bridge) : " What is it, Pat?" Pat: " Shure an' Oi dunno." Officer: "Well, what do you see? " Pat: "Oi see a rid an' a grane bight, an' Oi think it must be a dhrug shtore." GIVING A FREE PRESCRIPTION.—Tramp: "I jus' dropped in, mum, to oiler my new cure for in- digest'ion, dyspepsia, and kindred ailments, mum. It may prove a great blessing to your family, mum, and I charge you nothing for the perscription." Housekeeper: " Well, I must say that's reason- . able enough. What is the cure ?" Tramp: "Live on plain food, and give your rich and indigestible dishes to the poor. Pm the poor, mum." A DREAM or TIIE FUTERE.—Some day a great genius will develop. He will invent a double- barrelled alarm-clock that will go off a second time after any desired interval. After the first rude half-awakening a man can enjoy the best part of any night's sleep—that of not having to get up right away—in the serene half-conscious confidence ! of the certainty of not oversleeping himself. SCENE: A refreshment bar. Uncle (who left hie nephew in the bar "refreshing ") : "Well, Tommy, you see I'm back; are you ready? What have Ito pay, miss ?" Miss: Three buns, four sponge-cakes, two sandwiches, one jelly, five tarts, and—" Uncle : "Good gracious' boy; are you not ill?" Tommy : "No, uncle; but I'm awful thirty." WOMAN'S WAY.—It was a dreadful story, the latest scandal out. That Mr. Brown sat down to tell young Mrs. Brown about. She listened to it, eager, till all of it she heard—Then cried out. " Stop ! Ido not care to hear another word !" AN ALTERNATIVE.—Master (to coachman) : "John, just go down to the well and draw some water for Bridget." John: "I was engaged to drive the horses and not to draw water, sir." M,ster : "Oh, well, just get the horses and carriage out and take Bridget to the well then." "I THINE you should make a reduction in my bill for giving me that horse," said Jack Dasher to the liveryman of whom he had hired a horse and sleigh to drive out his girl. " Why, that's the fastest and most spirited horse in my stable," said the liveryman. "That's just what I object to; I had to use both my hands to drive." "Yes, brethren," says the clergyman who is preaching the funeral seraion, "our deceased brother was cut down in a single night—torn from the arms of his loving wife, who is thus left a dis- •consolate widow at the early age of 24 years." " Twenty-two, if you please," sobs the widow in the front pew, emerging troni her handkerchief for ,an instant. " WALE up," shnnted a showm:n, "and inspect this curious phenomenon—a real live woman-fish, the only one in existence The place was -crammed. The curtain rose, and a young woman, -dressed all in black, appeared on the stage and said : " Ladies and gentiem.,:., I am the woman. fish. There's my marriage eertifi ate. My hus- band's name was Fish. He was ki led last sum- quer through a fall off a scadoldinT, and he has 14t -me with four children unprovided for, I will take the liberty to ga round and make a collection on their behaif." A REASONABLE DEnnerieN. —Jack Cutter: 4‘ Your office is quite near mine. Your hours are -from nine till four, are they not ? ' Tom Bigbee: " Yes ; how did you know?" Jack Cutting: "Oh, I see you going to work about ten and coming home about three every day.'</p>
        <p>'TRIM) AN PROV.e) ! Sufferers from Gr, Lumbago. Pie, Pains in the Back, Drepu. Wind and Water Complaint, Disaser rf Kidneys Blf.ad,r Stone, Sciatica, Ithf umatism an+l Gout will find 05 +ire Dore in liolroTri'+ Gravel Pi l. Try a smallbox and not ,aMslied our moue+, will be returned. all Chemists, or pou, free for 12 stamps. from HOLROYI Cheekheaton, York. Do be put off. canny. g tiin write to the Pr •prietor and a box will lu (.4 next</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>DISAGREEABLE GRASS.</head>
        <p>DISAGREEABLE GRASS.</p>
        <p>In some parts of the New Mexico there grows a grass which produces a somniferous effect on the animals that graze upon it. Horses, after eating the grass, in nearly all cases, sleep standing, while cows and sheep almost in- variably lie down. It nas occasionally happened that travellers have stopped to allow horses to feed in places where the grass grows pretty thickly, and the animals have had time to eat a considerable quantity before its effects manifested themselves. In such cases horses have gone to sleep on the road ,and it was hard to arouse them. The effect of the grass passes off in an hour or two, and no bad results have ever been noticed on ac- count of it. Cattle on the ranches frequently come upon patches of this grass, where they feed for perhaps half- an-hour, and then fall asleep for an hour or more,when they wake up and start feeding again. The programme is repeated perhaps a dozen times, until thirst obliges th...m to go to water. Whether, like the poppy, the grass contains opium, or whether its sleep-producing property is due to some other substance, is not known.</p>
        <p>the I- have had time te- eat</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>HEALTH AND COLOUR.</head>
        <p>HEALTH AND COLOUR.</p>
        <p>Whistler's yellow, room may have been artistic, but it could not have been healthy. Yellows are best suited for halls or vestibules, but should pot be used extensively in the dwelling. Being non-absorbents of light, and act- ing as strong reflectors these colours will be found parti- cularly trying to weak eyes, besides affecting the brain and the spirits, and creating a distinct disturbance of the nervous organism. On the other band, we are told, buff and creams are recommended for the nursery and children's sleeping rooms, because of their tonic quali- ties. They are better than white, which is apt to di a- turb the young optic nerves by its brilliancy, and are calculated to impart a cheerful but not too boisterous temperament as well as a good complexion to the young- sters. For the library or workroom there is no colour like coffee. Coffee, in fact, is the most soothing thing in the colour world, it seems. It induces a most plea- sant nirvana feeling such as nothing else does ; it con- duces to habits of speculative thought; it produces a philosophical calm, and is eminently successful as a mild incentive to literary efforts. Indeed, there seems to be some delicate and abstruse connectien between coffee as a beverage and coffee as a colour for domestic decoration. The coolest of all colours is red—not the flaring glaring red, but the dull Pompeian red and terracotta. These are cool because of their peculiarly absorbent qualities. It is almost impossible to light or warm them, and while the cool white room of the Wordsworthian poem is all very nice to read about, and does at first sight impress one as being such, it does not compare in cool- ness with one in dull red. It is impossible, however, that a terracotta town would be considerably cooler than a whitewashed one. If so,the old Pompeians were right when they cooled off their atria and patios by painting the town red. The only other colour which approaches Pompeian red for coolness, is dark green, but it has been found to have so depressing a mental effect on most people that it is very sparingly used. It was not by accident, we are told by Max Nordau In his now famous work on " Degeneration," that violet was chosen by many nations as the exclusive colour for mourning, and by us also as half mourning. Painters suffering from hysteria and neurasthenia will lee inclined to cover their pictures uniformly with the colour most in accordance with their condition of lassitude and ex- haustion. Thus originate the violet pictures of Manet and his school, which spring from no actually observ- able aspect of nature, but from a subjective view due to conditions of tho nerves. 'When the entire surface of walls in salons and art exhibitions of the day appears veiled in uniform half mourning, this predilection for violet is simply an expression of the nervous debility of the painter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>STAGNATED ENERGIES.</head>
        <p>STAGNATED ENERGIES.</p>
        <p>" I have lived long enough," says an eminent clergy- man, " to learn that the secret ot happiness is never to allow one's energies to stagnate."</p>
        <p>_ It's easy talking. Doctor, but we have to allow some things to happen whether we will or not. When a man hasn't a morsel to eat, and can't get food anywhere, he is obliged to " allow " himself to go hungry. And " energies" come from something—are dependent en something. When that something fails them the ener- gies stsgnate, in spite of all the doctors in the world— theological or medical. You can't stir up your energies i as you stir meal and water—with a stick. Hear a little testimony on the point : A lady residing at Manchester—who, for private reasons does not wish her naive published—writes as follows. We ougt,t to mention, however, that Mr. Thomas Evans, Chemist,24, Manchester-road, Burnley, who sends us the particulars, will vouch for the accuracy of the statement and re-</p>
        <p>spond te any inquiriese Speaking of a personal experience dating back to Oct- ober, 1887, the lady says, " I had not the least energy everything being a trouble to me." In other words, tier energies stagnated—a state of things which the clergyman thinks one ought not to allow to happen. Perhaps when we hear her explana- tion we may conclude the clergyman doesn't know as much about how the body and mind are fastened to. gether as he fancies he does. " At that time, " continues the lady. " my nervous system had been subjeated to a great strain. I became languid and weary ; I waS always tired. I had no ielish. for food of any kind, and what little I ate disagreed with me, giving me pain and distress at the chest and sides. No matter how much I dieted myself the pain did not abate. There was also an empty, gnawing sensation at the pit of the stomach which nothing seemed able to re- lieve. I was also troubled with pain and palpitation of the heart, and lost much sleep at night in conse- quence. " Later on I became very excitable and sensitive to outward influences. The least noise unnerved me. Ai time went on I grew weaker and could barely walk shout. The doctor's treatment failed to relieve me, and I became very anxious and discouraged. In November, 1889, a friend of mine living at Chorlton-cum-Hardy, near Manchester, told me of the benefit he had derived from a medicine popularly advertised as Mother Seigel's Cnrative Syrup, and advised me to try it. I did so, and after having taken three bottles of this preparation, all the pain left me and I was completely cured. Since that time (now nearly three years ago) I have been strong and in the best of health. Had I known of, and used, this medicine at the outset, I should have been saved two years of suffering. (Dated) Urmston, near Manchester, October 10th, 1892." _</p>
        <p>_ If our clerical friend (above alluded to) please, we would remark that energy is compounded of physical and mental health or rather, is an outgrowth of it When one is radically ill he is incapable of any continued effort ; and any effort at all is made agaMst Nature's protest and at heavy expense to the man who makes it. There is nothing under the sun so good for the energies and to prevent, or cure, indigestion and dyspepsia as Mother Seis.,,el's Curative Syrup. Keep up the fire and the kettle will boil. Fruit grows on trees, not on the tip of a conjurer's ward. Nature has but one way of doing things. It is good to be ambitious and pushing, but the stomach commonly decides what amount of success will come of it. If the "D.D." please (again) the secret of happiness lies in a perfect digestion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>IRISH PRIESTS AND THE ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>IRISH PRIESTS AND THE ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>EXCITING SCENE IN A CHURCH. On Sunday an incident of an extraordinary character occurred in the chapel it Kill, county Meath. The officiating priest was the Rey. Father M'Donnell, who, it will be remembered, took a prominent part in the Meath election of 1892. the proceedings at which afterwarda formed the subject of a petition. Having announced the regulations of the week, Father M'Donnell, from the altar, proceeded to say that as regarded the polling on Tuesday he would like to explain the seriousness of hay- lag a vote. It was a very serious weapon, and, as they knew, their children had not got it, as they might not have discretion how to use it. They all knew the side he had espoused in this contest. In Mr. Jordan (Nation- alist) they haa a very proper representative. At this stage Mr. Paul Larkin rose at a little distance from the altar rails, and said :-1 would die in defence of my religion and my faith, but I decline to listen to you misleading the people. Father M'Donnell : Sit down. Mr. Larkin.—l am here, and I defy any man, woman, or child, in the county to point to one discreditable act of my life. I fought Ribbonisrn, I fought tar dlordism, and I am here now to fight clericalism. Father M'Donnell again told Mr. Larkin to sit lown. Mr. Larkin said :—" We will 'have no politics. Give us the gospel of the day." Father M'Donaell said he would give it to them at second Mass. At this point somebody in the chnrch cried out. " Hurrah for Parnell." Others said, "Take your orange man back to the North ; we don't want him here. Father M'Donnell again asked Mr. Larkin to sit down while be pave his opinion. Mr. Larkin said he might go on and finish the Mass or deal with the gospel, but that he would listen to no politics. After some further argument Father M'Donneliflnished the Mass. The congregation dispersed quietly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>EXA</head>
        <p>THE EXA</p>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>'øy</p>
        <p>MY DEAREST NELLIE,</p>
        <p>Most of this season's hats are exceedingly be. coming. Both straw hat and bonnet shapes are dyed in distinct shades. Those in a burnt tint are usually becoming; they trim prettily with black feathers and ribbons, combined with roses shaded from pink to damask. The sailor hat shape is one of the -first favourites; it is sometimei modestly trimmed with a plain ribbon band, and again it bunts forth bedecked with chiffon and flowers. Bonnets are still quite small in size, and fit closely on to the head. The shapes are covered with a network of beads or lace, mounted on to a coloured lining, the trimming arranged to set out widely at the sides both at the front and back. ,A pretty bat is made in green and cinnamon tinted straw, trimmed with shot glace ribbon and shaded foliage.</p>
        <p>Ipeis theaeati:</p>
        <p>the huge sleeves anct wide plain skirts, the distinctive feature of this season's fashions is the craze for lace and all sorts of showy trimmings and Persian bands. White in some form seems to be really necessary to the success of the fashionable gown, and so one must wear a bertha of lace or muslin whethek it is becoming or not. Laces of all kinds, real and imitation, are made into old-fashioned berthas, bretelles and cellars, but the, fad of the hour is for lace insertion on gowns and silk waists. Tiny niches are made of the narrow lace edge by gathering it quite full and putting two rows together on lengthwise and ending them with little rosettes of lace. Black satin is being used a great deal for skirts and sleeves. Milliners' folds of silk or velvet are revived for dress trimmings, and make an effective skirt decoration set on either side of a band of lace insertion around the bottom.</p>
        <p>I saiv a remarkable neat hat and ...louse at a tennis party the other day. The former, which was shady and turned'up in front with ribbons, was made of wired net, with a plissé Chiffon brim. and the trimming was a four-looped bow and a trail of roses' and buds. The blouse was of grass cloth, trimmed with fine embroidery and insertion. Another blouse, an evening one, which I think you would like, was in ivory satin, the square neck outlined with pearl passementerie, and the front draped with strings of pearls and crystals, hanging from the neck, and just caught in at the waist, so that they fall lightly, but do not sway about like an ordinary deep fringe. The design would look well in any delicate-tinted satin or broche, with pearl or iridescent beads, it is particularly hand. some and serviceable in a rich black fabric, with cut jet. An elegant evening gown had a very full skirt of black silk muslin mounted on a foundation of bright pink taffetas. At the edge of the skirt are arranged bouquets of shaded pink roses. The very low taffetas corsage, without shoulder-strap, is seamless. It is draped front and back, in rounded, horizontal pleats, with pink silk muslin spangled with gold, finished with a belt and bow of water. green satin ribbon. The puffed elbow sleeves are of green satin, draped with bouquets of roses. Another very pretty one—suitable for dinner</p>
        <p>parties—is made in black armure, of which the long straight skirt is severely plain. This is pro- vided with two bodices, of which one is cut low, with a bertha of tulle worked with jet, while the other is high, with a deep collar of paillette- sprinkled guipure ; yet another has a draped yet closely-fitting bodice with a yoke of jet. The strong brilliant colours which have been so much worn by grown-up people—there are signs, however, that these glaring tints have had their day—have, happily, not become fashionable for little girls' frocks, which, almost without excep- tion, are pale and delicate of tint, if not white. Chi.l6 zephyrs, muslins, plain, spotted, and flowered, and the pretty new grass-c 1 'th are the most popular of the so-called washing materials, while the übiquitous cr6pon, in small patterns, and alpaca, are more used for useful weather frocks than anything else, our old friend, serge, being more or less set aside until the seaside season begins. A pretty dress for a girl from eight to twelve years old is of pale grey alpaca; the skirt is plain and full, and fast.ens over the bodice under a sash of soft, white silk, which the fronts of the bodice slightly overhang. There is a very large cape collar of the alpaca, edged with a frill of the white silk, tacked lightly under the edge of the collar, so that it can be easily removed, and re- newed when soiled, and the bishop sleeves are finished at the wrist with little frills of silk. The sash fastens at the back under a large rosette. Your loving (TENNIS.</p>
