      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>INER, SATURDAY, J</head>
        <p>INER, SATURDAY, J</p>
        <p>The Queen misses her gillie, Mr. Francis Clarke, very much, as it was always his strong arm that helped her in and out of her carriage. His place of late has been taken by Mr. Rankin, another tall Highlander, and he will now continue permanently in the post. Plate that has become dull and tarnished may be made to look like new by boiling it in a mixture composed of one pound of unslacked lime, one pound of alum, one pint of vinegar' one of aqua vitte, and two quarts of beer-grounds. After being boiled in this the plate takes on a beautiful gloss. Coffee-pots are an Oriental invention,and are supposed to have come from Arabia in A.D. 1409. About the same time they were used in Persia but they did not come to France until 1662, and made their appearance with coffee in 1650. Mr. Chauncey M. Depew says he has noticed "three things about men who do not take proper rest and re- laxation. First, they become nervous and restless. Second, they become irritable and querulous, and their work does not maintain its high standard, or if it is work of the head the judgment is likely to err. In the third place, they die suddenly',"</p>
        <p>For Tight Shoes. If you are so unfortunate as to be wearing a pair of shoes that are to tight or too stiff, or uncomfortable in some way, pour a drop of alcohol on the outside of the shoe while it is on the foot. It will afford immediate relief to the aching foot. It is said that water poured into a pair of new shoes and allowed to remain for a few minutes will, by taking all the natural heat out of the leather, make them perfectly comfortable. Soles cut from several folds of brown paper and placed tresh every day in the shoes have a wonderfully soothing effect upon the feet.</p>
        <p>A Costly Wedding Gift. The ladies of Florence have subscribed twelve thousand francs for a beautiful gift to he shortly pre- sented to the young Duchess cf Aosta. It is a " giglio," or lily of Florence, composed entirely of large brilliants, which can be worn either as a diadem, with one row of brilliants resting on the hair as a brooch. The jewel has been designed and made by one of the foremost Floren- tine jewellers, and is to be given to the royal bride, to- gether with an album containing all the names of the lady subscribers.</p>
        <p>Rhubarb and Children. Rhubarb, like many other drugs, has a double action, , for in large doses it acts as a purgative, whilst in small doses it is an astringent, and cenfines the bowels. It is used largely for children—why, it is difficult to say, for nine times out of ten it does infinitely more harm than good. The horrors of the Inquisition, the greatest , enormities charged even against the vivisectors, sink into insignificance before the horrible cruelties winch are constantly inflicted on children by the forcible adminis- tration of rhubarb and other equally nauseous drugs. If the bowels are confined, it generally arises from some error in diet, or want of attention to the general princi- ples of health, and the temporary difficulty is often readily removed by giving the child a little fresh fruit or a few figs.</p>
        <p>A Throat Clearer.</p>
        <p>Glycerine of tannic acid is useful in affections of the throat. In many cases it acts like a charm. In a relaxed sore throat there is nothing better than swabbing out the throat with this substance. The patient shou'd it in a chair with his head slightly thrown back and his mouth wide open. The operator who is about to perform the friendly office for him now dips the brush in the glycerine of tannin, and when it is thoroughly covered thrusts it into the open mouth, taking good care to go right to the back of the throat. The patient generally expectorates freely and forcibly, and it is a good plan to get behind him as soon as the application is finished. A basin or other utensil should be provided for the patient to spit into. If be does not at once express his relief it is because the astringency of the preparation prevents him from uttering the words of gratitude. The tannic acid gargle may in addition be used three or four times a day. Enlargement of the tonsils may often be treated with advantage in the same way.—" The Family Physician."</p>
        <p>Women and Cricket. A few weeks ago Mrs. Grundy (or was it Mr. P) was shrieking with horror at the audacity and impropriety of girls playing football. It is not so many years ago since the same indignant howl went up on account of the lady cricketers. But the outraged feelings have subsided since then, and now we have feminine elevens all over the country. Lady Idina Brassey, says the" Queen," is one of the pioneers. In 1837 she founded the " White Heather Club," of which she is captain. Lady Evelyn Goschen , was its secretary,a post now held by Miss Street. Others of its members are Viscountess Cantelupe, Lady Henry Nevil, Lady Rose Leigh, Lady Constance Rackburn, Vicountess Marsham, Lady Sandhurst, Lady Milner, the Hon. Mrs. I'. Brand, the Hon. Mrs. Bevan-, and many others. Besides this club there are several others. Several Irish ladies are well-known cricketers,amongst others being Mrs. Saunders, and the Misses Bicknell, of King's County, Miss Maud Golching, Mrs. Darby, of I Leap Castle, and Mrs. Barnes, of Barnes Court. One of the most famous of lady cricketers is Lady Harrie, wife of the late Governor of Bom- bay, himself a great cricketer. Lady Harris's love of the game, however, dates from her girlhood. During her reside nee in India she captained numbers of elevens, made up of men and women. Lady Harris considers cricket very harmless and plea- sant, and far less fatiguing than tennis. She thinks it a, good game to be taught in girls' schools,both for phy- sical and moral grounds. It develops muscle and gives them the free nse of their limbs, whilst it also creates a feeling of esprit de corps. At Madame Otterberg's Phy- sical Training College cricket is compulsory, and at several schools throughout the country cricket has taken root. In the twentieth century the "Daily Telegraph" may perhaps be getting up a shilling testimonial for a lady W. G.</p>
        <p>The Care of Lamps. A writer in a recent issue of" The Paris Mode" gives some useful hints on this subject. " The care of lamps," says he, " is one of those domestic matters that can be entrusted with safety to experienced servants only. Where the establishment is small, and the servants are I ill-trained and slipshod in their ways, as untrained girls so often are, a wise mistress will see to the matter her- self even if she dislikes the penetrating and unpleasant odour of mineral oil. The terrible accidents from the incautious use of paraffin that are daily recorded in the newspapers, should make us all careful of our lamps The person who employs paraffin to light the fires de- serves to be immediately arrested and locked up as a danger, not only to herself, but to the public. The criminal foolhardiness of ignorant people, who do not realise the dangers they run, cannot be too strongly con- demned. Often they are lucky ; indeed, as the German proverb says, "they have more luck than sense.' Noth- ing comes, for a time, of their reckless daring • when, emboldened by this immunity from evil, and heedless of warnings, they try experiments once too often. That they seldom live to tell how it happened Is poor comfort to those whom they have, perhaps, deprived at once of health and home and those dear to them. The use of very cheap paraffin is to be deprecated, because it often gives off an explosive gas. Employing lamps of inferior construction , or lamps having glass or china receptacles for the oil, is another danger. Placing lamps within the reach of children, setting them on rickety tables, cirrying them up and down stairs when lighted—or even moving them' from one room to another—are the primary causes of most of the fatal disasters of which we read. Everyone should know that water does not ex- tinguish burning paraffin oil. It only floats it round the room, spreading flame on every side. Heavy mats satur- ated with water, sand, flour, or, if nothing better be at hand, the earth contained in geranium pats should be used. Milk is said to be very efficacious in extinguish- ing this dangerous substance when alight."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>HELMSITEY MURDER.</head>
        <p>THE HELMSITEY MURDER.</p>
        <p>HUDSON SENTENCED TO DEATH. The trial of Robert Heseltine Hudson for the murder of his wife and child on Roper Moor, near Helmsley, on the Bth June last, took place at York Assizes on Tuesday before Mr. Justice Mathew. The prisoner pleaded not guilty. Mr. Waddy, Q.C., who prosecuted on behalf of the Treasury, said that the jury would probably come to the conclusion from the evidence that a deliberate, long-conceived, and carefully studied plan to do his wife to death had been executed by Hudson, and that be killed his wife with the deliberate intention of marrying another woman and getting money by a° doing. Evi- dence was then given as to the ending of the bedies, of the prisoner's movements before and after the crimeland of the discovery after the arrest amongst Hudson's be- longings of weapons corresponding with those used to commit the crime. Three, lunacy doctors were called to prove that Hudson was sane. For the defence it was suggested that the prisoner was suffering from the earlier stages of epilepsy. Mr. Justice Mathew in sum- ming up said there was nothing to show that the prisoner did not know what be was doing., Hudson was found guilty and sentenced to death. NCR</p>
        <p>THE WESLEYAN CONFERE The Wesleyan Conference meets this year at Plymouth. At the opening session on Tuesday the Rev. Dr. Waller, secretary to the Conference, was elected President, and the Rev. Marshall Hartley was elected secretary in Dr. Waller's place.</p>
        <p>aus BABIES ON FIRE, BABIES BURNING UP, with itching and burning eczemas, and other torturing, dis- figuring skin and scalp diseases. None but mothers realize how these little ones suffer when their tender skins are literally on fire. To know that a warm bath with CS:TIOIRA Soar and a single application of Chymous (ointment), the treat skin cure, will in ttp: majority of cases afford instant relief, permit rest and sleep, and point to a speedy cure, and not to use them without a moment's delay, is to fail in our duty. Think a the years of suffering entailed by such neglect, years not only of physical but of mental suffering by reason of (.118figurenent, added to bodily torture. Cures made in Infancy ind childhood are speedy, permanent, 1 and economical.</p>
