      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>MARK OF THE BLACK CRCEOR</head>
        <p>MARK OF THE BLACK CRCEOR</p>
        <p>EY SYLVANUS COBB, J.</p>
        <p>--- This is a story of love and war, and is full of adventures in the Highlands of Scotland, on the sea, and in the jungles of India. It is intensely interesting from the first to the last.</p>
        <p>CHAPTEI V. GLENALPINE.</p>
        <p>Tony Piper, though warmly attached to the interests of the elder of the two step-sons of Birch mear, yielded his service to the younger son most gracefully and cheerfully. Ile was a talkative fellow, and stood little upon ceremony ; bu. he knew his place, and knew how to keep it. "Did your honour see that daft looking loon that's hanging around here since yesterday ?, " he asked, after they had started on their way. " Whom do you mean ?" returned Arthur, per- fectly willing m have his mind withdrawn from the subject that harrowed kiln. " Why, the man that just left* you as we catne up, to be sure." Are you serious, Tony ?" "What do you mean, your honour! Arthur looked into the valet's face, and made sure that he was speaking with honest purpose. "I mean, Tony, that I have seen no one in the glade save yourself and your master." " Didn't ;on see the tall, ghostly-looking man, with the grizzled face and the harlequin dress ? " "I saw nothing of the kind." "I saw him, sir, corning away from the glade; and I supposed, of course, he had been speaking with you." Arthur had commenced a further remark when his attendant gave a sudden caution of silence. " ! There he is ! " Tony was so burdened with luggage that he could not point with his finger, but the inclination of his head gave suffilient token of the direction, and upon looking that way Arthur saw a tall, gaunt form, clad in a rough hunting-suit of buckskin, just passing from sight beyond a clump of maples. Ho had time to see t/fat the stranger had long grizzled hair of a sandy hue, which hung in tangled masses over his shoulders, and that his beard, of the same colour—once ybllow, but now thickly sprinkled with silver—was worn full, and flowed down upon the breast. The man's profile was for an instant exposed. The brow, though partially covered by the fiat-topped Scotch cap or bonnet, was seen to be full and ample, while the lower features were strong and prominent. Thus much Arthur saw, and then the figure was lost in the thicket. "That is the man, sir," said Tony. "Who is he? What is he ? " demanded Arthur, with a show of more than ordinary interest. "That is what I want to know, your honour. I saw him first hanging around the park yesterday, when the party was in the garden. Then I saw him again this morning, coming out from the summer-house just after my master had gone by. I saw him next as we came into the glade to meet you.,, "What does Harold think of him ?" "Harold has not seen him." "Didn't you say anything to him about the man ?" "No, sir. He seemed to have enough of his own affairs to think of, so I didn't trouble him."</p>
        <p>Arthur had not.the least idea who the stranger could be ; and though his appearance had deeply interested him, he did not deem it worth while to prolong conversation upon the subject. The distance from the manor to the great high- way of the York and Manchester mail-coaches was not far from tw9 miles. Our friends struck the road close by it respectable inn, where the luggage was deposited, and where Arthur dispensed with furthur service from the valet.</p>
        <p>" Does your honour go towards Lancashire or to the south ?" Arthur thought it most likely he should take the first-mentioned course; and having given Tony a half-crown he dismissed him. _</p>
        <p>But our hero had no idea of waiting for the mail- coach. He meant to take a private conveyance towards Skipton; avoiding the mail-route alto- gether. In short, he had it in his mind to make the best of his way to the Lancashire coast. ,Ile had gone into the stable-yard for the purpose of conferring with the hostler, when he felt a hand upon his shoulder, and upon turning he beheld the stranger whom he had seen in the wood—him of the unkempt hair and the flowing, grizzled beard. Arthur's first impression was that he had a maniac to deal with, and he stepped back and looked him over. _ _</p>
        <p>The man was slightly above the ordinary height, his frame strong and well proportioned, but so thin of flesh as to give him rather a cadaverous and ill- omened appearance. Ile wore leggings of thick leather, and his buck-skin skirt, or frock, secured at the waist by a belt, was worn and soiled, and the binding frayed in many places. The expres- sion of his strongly marked, angular features was stern, but not unkind. The sharp grey eyes were deeply sunkcn beneath the shaggy brows, and looked like ewo bright burning lights glooming from their cavernous depths. "My friend," said the stranger, with extreme deference, "you will excuse me for interrupting you; but I wish to ask of you a question." "You can ask it, sir." .</p>
        <p>"I saw you, not long since, in company with a gentleman whom you called Harold. He was a dark-faced, rotund man, approaching the middle- age, and I fancied that he had an evil eye. You were with ucli a man ?"</p>
        <p>"I think I recognise the individual to whom you allude," replied Arthur, wondering much what could be at the bottom of the question. " Who is he? What is he? Pardon me, sir ; but I would like to know." The man was eager even to tremulousness, and his hands were clutched tightly. "lie is the Honourable Harold Berwick." "A son of Sir John ?" _ _</p>
        <p>Ordinarily Arthur would have replied to such a question that Harold was only a step-son, but as such an answer in the present instance might call for explanations which he did not care to make, he simply said. Yes."</p>
        <p>_ A shadow passed over the stranger's face, ilia hands sank by his side, and a murmur of disappoint. ment fell upon his lips. "Harold Berwick ! A son of Lord Birchmear I He is not what I thought. But you know him well? You cannot be mistaken ? " "I have known him for years." "Then he is not the man. I beg your pardon, sir. I have been mistaken. Semething in that man's eye—something in his whole face—reminds me strongly of a picLure long since fixed in my</p>
        <p>mind. Arthur was strongly tempted to tell the strange man the name which Harold had borne in other years, but on reflection he concluded that he would not do so. There was something in the look and tone of the way worn stranger that made him shudder—not with fear for self, but with fear for the person thus eagerly sought after. Presently the landlord came out and the stranger turned away, once more begging the youth's pardon and thanking him for his kindness. "Do you know that man ?".Arthur asked of the host. "I know only what I have heard within the last four-and-twenty hours, sir," the inn-keeper replied, when he had watched the receding form until it had disappeared beyond the arch of the gateway- " I don't exactly like him hanging around here, Mr. Arthur, and yet he's been as quiet and as peaceable as a lamb. You know Sam Follet works the Otley coach!" Arthur said he had seen him. "Old Sum," pursued the landlord, mysteriously. "says he's known this man for years, and when Sam says he knows you may set it down that he DOES know. According to his account this strange. looking and stranger acting individual is a gentle- man and an Irish boronet, and his name is Sir Philip GI enal pine. " " GLENALPINE " repeated Arthur, with a start of interest and surprise. "Sir Philip Glenalpine I He was apprehended for murder while I was at Oxford and was tried at York." "Not exactly tried for murder, your honour. His friends made application to the Lord Chan- cellor for a commission of lunacy, which was granted. Sir Philip was decided to be of unsound mind and was placed under restraint. At the en.‘ of a year the doctors pronounced him recovered. and he went abroad. There's something strange *bout him, Mr. Arthur; something very strange. He's get a heavy load on his mind, and I don't think he's exactly sound up here for all what the doctors say." The inn-keeper tapped himself upon the fore- head as he voile, and as his listener did not reply lie proceeded: Last eight he came in quite late, and after 'he had crif. ease of brandy and water, he went emilettileales Es the owner of tht Wirlour and</p>
        <p>took a leather case out of his breast-pocket, not much larger than a card-case, and out of this he took a small book covered with red vellum. I happened to be coming in from the kitchen while he had it open and i saw on one side of the leaves what looked like a name written, and on another was a black cross—a cross as large as the page would admit and as heavy and as black as it could be made. He heard my step and shut his beck, and as he did so I was sure that I saw another of those horrid black crosses farther over. Of course I couldn't help wondering what he was porin3, over that pge for when there wasn't a bit of reading on it, only the single name I told you of." It was certainly very strange, and Arthur felt that he would like to understand it; but he could -gain no further information from his host. It was settled, however, that the wayfarer was Sir Philip Glenalpine ; that he had been for years a wanderer up and down the earth, without home, and so far without friends; that he made no new ones, and kept almost entirely aloof from his old ones. What was the purpose of his life no one could tell, but that he had a purpose—a deep and fixed purpose— was evident. Arthur wondered now more than ever what Glenalpine had found familiar in the face of Harold, and he thought he had done wisely in not reveal- ing the fact Cal his step-brother had once borne another name. Upon what slender threads hang the destinies of human life! What tiny motes change the colour of its lights, and what seemingly insignificant trifles give new course to the whole tide of its events! Had Arthur spoken the simple sentence in Glen- alpine's hearing, which had once been almost upon his lips, how different might have been his whole future! In the meantime there was deep commotion at the manor-house. Bashaw and Lundie had disap- peared with Lionel Talford, and it was whispered that there had been a quarrel and a duel. The whisper reached the ears of Sir John, and he sent for Harold. "Indeed, my lord," said the step-son, when he had been asked to explain the matter, "I wish some other lips could frame the speech for this unhappy story. I wish I could forget it." "Out with it !" cried the baron, impatiently. "You need not fear to tell me all that has hap. pened. Has there been a quarrel?" "Yes, my lord." "And a duel?" "Yes." "And Arthur is at the bottom of it !” "Yes—or I should say—Arthur's unfortunate weakness was at the bottom of it." "He was drunk, eh ?" "I am sorry to say yes." "And now, tell me how it was. Mind you, I want the whole story. lam prepared for it." Thus bidden, Harold went on to give an account of the scene at the gaming table, making it appear that the entertainment had been gotten' up at the instance of the guests, and that only a very quiet and innocent round of amusement had been antici- pated. When he came to speak of the quarrel he appeared to endeavour to gloss over Arthur's share in it, but he did it in a manner so malevolently adroit that the reflection was far worse than the actual fact had been. He gave Sir John to under- stand that Talford had been brutally attacked and knocked down, besides having been otherwise aspersed and abused. And then he told of the duel, representing that Arthur had gone forth in drunken bravado ; had met his ant,,z,onist in deadly animosity; and had shot him before the fire could be exchanged. -</p>
        <p>"Enough! Enough !" groaned the old noble. "Tell me no more of that disgraceful scene." For a little time Sir John sat with his head bowed upon his hands. " Ah, ' he murmured, speaking with himself, but loud enough for Harold to hear, "the boy will never know, how I have loved him. He will never know what a heart he has trampled upon and broken." A brief pause, and then he started to his feet. He had overcome the momentary emotion of tenderness, and his worn features were fixed and stern.</p>
        <p>"Where is he now ?" "He has left the manor, my lord, and taken his portmanteau and travelling cloak with him." "He has gone away ?" "I think he has." "He had no money for a journey." "He had a goodly sum, my lord." " Ah ! he won it at the gaming-table?' "No. He lost there."</p>
        <p>"How? And more money still?" "Yes. Tony, who accompanied him to the inn beyond the eastern preserve, saw him open his pocket-book, and discovered at least a thousand pounds in it." "No more! no more! Leave me now, Harold." "Would you have him followed, my lord ?" "No. Let him go." Sir John, when he had been left alone, went to his private cabinet, and applied a key to the lock of the drawer where he kept his money, but he found the bolt already withdrawn. The drawer had been unlocked by some one else. He opened it, and a fierce tremor shook his frame when he found that full two thousand pounds had been abstracted.</p>
        <p>"0, my God I" he groaned, tottering back, "and has it came to this ! Dissipation—debauchery— beastliness—rioting—brawling—murder I -- and now comes robbery I—robbery of his old father Verily the cup is full! Farewell, poor boy! The result must be upon thine own head. Had it been the other—had it been the son of Theobald Travers —who had come to this, the mystery would not have been so perplexing, and such utter astound- ment would not have oppressed me. But that the son of Tom Rainsford—of that brave and manly soldier—the son of Isabel—that he should have fallen—it is comprehensible ?"</p>
        <p>The old man sank down into a chair, and bowed his head upon his hands, and so he remained for a long time. When he finally came forth from his chamber his step was firm and the expression of his face was stern and unflinching. But it had required vast effort on his part to assume this car- riage. The days passed, and faithful servants were upon the track of the fugitive. At length word was brought to Birchmear that Arthur had left England. Also bank-notes had been gathered up which he had paid out on the road, and Sir John, when he saw them knew them to have been taken from his cabinet.</p>
        <p>"It is well he is gone," the old man said. "I wish to see him no more. Ile hath made his own pillow, and he must lie upon it." And yet the Lord of Birehmear was not happy. Something that he had loved was lost to him, and the great promises of life seemed to have failed him. Once her ladyship surprised him in the act of looking at a miniature set in a tiny frame of gold and encased in a morocco book. Ile shut the case Trickly when he heard her step, and hid it away In his bosom. She dared not give token that she had observed the movement. In many things she hesitated not to bear down upon her husband with a strong hand, but she ventured not to probe him to the heart. She had discovered that his heart was different from her own, and if she would retain control over him in any degree she must appear to respect his little weaknesses of affectionate remem- brance. _ _</p>
        <p>"Harold," she said to her son, "we must find some means of lifting Sir John out from this slough of despond into which he has fallen. He has de- clared his intention of making you his heir, but he has not yet done it; and if he be not aroused he may die without doing it at all. To-day, as I entered his chamber, I found him, as I have found him before when he knew it not, with an open miniature in his hand, upon which he was gazing with tearful earnestness. He hid it away when he knew that I was near."</p>
        <p>"Did you see it ? " "I have seen it often enough, Harold. It was the picture of Isabel Itainsford. We must drag that memory out from his heart if we can. We must find something to occupy his mind." The something to occupy Lord Birchmeaes mind, however, came without the aid of her ladyship or her son. The secret committee of the board of directors of the ,East Indian Company had fixed upon Baron Birchmear as the man to execute an important commission to the Indian Government. The business was of the utmost importance, requiring, also, extreme circumspection in its management, it being nothing less than a rupture between th‘i governor-general and the governors of Madras and Bombay, which threatened the moat serious consequences. Sir John was not only inti. mately acquainted with all the parties concerned, but he was also thoroughly versed in the Blake of the company's polity. Would he go? His answer was prompt and decisive, He would go. And Lady Birchmear and Harold obtained per. mission to go with him. They did not think ssala x4r trust him so fe.r away alone. ( To be continued I</p>
      </div>
