        <p>DI a I and varied are the questions which have kill(' a part in the present election, but it is cer- faejl.torprising to find the Indian Mutiny a potent Itee—r In the fight. It is so, nevertheless, and Mr. I 412,Ra Whiteley has to thank the great rising in ,Z,rldalt illoriti y. Empire for at least one unit in his 'itl Speaking at a meeting in Cheshire on be ;11!.(143' night, the Mayor of Stockport stated that Vote lmself heard a man say, " Oh, I cannot give my to Zr Joseph Leigh, because he voted for the 4 'llan Mutiny!' Probably Mr. Whiteley will feel Oth loterly pride in the intelligent electorate the confounds the Indian import duties with Rrstit revolt.</p>
        <p>%ea • heti t interest is being excited throughout Cum. -Of tballci and Westmoreland by the approaching visit thele German Emperor to Lowther Castle, Penrith, 41—'4 ef the Earl of Lonsdale. It appears at the Cliff-, Emperor will travel from Cowes to either 011 tzn and Lowther or Penrith station, arriving etexid'Morning of Sunday, August 11th, and will s the opening day of the grouse season on the b t;re, probably on Shap Fell. Another day will a: 14,evoted to a tour through the most charming toe,"on of the Lake country. Driving from Lowther jh 4 eeley Bridge the Emperor will go up Ullswater &amp;it et,eamer. From Patterdate, Lord Lonsdale will "ther '0 'lie Majesty over Kirkstone Pass to Ambleside, Ti, e another steamer will be waiting. „,!Lat vessel will go the le.ngth of Windermere, and rthablY hack again. Arrangements will then be Qtqade for driving from the Windermere district ,y urasniere, Rylial, Dunmail Raise and Thirlmere 11:lEeswick, and probably thence back to Penrith. e time ago something was said about his helest Y being taken to Wiiitehaven, but that was w,., rore the election, and "we never mention it ok:,</p>
        <p>It i 8 Stated that Madame Bernhardt is writing rot wemMrs. Two private secretaries have been e 'do itg for some time in arranging the mass of clime "Ibm Ms, which the great tragedienne intends to basis of the forthcoming volume. An ifor Tb`can firm of publishers off-red her 500,000d01s !Ithiou""nei. coPyright,and subBequently increased this 'lO bee-0." "00.000dols. But Madame is ambitious :14%N Ize her own publisher,and has declined every 'ths,LBlll to deprive her of this pleasure. It is q stated trat 48 consented to the memoirs appearing r,4neo„n— a,tl American and a Parisian journal simul- :ll4 ,on tlie understanding thit a French and ''atorv"R;lBll version appear in both papers. The i Lai tn":: 11,er life ought to create a sensation, and t loave oned that no caprice will cause her to memoirs unfinished.</p>
