        <p>The new members of the House, without excep- tion, have still to win political distinction. Some of them are absolutely unknown outside their own local circles, and one or two who are better known tan hardly be described as eminent politicians. The new member for North Kensington, Mr. Sharpe, was a distinguished member of the Ceylon Civil Service. Mr Marks, who has defeated Mr. Benn, is the proprietor of the " Financial News," and is well known in the financial world. Mr. Gedge, who has been elected for Walsall, sat in the Parlia- ment of 1886, and was distinguished for his narrow views and fervent Churchmanship. Mr. Cruddas, who along with Mr. Hammond now represents Newcastle-on-Tyne, is a member of the great Arm- strong firm, and no doubt owes his election in part to his connection with Elsvvick. The member for Bow and Bromley,Mr. Lionel riollan(I, is one of the better sort of Conservatives. He has taken great interest in social questions in the East- end of London, and this partly explains his success at the polls. Major Dolbiac, the victor in North Cambervvell, served with distinction in the Egyp- tian war, and still carries in his body a bullet which he received in one of the battles on the Nile. He is a son-in-law of Colonel Seeley, lately one of the members for Nottingham. Mr. Faithfull Begg,who has won one of the seats at Glasgow, is a son of the late Dr. Begg,one of the leaders of the Free Courch. He is a well-known stockbroker, and it is reported that during the active speculation in the South African market he has made a considerable fortune.</p>
        <p>Now that Mr. Walter Maclaren has fallen, the ladies who agitate for the enfranchisement of their sex will require a new parliamentary champion to espouse their cause. The circular which they have just issued gives a list of 316 candidates who have pledged themselves to woman's suffrage, and in so hopeful a list they will have no difficulty in dis- covering a leader. It is remarkable that, of the 316 advocates of the principle, about one-half are Un- ionists and one-half Liberals. Women, therefore will have a large party of supporters in the House if the members who are pledged have the courage in the division lobby of their opinions on the plat- form. Lord Salisbury probably would not be unwill- ing in due time to take up the movement, as the enfranchisement of qualified women would mean a large increase of Conservative voters. The advanced few would be on the side of progress, but the more timid mass would unquestionably be on the side of cautious Conservative reform. If the statements in the circular are to be accepted. the granting ef a vote to qualified women would mean the additien of upwards of three-quarters of a million voters to the electorate.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>LONDON LADY'S LETTER.</head>
        <p>A LONDON LADY'S LETTER.</p>
        <p>THE following letter bears in its simple, honest, and intelligent wording, so absolute.a stamp of truth that we would not add one syllable to it :—Gentlemen,—l am writing a few lines in praise of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People. It is six weeks since I ceased tak- ing them, so I think I have waited long enough to prove whether they hate done good or not. I have had six boxes altogether (they are 23. 93. per box), but I can- not thank Gad enough that I was persua lied to try the Pills, and for the benefit I have derived from them. I am not the same creature. For five years I have been suffering from anEemia. I had medical attendance. It did no good whatever. Since than I have tried differ- ent tonics, and spent a small fortune on them, but they have done little good. It was about three months ago I bought the Pills, and I ant much better in every way. In fact, truthfully speaking I have never enjoyed such good health all my life. Shortness of breath , palpitation of the heart, very bad headaehes, through bloodlessness—l feel nothing of now ; I took one pill after breakfast; two after dinner ; and one after tea. I do so hope that if anybody should re ad these lines, who is having bad health, to do what they can te buy six boxes for 133. 9d.,, and they will not, I am sure, regret it. I recommend them to everyone who complains of illness, as I have such faith in them through experience. I feel I have not praised the Pills enough, or I should say, that I cannot praise them enough. Yours sincerely. (Miss) E. TAYLOR. 2, Park-cot tages, Hendon-lane, Flicchley, London, N., Tune 27 , 1895.</p>
        <p>This lady has been personally interviewed by an im- portant London newspaper, and her statement, with portrait, will be reproduced in an early issue. Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pals People are the best possible tonic. They cure influenza, rheumatism, sci- atica, neuralgia, paralysis, locomotor ataxy, nervous headache, scrofula, chronic erysipelas, and sallow com- plexion. They area specific for the female sex, and in men cure all cases from worry, overwork or excesses. Genuine only in pink wrapped boxes, bearing in red letters, the full name, Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People ; sold by chemists,also by Dr. Williams' Medicine Company, 46, Holborne-viaduct , London.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this Board was held at Dutton Work- house, on Tuesday afternoon, under the presidency of Mr. J. Knowles. In addition to the membe's of the Runcorn Rural Council there were present the Rev. Dr. Gastaldi, and Mr. A. R. Norman, Mr. J. Ashton (clerk), Mr. G. Ashton (deputy clerk) and Mr. Cunnah (work- house master).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>VAGRANCY.</head>
        <p>VAGRANCY.</p>
        <p>The Clerk to the Newhaven Guardians submitted to the Board four resolutions which had been adopted by the Unions of Kent, Surrey and Sussex. at a conference held at Lewes on the 18th ult., and which they recom- mended the Guardians to adopt. The resolutions were in favour of uniform treatment of casual paupers by adhering strictly to the present law and Local Govern- ment Board orders, and that the Local Government Board should be called upon to take steps to enforce the Act of 1882 to secure that end. Further that the Guar- dians urge upon the magistrates and police authorities the desirability of strictly enforcing the Vagrancy Acts, and that the Local Government Board be asked to pro- mote such legislation as would provide for the children of tramps convicted of leading a vagrant's life, to be detained in reformatories or schools at the cost</p>
        <p>of the State.—Mr. Williams thought that th. part applying to children would have the sympathy of all the members. As regarded the adoption en bloc of the Act of 1880, he was not sufficiently well acquainted with it to recommend it to be adopted. He thought the three counties mentioned were very differently situated with regard to vagrants, than the two counties of Lan- cashire and Cheshire. He thought it would be the wish of the Board to pass a resolution in support of those submitted to the Council.—Mr. Mclntyre said they had not sufficient accommodation tor vagrants to carry out the Act of 1892 in its entirety. The question might , arise whether they should not have to make provision for the increase at the same ratio as it had increased. The propagation of vagrancy went on while vagrants were allowed to take their children with them.—ln answer to a question, the Workhouse Master said they were in the same position as most unions. If they adopted the Act of 1892, they would want extra accommodation, as the Act stipulated that the vagrants should be kept for 48 hours.—Mr. Stokes spoke of the importance of the last resolution referring to children of vagrants.—Mr. Mclntyre said from a statement of the late secre- tary of the Local Government Board he under- stood that where the Act had been put in force in its entirety, it had reduced the number of vagrants.—The Workhouse Master said the contrary was the case with regard to the Chester Union. They adopted the Act, but the number of vagrant: increased and they had to discontinue it.—lt was ultimately decided to support the resolutions which had been passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>MAINTENANCE OF PA- UPEZ LUNATICS..</head>
        <p>THE MAINTENANCE OF PA- UPEZ LUNATICS..</p>
        <p>A communication was read from the County Asylum, Chester, stating that at a meeting of the committee of visitors the rate of maintenance of pauper lunatics in the asylum, for the quarter ending 30th September, was fixed at 433. Sd. per head per week.</p>
        <p>FINANCE. Mr. Heywood, in submitting the report of the Finance Committee, said they had not sufficient money in the bank to meet the whole of the County Council call. But the Finance Committeeproposed to pay a certain por- tion of it. With regard to the out-relief he was pleased to Fay that it continued to be small. The amount for Runcorn was £B5, Frodsham £3O. and Bud worth £l5. The amount in hand was £1,773 34. 101. The call of the County Council was £4,175.—Mr. Woodward seconded the adoption of the Committee's report which was agreed to. This was all the business.</p>
