      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, jULY 27 1895.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, jULY 27 1895.</p>
        <p>AMERICA AS IT IS AND ENGLAND AS</p>
        <p>IT OUGHT TO BE.</p>
        <p>AN INTERESTING LETTER.</p>
        <p>A special Session of the Bonehill Branch Tem. perance Lodge, Latchford, was held in the Temper- ance Hall on Monday evening. Bro. W. Pearson, Chief Templar, pre,sided. Owing to the fact that Bro. J. Ditchfield had stated that he would read a very interesting letter on July 22nd from the Rev. Joseph Ellison, of Carver, Mass. U.S.A., and a late member of the Lodge, there was an unusually large attendance of members. Mr. Ellison is the son of Mr. Luke Ellison, Latchford, and previous to his departure for America he was strongly associated with the temperance lodge in question. In his letter the rev.gentleman gives much information of an interesting character to which the members of the lodge listened with great interest. We quote the following from the letter, follow- ing a sentence in which the writer desires to im- press upon their minds the fact " that you are Citizens of no mean country " The importance of citizenship is great. It is one of the great bless- ings of this age. Men, not kings or princes, are the rulers of this day, and in their hands are the com- ing nations to be fashioned for honesty, purity, prosperity, and ultimately for God. An educated citizenship, having its life expression and thought expression in the ballot, is full of glorious possi- bilities for all coming civilisations. An ignorant citizenship, with minds intoxicated by social im- purity, by hereditary carnality, or by .drink, having its expression through a free ballot, is a grievous, a very grievous curse. It is a millstone on the wheels of progress and must be cut off by an un- sparing hand. I speak as a brother when I urge you never to allow anything or anyone to keep you down, to 'keep you in ignorance, to hold back from you that liberty of thought, the exercise of power, that independence of life which are your own. Value these more than gold or rubies. They are the principles which make noblemen, and none are noblemen without them. When I write about an educated citizenship I mean simply this The citizen must make tiimself acquainted with life around him—with life as it is lived, and life as it is governed; and how it ought to be governed. He must study what is generally termed "socio- logy" and politics; and not only study them, but also form decided opinions upon them ior himself. When these opinions are formed, express them in the ballot box and elsewhere. Pray never go to the poll without opinions. The ballot box is for opinions not for papers. Let those ideas be your own well- thougnt-out principles; for in this way alone can we hope to make the world better. • Let me further urge upon you that the ballot box has been chosen by the eminent thinkers of this civilization and age as the only way in which we can give public expression as to the way we want the world ruled. To neglect it, therefore,is a sin against the age and the generations yet unborn. When a citizen is in the ballot room he is face to face with the drink curse, with the opium traffic, with the gambling den, with the causes of social wars between capital and labour. Face to face, too, with all that shall I make this world worse or better than it is. The educated citizen can, at the ballot, say whether he ' will ever have the earth cursed any more with national wars, with broken-hearted widows and mothers, whose dear ones' blood has helped to swell the tide of foreign rivers. The educated citizen at the ballot can put down all oppression and every oppressor, and can build up a large, free, and prosperous life. How many young men in the dear home village lodge will give me their hand, while we pledge together that throughout life we determine upon reform,upon progress, upon liberty, upon education,a free bible, a free conscience,a free worship of God? Determine, too, upon the total annihilation of all social evils as they come to us through the drink traffic. Let us say," We self- ruled people are men—and we insist upon all pure principles, and make no compromise with wrong." Now let me say something about temperance. America is a long way ahead of England in temper- ance legislation. Compared with the United States.or at least portions of the States, Great Britain is 25 years behind. For years " Local Option" and " Sunday Closing " have been as common as stones in Massachusetts. Once a year, every citizen, of every town, is called upon to decide by ballot whether he wants any liquor sold in his town. I am enclosing with this letter the" Yes" and "No" ballot used in Carver at the Spring Town meeting this year. Let the members of the lodge see them. We had a majority of " No" votes, and the town appropriated a sum of money, to be used for the suppression of illegal liquor selling. If a man is charged with drunkenness, he is often let off with a caution the first time, if he has a good reputation. But let him repeat it, and become a " common drunkard," and he is summarily imprisoned for six months or a year. I tell you all sincerely, if a man gets drunk here, one of the best things he can do is to conceal himself, or hire a cab and drive home. At the same time there is an awful amount of drinking. And oh! what foul, wicked stuff these chemical beers and chemical spirits in America are 1 They make their victims writhe in sin and filth and slime.</p>
        <p>Taking the country as a whole, I think the drink question is a perplexing one to the majority. Moral 'suasion "is practically abandoned as a cure. Everybody now looks for legislation upon the matter. We have a growing political party, called the Prohibition Party, whose chief plank is to pro- hibit not only the sale but the manufacture of the accursed thing upon the free soil of this republic. Even the churches, believing as they do in God,and in the power of the Christ over human souls, look to the legislature as the divinely appointed medium for the overthrow of this blood-poisoning octopus. But what kind of legislation is it to be? We have, as I wrote above, "local option " and "Sunday closing," but all feel that these are failures, and something more radical is required. Three months I ago a bill was introduced in the Massachusetts Legislature asking for the " Norwegian or Gothen- burg System "of licenses. But the temperance men and saloon-keepers joined hands and overthrew it. The temperance party were afraid it would give the drink question a legal position in the State it has never yet enjoyed; and the saloon-party feared the financial loss arising from the traffic being taken from private hands, and sold by State officials. Had the Gothenburg bill passed, the State would have established depots for the sale of lipuor in diff- erent quarters, by independent officers, who would receive no benefit whatever from either large or small sales. The argument runs thus :—These saloon men are in this business to make money. They run the rum-shop because there is more profit in rum than in tea or sugar; and so if you take private profit out of the liquor, nobody will sell it, and the demand will go down, and the temptations will decrease, etc. Well, the State is not prepared to go that far yet, but I must confess the idea is taking strong hold upon many minds in America. One thing must be sure to all of us—we are not going to kill this evil all at once. To do so would be a species of social intolerance over the demands of a large number of free people who still demand the drink. Whether, then, is it better to " control "it little by little, by wise and timely legislation, or wait and pine, or pine and wait for that happy golden age when a sweeping law shall purge the earth of her sins? Yours in faith, hope and love, JOSEPH ELLISON." Alter the reader had resumed his seat, Bro. H. Blease rose to move that a hearty vote of thanks be forwarded to their respected Bro., J. Ellison, also conveying to him the kind regards and wishes of the Lodge. Bro. J. Corbett seconded the proposi- tion in a very able speech, supported by Bra. H. Richardson, J. Boscow, A. Ford, J. Ditchfield, and the Chairman. The proceedings aterminated by singing and prayer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>CURIOUS COUNTING INCIDENT.</head>
        <p>CURIOUS COUNTING INCIDENT.</p>
        <p>An extremely curious incident is stated to have taken place in connection with the counting of the votes in the Romford election. By some means some voting papers mysteriously disappeared during j the process of count- ing. This discovery was made just before the returning officer was about to declare the result, and a search failed to discover all the missing papers. There were in the end forty-six unaccount( d for, and it was decided to add twenty-three to the bona fide total of each candi- date, both candidates approving of that arrangement. •</p>
        <p>• Fenton RowbOttom, a moulder, at Lockwood, Flud- dersfield, attempted to murder his wife on Tuesday by shootmg her with a revolver, and afterwards committed suicide. The wife received only a flesh wound.</p>
