      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>CORPORATION FINANCE.</head>
        <p>CORPORATION FINANCE.</p>
        <p>THE ANNUAL ACCOUNTS.</p>
        <p>The annual statement of accounts of the War- rington Corporation, for the year ending March 131st, 1895, has just been issued, by Mr. John Fair- hurst, borough treasurer. They contain a large amount of valuable information extending to up- wards of 175 pages. We proceed to give some of the more interesting portions of it. CHARGED ON THE BOROUGH RATE.-BALANCE IN HAND. The balance in hand in the Manchester and Liverpool District Bank on March 26, 1894, was £766 15s. 7d., and the borough rate ordered on April 1894 for Warrington and Latchford has realised £10,347 4s. 3d.</p>
        <p>WATCH DEPARTMENT. The receipts on this department for the year have been £2,541 19s. 4d., the highest item of which is £2,361 15s. 11d. paid by the Lancashire County Council as one half the Charge for the pay and clothing of police. The rest is from fines. licenses, Ste. The expenditure has been £5,250 US. 4d., composed chiefly of £3OO for the Chief Constables' salary, £4,217 es. for constables' waves, £305 17. 4d. for clothing, itc., of the police, and £5OO, the salary of the clerk to the magistrates.</p>
        <p>RENTS, TAXES, RIO. DEPARTMENT. This department has realised £132 15s. sd. from rents on putellic buildings and the expenses in repairs, etc., have been £76 2s. id.</p>
        <p>CEMETERY DEPARTMENT. This department has brought in £1,139 ss. 6d., to- wards which the purchase of graves, headstones, etc., have realised £976 23., and the church burial fees £163 34. 6d. The expenses were £1,519 153. 101 of which £733 103. 9,1. was required for interest and principal upon the debt; salaries of the late and present registers, £ll7 43., gardeners' andlabourers' wages; £265 153.3 d.; paid to the various clergymen of the town, £156 133.4 d., and also various minor items.</p>
        <p>SALARIES DEPARTMENT. Towards this department £1,040 has been transferred from the improvement rate, and £320 from the water- works revenue account, making a total of £1,360. The Town Clerk's department requires the expenditure of £668 93. 11d. ; the borough surveyor's department, £702 ; and the horoueh treasurer's and rate collecting department, £l,OlO 103., making a total of £2,400 19s. 11d. BOROUGH ANALYST AND CONTAGIOUS DISEASES DEPARTMENTS. These two departments have received from the County Council a total of £4B 133. 5d., and the salaries, chemi-</p>
        <p>cals, etc., were £6l 33. 4d. MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS. The municipal elections in November, and the bv- election in August, 1894,have cost the town £156 83.7 d., and towards this, the paltry sum of 2s. has been realised by is. each from Alderman Platt, and Councillor Flood, as fines for resigning their seats on the council. TOWN HALL AND BANE PARE DEPARTMENTS. The Town Hall has cost £629 81r,2d„ for Maintenance during the year, it being chieiiy eionvosed of plumbing, and other repairs, and £124 lie. for 6130,12allkeeper's and cleaner's wages. Nothing has been received to counter- balance this. The Park has cost £1348145. 3d., of which £549 163. 7d. has been for gardeners, labourers, and park keeeper's wages, and £6OB 18s. 9, for the pulling down of the old wall and ,erection of the new gates in , Sankey-at.</p>
        <p>BATHS DEPARTMENT. The total receipts of the baths for last year were £486 13s. and the expenses £920 Is., consisting of £334 173:7d. far interest and repayment of principal of the department, £176 7s. 6. for bath keeper's and assistant's wages, and various other items. SCHOOL ATTENDANCE DEPARTMENT. This department has coet the tawn £6Ol 15s. 9d., con- sisting of 54 weeks' salary for Mr. G. Riley, 11.41 Iss. ; ditto for Mr. William Jones, £94 12s. 61. ; ditto for Mr. William 'Umber, £B2 10s. The maintenance of children at Industrial Schools has cost £228 us. 7d., and there is a farther donation of £3O to the training ship " Clio."</p>
        <p>- _ VARIOUS DEPARTMENTS. _ Towards the sinking fund for the debt upon the Town Hall, Baths,&amp;c., the sum of £1,015 123.3 d. has been paid for interest, and repayment of the principal. £225 17s. 81. has been required for Parliamentary expenses, and £572 193. 7d. for rent, &amp;c., on open spaces.</p>
        <p>MISCELIANEOUS DEPARTMENT. This department has realised £159 9. 6cl. for transfers ot annuities, &amp;c. In the expenditure we notice £52 103. for silver caskets containing the scroll which entitles Alderman Holmes and Major Pickmere to be freemen of Warrington ; expenses at the opening of the Ship Canal, £l6 183. 2d. ; and opening of Walton Lock, £2l 103.81.; priating, stationery, &amp;0., £450 us. 7d. ; professional services, re Snip Canal,£B4 ; re alteration of union boun- daries, £l9 23. ; deputations, £B4 63. There is also a transfer of £982 175., the proceeds of a 141. rate, to the museum and library fund. After the expenses are paid, there is a balance in hand on the borough rate fund account of £132 163. 7d. CHARGED ON THE IMPROVEMENT RATE POND ACCOUNT. This fund has reatzed during theyear £24,995 193.7 d., and there was a balance owing to rthe bank at the com- mencement of the year of £6,215 17s. 6d. •</p>
        <p>MARKETS AND WEIGHTS AND MEASURES DEPARTMENTS. The market has paid its way during the oast year. The income was £5.471 3s. 5d., including £2,848 54. 11., for fair and market tolls. The expenditure was £3,083 15s. id., composed of £312 7s. for wages, and other items. The weights and measures department has also come out at the right side with income £258 155.91 and expenditure, £157 17s. sd. The income is derived irom stamping and adjusting of weights and measures. The wages of the department have beeo £l4O 2s.</p>
        <p>FIRE ENGINE DEPARTMENT. This department has caused an excenditure of £462 134 51, the wages of which were £94 lls. 11d., and £lOO was also transferred to the fire engine renewal fund. Only El. 3s. has been received to counterbalance this.</p>
        <p>MAINTENANCE OF ISTMLDINGS. Rents etc. of municipal property have realised £1,347 84. CM and the expenses of the property have been £1,705 is. 9d.</p>
        <p>LIGHTING AND STREET IMPOVEMENT. Eighteen months lighting of streets have cost £3185 4d. and with other minor iterni,the lighting depart- ment has required £3200 164. 101, and street improve- ment has also required £Bl 193. 3d. £lO6O was also re- quired for the salaries depArtment. SINKING FUND DEPARTMENT. For payment of interest and principal on the sinking fund, £792 I.ls. 53., has beea paid on the Longford depot, and part cost of an infectious diseases hospital ; £124 4s. 1.1. d., on the completion of an infectious diseases hospital ; £684 3s. 9d., on extensions to Longford depot; £827 6s. 61., on the widening of Bridge-street ; £524 95. for further extensions of Lnnefnrd, £155 103. 7d., for street paving works ; 23108 63. 2d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>SCAVENGING.</head>
        <p>SCAVENGING.</p>
        <p>Scavenging has cost the town £2827 154. 9d. of which the most considerable item has been £1650 12s. 101. for wages; £2Ol has been received from the markets'depart- meat.</p>
        <p>HEALTH AND HOSPITAL DEPARTMENT.</p>
        <p>This department has only received £7. while the ex- penditure has been £892 133. 91, of which £460 4s. 6d. was revireil for Wari and salaries. The hospital has required £2013 13+. 8d ,of which wages and salaries amount to £5BB 153. 6d. The receipts have been £175 103. 63., £175 being received from the Newton Commis- sioners.</p>
        <p>NIGUTSOIL, LONGEORD, ETC. The nightsoil department has realised £420 6s. ld.,the expenditure being £4 709 63. 21., wages being £2,249 4s. 7d., and cartage £BlO 83. 21. The Longford manure works have received £3,095 Is. 7d. for concentrated manure and £l4 163. 11d: for scrap iron, and have expended £2.719 74. 91. of which salaries and wages have been £1,544 193. 61. The wages in the intercepting depots amount to £367 63., and the total cost £792 43.</p>
        <p>HIGHWAYS' DErearunisv. This department has realised £5,207 2s. which has been received chiefly from the Counts Council for the repair- ing of high roads, and from the various Corporation departments. Wages have required the sum of £2,670 18s. 61, and there is quite a host of miscellaneous charges, which bring up the total to £0,260 14s ad. GENERAL SEWERS ACCOtINT. The receipts on this account have been £2,186 12s. 4d., and after paving all expenses, the balance in hand remains at £353 is. 10d. •</p>
        <p>MUSEUM AND LIBRARY. These institutions bring in an income of £1,284 16s. 4d. being proceeds from a 11 i. rate, for subscriptions, fines, sale of catalogues, etc. The balance in hand after pay- ing all expenses is £ll2 93. id., salaries being £474 18s. general expenses £l3B 4s. 41., museum £202 14s. 101. and library £293 ss. id. TECHNICAL INSTRUCTION. Graats, fees, etc.. have realised £1,913 184. 71. to this fund, and the expenses have included £251188, general expenses; £434 3s. 31.. School of Art; £625 194. 5d., School of Science ; and £122 4s. 91.,Continuation School, and there is a balance in the treasurer's hands of £479 123. 61. GAS WORKS. The gas works show a profit on the year of £12,118 11s. Bd., and the income includes £37.760 is. 61. for gas, £847 11. s. 5c1., for meter rents, £2,525 lls. 11d., for tar productions, and these With various other items makes a total of £53.868 O. 'B.ld. Wages and repairs have required £28,710 11s. 11d.-", and there are also other larger items of expenditure. WATER WORKS.</p>
        <p>!SWIMMING GALA AT THE WARRING-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>TON BATHS.</head>
        <p>TON BATHS.</p>
        <p>EXCITING EVENTS.</p>
        <p>The Gala at the Warrington Corporation Baths on Tuesday evening was by far the most successful and at- tractive of the present year. There was a very large at- tendance in the first place, while in the second a pro- gramme consisting of events of more than ordinary in- terest had been prepared. Another matter, which- the committee deserve commending upon, was the prof vision of a canvas screen raised above the pavement o the bath side, and which in a great measure did away with the disagreeable splashing which the holders of front , seats have hitherto been subjected to. The hon. officials I were :—Judges, Messrs. Derbyshire, Grace, Critchley, and Toft ; handicappers, Messrs. Derbyshire, and Grace; starter, Mr. Derbyshire; check starter, Mr. J. Beswick ; referee, Mr. E. A. Birch ; goal scorers, Messrs. Wilson, and Lloyd. Mr. D. Walsh, hon. sec., of the Swimming</p>
        <p>. _ • Club, was also present. The first event was the final of the 50 yards club handi- cap, the heats for which had been swum off some days before. Those pitted against one another for the three 'medals were Graham, Milner, Robertson, and Walker, who eventually came out with Graharn.lst; Robertson 2nd ; and Milner, 3rd.—An event following this was the boys' championship of Warrington, one length race. This competition was exclusively for boys under 11 years of age, and six of these youngsters competed, and the 'winners were A. Baker, Ist : C. Turner, 2nd ; and W. IBroadhurst, 3rd. It was, however, stated that Baker o•was above the stipulated age, therefore the decision was reserved for discussion in committee. An item having attached to it very broad interest was the 50 yards open amateur handicap for three prizes, viz: Handsome marble clock, presented by Mr. Percy Davies ; 2nd prize, gold breast pin • third prize, cruet stand, presented by W. Smith and Co. There was also a fast time prize, value one ginuea. In this handicap there were 12 beats, represented by 54 competitors and some very fine swimming was witnessed. J. H. Tyers and J. H. Derbyshire (Little Rob) were each " scratch " in a heat, but though swimming splendidly they were both unable to come out first. The winners of the respectivel heats were :—First, J. Taylor, N.H.S.C. ; second, J. Molloy, L.G.S.C. ; third, D. Holland, N.H.S.C.; fourth, Fair Turner, W.S.C.; fifth, R. Cheadle, V.S.C. ; sixth, W. Ashton, W.S.C.; seventh, R, Gardener, C.S.C. ; eighth, J. Clark, N L.G.S.C. • ninth, F. Garside, W.S.C.; tenth, W. Wright, W.S.C.; eleventh, W. Bonnet!, V.S.C. ' • twelvth, S. H. Tyldesley, In the second round of two heats between these 12 com- petitors the winners of each six were Ashton Ist, Cheadle and Holland equal, both to swim in final; and in the second lot Tyldesley Ist, Gtrdener 2nd. In the final between these five, Tyldesley was first, Ashton second, and Cheadle and Gardener tying they swam off again but Cheadle was declared the winner owing to Gardener's starting before his time. Tyers' time in the race was 28 1-5 secs., thus coming below the record time by 1-5 sec. Derbyshire did the distance in record time namely 28 2 5 secs. It might be added that Derbyshire last year swam second to Tyers in four national champion- ships; this year he has been second to Tyers in the 100 yards, mile, half-mile, and third in the 220 yards. Tyers' has won every championship for which he has competed since be was 13 years of age. The first ladies' handicip this season was also included in the programme, and comprised two heats of 4 and 5 competitors, respectively. The two winners of heat 1 were Miss M. Dodd Ist and Miss M. Baker (scratch) 2nd; of heat 2, Miss E. Mather Ist and Miss J. Bolton 2nd. In the final for three prizes Miss Bolton came first, Miss E. Mather second, and Miss Baker third. Needless to say this event was productive of much amusement and applause,while the winners were heartily applauded for their successful efforts. " Rob " Derbyshire was the next person to whom the spectators looked to for entertainment,and before he had finished his performance it was unanimously accorded that they had got it. Derbyshire is the holder of the championship of Yorkshire for trick swimming, which he has won for three years in succession, and now pos- sesses the shield. His exhibition at the gala was a good specimen of his skill, the Fouthtul swimmer performing various difficult feats and evolutions in the water,such as seal swimming, crab swimming, ship wreck, torpedo. monkey up a stick, &amp;c., Szo., all of which were interest- ing and some amusing. FIRST ROUND LIVERPOOL AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. WATER POLO MATCH.</p>
        <p>This event was looked forward to with pleasurable feelings as the match between Warrington and the Liver- pool Diamond Tin the first round of the above league promised to be a stiff one. Owing to the authorised re- feree having missed his train connection at Wigan the match was proceeded with,with Mr. Derbyshire (starter) acting in that capacity. The goal scorers were Messrs. B. Lloyd and S. Wilson while the teams were represented as follows : Warrington : Goal, J. F. Dixon; left back, P. Furness; right back, S. Beaman ; half beck, G. A. Sntson ; left forward, W. Ashton ; centre forward, T. Locker (Cap- tain) ; right forward, F. Turner. Diamond T: left hack, R. McLachlan ; goal, W. Quin- lan ; right back, M. Robinson ; half back, M. Jones; left forward, W. Williams; centre forward,W. Knowles; , right forward, J. Moore. I For the first half of the game Warrington defended the deep end of the bath, and Knowles for Liverpool getting the ball passed to Jones who shot a goal in the first minute of play. Locker was next in possession and I passed to Scotson who put in one of his long shots, jest shaving the posts. Warrington were fouled twice after, and from one of these Beaman passed to Locker who drove the ball over the bar. A grand save was immedi- ately afterwards made by Dixon while Turner gave relief, and a series of smart exchanges took place and out of which the Diamond forwards making a sudden rush gave the ball to Moore who scored the second goal of the match. Locker was first to the hall on restarting and immediately passel to Turner who shot strongly but unsuccessfully. The visitors were next on the offensive causing Dixon to fist out smartly, but Scotson passing to Locker that player gave the Liverpool posts a shock. Turner next received and made another futile shot and a stand was made close to the home goal which was again invaded by a neat shot from Williams. The re- commencement of hostilities saw a bout of passing between Furness, Beaman, and Scotson, while a throw- out gave Locker a grand opportunity, but he was fouled right in the visitors' goal-mouth,and a shot from Furness was likewise stopped. Scotson made a pass to Ashton but Locker received and nothing was effected. Beaman and Turner eventually relieved in a dangerous position, the latter again shooting wide. Dixon was again com- pelled to save while Locker and Furness each failed again at grill, so that the first half of a hotly-contested match ended with the visitors leading by 3 goals to nil.</p>
        <p>Warrington with the shallow end of the bath in their favour reversed the order of things in the second half, Locker Immediately securing and transferring to Bea- man, the latter passed to Scotson and another transfer- ence to Locker through Turner enabled the former to put in a shot about which there was some doubt as to where it landed, but the Liverpool umpire allowed a goal. Immediately the game got going again Looker passed back to Scotson and the latter by a long shot scored finely. The same_manceuvre was repeated, but</p>
        <p>the shot went wide and Turner threw up, but the ball was returned to the back and Dixon rushed out and just saved,but before he could return,a Liverpool player was . .</p>
        <p>seen in the act of 'throwing When he was fouled for standing. Turner next by a smart shot scored again while Scotson made another good attempt. A throw-out gave Beaman the ball and he passing to Locker the latter shot over the posts. Timely relief was given by Turner who transferred to Locker without effect though Bea- man saved again at a critical moment. Scotson and Knowles disagreeing in the water were ordered out. Locker and Ashton were next to the fore and the former passing his opponents scored again, which brought Scot- son into play, but owing to the non-appearance of I Knowles, Ashton went out to equaliso matters. Some desultory play ensued and Locker shot twice unsuccess- fully, but Beaman scored immediately after. Sharp play I was next witnessed, in which Scotson received, and ; passing to Beaman, the latter made a good attempt. Dixon saved again splendiily, and some even play fol. lowing,the call of time arrived with Warrington winning</p>
        <p>by 0 goals to 3. The last item on the programme was a novel proceed- I ing, called Angler v. Man-fish, in which Mr. Walton, treasurer of the Warrington Anglers' Association, took the part of the former and Professor Barlow the lat- ter. It was the angler's part to tire the fish in 10 minutes by means of a reel-rod and line attacked to him, but Mr. Barlow's staying powers as a swimmer gave him the victory. Some very amusing remarks were made and the contest served to send the spectators home in a good humour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>PARISH COUNCIL MEETINO.—A meeting of the above Council for Stockton Heath was held on Tuesday even- ing in the schoolroom, to consider the contents of a letter which had been received by the Runcorn District Council from Mr. Hall, of Ivy Bank, Appleton, com- plaining of not receiving a snfficient supply of water at his residence. The letter had been forwarded from the Runcorn District Council to the Clerk of the Appleton Parish Council to see what they could do in the matter. The Clerk was instructed to write to Runcorn to the effect that steps would be taken to prevent the inconvenience. —Mr. Pearson mentionPd that. Mr.. E.c,,erton.Warburton</p>
        <p>The Water Works also show a profit on the year of bad asked a prohibitive price for the land it had been £10,216 3s. Id., the income from the water rate being suggested would be suitable fora recreation ground. He £13,943 Os. 2d., which with other items makes a total should like to draw attention to the fact that Mr. War- of£l4,lss 9s, 11d. Salaries,alnount to £720, and the Laudon or his predecessor had enclosed some common cost of the Winwick pumping station has been £879 land in the northward or old London-road, and the es. Ild, question arose as to whether it was in the .province of Following these statements comes a summary of the the Parish Council to compel him to put the fence back various accounts put in a Concise form, and then a to where it originally stood. He could remember the statement of the bond debt account, and a list of bonds land formir"et part of the road. He suggested that the and annuities. There is also, a statement showing the clerk might be asked to enquire what the Councirs rates of the borough for each year since it received the position in the matter was.—ln reply to Mr. Marsh, Mr. charter of incorporation. From this it appears that the Pearson said the strip of land would be eight yards average rate of the last nine years has been 53. 9d., wide and from 150 to 200 yards long. The highway which is id. less than the average of the first ten years. was narrow there, and is would be a great advantage to At the end of the book then is a general statement of have it made widen—Vie Clerk was instructed to ascer- the affairs of the Warrinattoo Grammar School. - tam la what powers the Council had in the matter.</p>
