      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>HANDSOME BEQUESTS TO THE WARRINGTON CLERGY lorsrrrturoNs. The late Mrs. Fraser, widow of the late respected Bishop of Manchester:has made a bequest of £l,OOO to the Warrington Institution for the relief of widows and orphans of clergy, besides a large num- ber of other legacies to public institutions as well as bequests to relatives and friends have also been made. Mrs. Fraser's generosity to the Warrington Institution ismuch appreciated. The funeral of the deceased lady took place at Ufton Nervet on Satur- day afternoon. SEWING MAcnitins Hand EZ 188. 6d. Treadle £4 4s. with handsome walnut cover.—J, and W. Dvrroic 16 and 18 Sankev-st., Warrington.—Advt. PARISH CHURCH GIRLS' FRIENDLY Booms'. The first annual festival in connection with the Warrington Parish Church Branch of the Girls' Friendly Society was held on Saturday,when about 100 members journeyed to Chester. The weather was very unfavourable at the commencement but cleared up towards four o'clock. A service was held in Chester Cathedraltat which all the members were present, accompanied by the Rev. A. A. Markham. After tea the evening was spent in visiting the various places of interest, boating, &amp;c. CYCLES,-13}Altwain,Sole Agent, Bridge-Bt., Warring- ton.</p>
        <p>CATHOLIC TOTAL ABSTINENCE LEAGUE OF THE CROSS. _ _</p>
        <p>The usual meeting of the above society was held on Sunday in the League Hall Lecture Room, Mr. John Mullins, president, presiding. Mr. William Houghton, of Penketh, an old campaigner in the temperance cause, gave an earnest and stirring ad- dress on the benefits of total abstinence. He is one of the founders of the League in conjunction with a few working men; the inauguration took place some 21 years ago, and the society has been basun- mental in making many homes that were once miserable, bright and happy, A vote of thanks to Mr. Houghton for his address was moved by the Vice-President, and secondei by the President, the vote was carried with enthusiasm. Fur men took the pledge of total abstinence and were duly en- rolled members of the League of the Cross. There were about 100 men present, and the Society is in a flourishing state.</p>
        <p>HOUSEHOLD LINENS. hemmed and marked free of charge.— a. and W. DUTTON, dankey-st., Warrington.—Adrf.</p>
        <p>CHeiRCH The annual trip in connection with the St. Paul's Girls' Friendly Society took place on Saturday, when a visit was paid to Frodsham in 'buses supplied by Messrs. Maginn Bros., of Sankey-st. The party, who reached Frodsham at 3.15, were accompanied by the Vicar (the H. V. Pigot) and Mrs. Pigot, Mrs. Adams, Miss Westwell (mission lady), and Mr. Joseph Lawless (superintendent of the Girl's School). A short ser- vice was held in Overton Church, when an address was given by the Rev. J. J. Langham, vicar of Pad- gate. An adjournment was then made to Mersey View, where tea was provided, and where also the afternoon and evening were spent in an enjoyable manner until 7 30,when a start was made for home, which was safely reached at 9.30. On Monday,the Sunday school teachers went to Southport. accom- panied by the Vicar and Mrs. Pigot, Mr. A. J. Jolley (superintendent of the Boys' School), Mr. Joseph Lawless (superintendent of the Girls' School), Miss Westwell, Mr. and Mrs. Chantry, and Mrs. Smet- hurst. An excellent tea was provided at the Vic- toria Hotel, and thoroughly enjoyed. The day was spent in a very pleasant manner, the weather, as on Saturday, being fine. SEWING aikononts, Hand £2 18s. 6d. Treadle £4 4s. with handsome walnut cover.—l, and W. DUTTON, 16 and 18, Sankey-et., Warrington.—Advt. WORKINGMEN'S GOSPEL TEMPERANCE MISSION.</p>
        <p>A temperance meeting was held on Sunday night in the open space at the end of Bewsey-street, and was conducted by Mr. J. W. Tanner, who said some people stood up for drink, party politics, and the brewers, but he stood up for Jesus and the temper- ance cause. Mr. J. Rigby, the next speaker, stated there was positive proof that the temperance cause was right, and was ne best side to take up. Mr. Charles Firth, of Long Millgate, Manchester, also addressed the meeting.—On Monday night the first meeting of the week's mission was held, conducted by Mr. R. Storey, who said the mission was being held to scatter seeds of kindness for their reaping by and by. He imphr ed his bearers to cease sow- ing the seed of intemperance for their health's sake mind's sake, and soul's sake, and for the sake of all with whom they were connected. The Rev. C. H. Murray, Manchester, general secretary of the Man- chester and Salford District Temperance Union,and Mr. Jatnes Rigby addressed the meeting. WRING-LNG MACKINSS. Gereasrm, Ironmonger Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>LATCHFORD WITHOUT PARISH COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>LATCHFORD WITHOUT PARISH COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Latchford Without Parish Council was held on Tuesday evening, in a room adjoining the Baptist Chapel, Councillor Percy Davies (chairman of the Council) presiding. There were also present Councillor John Lee, Mr. George Nicholls, Mr. William Richardson, and Mr. George Pilling (clerk).—The Clerk said he had received a communication from the district auditor stating that the audit of the accounts of the Council would take place on the 29th July, and he required his (the clerk's) attendance at one o'clock on the after- noon on that day.—The Clerk said he had received an official communication with reference to the hours during which polls should be open for the election of rural district councillors.—The Chair- man said he supposed the Council were aware that the application of the Corporation of Warrington with regard to the extension of the borough had I been granted. There was no doubt about that, but owing to the dissolution of Parliament the passing of the necessary Bill had been postponed. The Council of Latchford Without appeared to have nothing to do. In certain districts such a council as this would be of some use with regard to looking after common lands, &amp;c. They were asked by the Corporation, as a Parish Council, as a favour, to join the Parish Council of Stockton lieath, hut it ap- peared the last named body would have nothing to do with them. (Laughter.) With regard to the future of the Parish Council of Latchford Without, he supposed that what was left of them would be joined on to Grappenhall. The Clerk thought the parish meeting would be quite sufficient to carry on the business of that part of the district-, and they could do with the present Parish Council. They could under that arrangement do all that was re- quired to be done, and Vsey would only need to meet once a year except in case of emergency. After further discussion the subject then dropped and Councillor Lee having proposed a vote of thanks to the Chairman, which was seconded by Mr. Richardson, the meeting terminated. LAWN kl OWEES.—Gintann , Ironmonger, Warrington and Rarlestown. AMERICAN ROOKER CHAIRS, sale price, 12s. 6/. BAMBOO Minus. with two trays, sale price, 4s. 11. d.—J. and W. DUTTON, Bank ey-st., Warrington.— Advt. A PADGIATE FAMILY DISPUTE.</p>
        <p>At the Manchester Assizes, on Friday week,before Mr. Justice Cave and a jury, Alice Schofield, of Moston. claimed from John Berry, executor of Isaac Birch, £B5 for services rendered.— Mr. C A. Russell appeared for the plaintiff; Mr. Shee, QC ~ and Mr. Overend Evans for the defendant. —The claim was for attendance upon the deceased uncle of the plaintiff. Isaac Birch of Padgate. The plaintiff said that she was called in to nurse her un de during his illness, and that he promised to leave her £B5 in his will in return for her services. This he did not do. She had lost money by going to Padgate, and ha d been put to some expense.— llrs. Clayton, another niece of the old man, said that the plaintiff was not sent for by her uncle, but came upon a visit and that no remuneration was agreed upo n.—A further witness said that during Mrs. Schofield's stay at Fadgate she spoke to him a great deal. It seemed to him that she was very anxious that he should understand shat she was not with her uncle in any capacity, but that her services were "a labour of love." —Mr. Berry, the defendant, said that it was his wife who suggested that the plaintiff was to receive £B5, but he subsequently altered it.—Evid- ence was given that the two nieces often squabbled, and that the plaintiff had friends who visited her at her uncle's house.—The jury found a verdict for the defendant.</p>
        <p>The. Royal Laundresses say CrMobley's " STARCH Gtoi Is unequalled for Imparting a beautlfnl finish to starched Imen, &amp;c., either cold or boiling water starch. Mr. —, Corn. wall, says :—" My wife will not finish our linen without you, "Starch Gloss." From Mrs.—. Bradford "Dear eh.— lam pleased to inform you that I have tried many so-oallnd Starch Glazes and Polishes, hut find none equal to yours." It should be used In every house. Sold at Id., 3(1., and fid., e most starch sellers. If not in stuck will procure it. T OBITO3LN7. Bllckburn. PAroprietor.—Adst.</p>
        <p>It is officially announced that Field Marshal Loid Roberts has been appointed to command the forces in Ireland. The appointment will be dated the Ist of October next.</p>
        <p>The Committee of the Liverpool Junior Reform Club unanimouFly passed a resolution on Monday night restricting the meaning of the word " Liberal," used in the memorandum, to such persons only as are willing to give a general support to the Liberal party.</p>
        <p>A FATHER'S CULPABLE NEGLECT.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>STARTLING DISCLOSURES.</head>
        <p>STARTLING DISCLOSURES.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Borough Police Court on Tuesday morning, before Dr. T. Starkie Smith (in the chair) and Mr. S. Wright, Thomas MacMahon, 28, Warburton-st., was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Scot- land-road at 11.15 on Monday night ; he was further charged with wilfully neglecting hie children in a man- ner likely tto injure their health en that date and divers other times.</p>
        <p>Inspector McAteer, N.S.P.C.C., prosecuted,and stated to the Bench that defendant was the father of five child- ren, John (12), Dennis (9), William (7), Maggie (5), and Kate (2).0a Monday morning about 11.30 he visited the defendant's house in company with P.C.Clark and found the door open and no one inside. There were four wins all devoid of furniture. There was no fire in the place, and only an old tick lying in the corner of the kitchen. They went to another house in the same street and found the defendant's five children there, insufficiently clothed and in a very filthy condition. Their whole bodies were swarming with vermin, and the chemise of the youngest had to be burnt it was so filthy. The neighbours told him that defendant had left on Satur- day morning, and had not returned, while the children. were left in the empty house. Witness removed the five to the Workhouse, where they were left. He must ask their Worships to deal most sternly with this case, and make an example of the man to the many other drunken fathers and mothers in Warrington. The Inspector added that defendant was nothing but a lazy, drunken,. wastrel. P.C. Clarke stated that on Sunday night from a com- plaint he had received he visited the defendant's house 28, Warburton-street, and found the children there, and no fire except what a neighbour had just put together and lit. The youngest child lay on an old tick with noth- ing to cover it except a piece of eld wrapping. The eldest was sitting on the fender warming itself and they had not seen their father since Saturday morning though he had given the eldest boy a small leaf and *lb. of butter for them all to live on. Witness then reported the matter to Inspector McAteer and in company with him visited the house again and it was as he had stated. The little girl's chemise was literally " walking away "and it had to be burnt. Mrs. Floyd, 31 Warburton-street, said she had known, defendant 7 or 8 weeks and had several times washed the children who were often in a very dirty state. Mrs. Plumpton, 35, Warburton-street, had taken the children in her house the day the inspector came because the landlord turned them out and they had newhere to go. She bad never seen so many vermin in her life be- fore as there were on the children. Miss Curry, workhouse portress, stated that the chil- dren were dirty when admitted and thoueb short of clothing they were well nourished.—Detective Sergeant Monaghan proved the charge, against defendant, of drunkenness.—The magistrates sent prisoner to gaol , for one month with hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>P.S. A.-ANN UAL MEETING.</head>
        <p>THE P.S. A.-ANN UAL MEETING.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the above society was held in the Bold-street Wesleyan Church on Sunday afternoon when the position of chairnsan was occupied by Dr. T. S. Smith. There was a large attendance and after devotional exercises the chairman said that the association had been in existence about 2i years and it still continued to give satisfaction and encouragement to its promoters. The meetings, he said, were better attended each Sunday and their object was to get to- gether persons, chiefly of the working clase, who did not attend any regular place of worship. He thought an hour spent on Sunday afternoon in that service was cal- culatedto improve the moral and general condition of the people. Mr. T. S. KENT (president), who accompanied the chairman on the platform, further explained that they gathered those who attended no place of worship and endeavoured to brighten the lives of those who were not. bright, to strengthen the weak, and to fill up the gap at present existing between the youth of the schools and the manhood of the churches. The speaker apologised for the unavoidable inattendance of the band. Mr. T. WORTHINGTON, of Wigan, who was introduced as speaker for the afternoon, said he was pleased to meet them at the annual gathering of the P.S.A. He took it for granted that each of them was interested in the spirit of Christianity and that they were each of them interested in the prokress of humanity. They were sufficiently interested to come there that day. He thought that one of the reasons that they did not pro- gress so rapidly as they might and ought was that they . failed to grasp fully the duty of Christianity and failed sufficiently to have within them an enthusiasm calculated to inspire them at all times to do their duty. What was their duty ? As Christians it was their duty to expo 1 evil. There was no small charge laid upon their shoul- ders ; it was a great cause indeed that they were called upon:to support and to discharge. There were thing. that they might do greater than being a warrior. They had it on the authority of scripture that " the man who conquereth himself is greater than he that taketh a city.' He ventured to say that there were not msny of them that had fully conquered themselves. Further, be slid, that before they made this England a righteous Engls.nd there was a great deal to do. The speaker then took up the question of the more prominent evils such as ganote ling and betting. Of the latter, he said he had lately begun to take an interest in racing—not that he ever went to a race—but ho had been studying the papers where one half of what should be news space was taken up by racing tips. Those tips he had reckoned up and found that there were nine chances to one against any- body winning anything. They had also, he said, got to de-throne drink and licentiousness, and must insist that the watch committees gave such instructions to the police that the morality of a town should stand foremost, be- fore everyone yed everything. Further he pointed out that those great acts were not going to be performed bye whistling or praying but by actual work, and he ex- horted them by all mealss to pray but not to stop when they had prayed. As a climax he observed that in all probability there were lots of houses in this town,which, during the last 30 years had never been visited on behalf of Jesus Christ, but for politics not one load been trussed during the past election. Solos of superior quality were rendered by Miss Toneg, of Bolton, and Mr. Thurgate Simpson, of Manchester. The choir, which had been largely augmented, gave the anthem" Arise and Shine," and throughout the after- noon the proceedings were of a most pleasant character.</p>
        <p>WEEKLY REPORT OS THE HEALTH Or WARRINGTON. Week ending 13th July, 1895. During the week, which was the '2Bh of the. current year, the fqllowing births and deaths were registered :</p>
