      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <p>Mr. CHAMBERLAIN indulged in any pleasing fancies of this kind his schemes will have been com- pletely upset by the flowing tide of Tory re- action. A writer in one of the magazines for kis month describes Mr. CHAMBERLAIN as a tlitician who is "too clever by half., It may tot, be added that he has at this election been tin successful by half. So far from being etae necessary wheel in the machine of govern- reha w'll find that he and the whole group of who describe themselves as Liberal aidlicnL: can be done without. This, we con- remarkable er' most remarkable outcome of a 78 The present Parliamentary /lal°ri:-Y has undoubtedly been elected as a h --nnist majority. In other words the country , voted not for Conservatism pure and simple, but for Conservatism tempered by the Liberalism as it is—of the Liberal Unionists. As a 14. atter of fact, however, the election has resulted ...11413,,atting absolute power into the hands of out- Zee-out Tories. The consequences that might e eaxlrPsceadteyd from such a state of things beginning to manifest themselves. hultring the election the Tories, as well ti'le other section of the Unionists. th's4Y, made a leading feature in their addresses of 3e4 6uPPort they would give Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S Nkii-Socialistic schemes of poor-law reform, old- t. tmsasinns, universal insurance against acci- -14011 e,and for providing facilities to workingmen to chase their houses. This was one of the means lople,cliring votes and of showing that the old `sril has been thoroughly permeated by the ti: Liberal Unionism. But now that the elec- th lela are nearly over and a majority is obtained r.e iBa marked change in the language of the belles' The newspapers of the party have already hagilv nto depreciate and decry the schemes which ache won thousands of working-class votes. These Up:,rees' says the leading Tory newspaper, rest illaj " dubious fundamental assumptions," and it Lett: Y hints that the new Uovernment had e°nfine itself to giving relief to land- kilo 'IS and: further State aid to denominational rae4e's' As to the universal insurance of work- accidents we are told that " manufac- Qoh:g eaPital is beginning to rebel against th7taa,tly growing. interference, which is more 4414,aging than foreign competition itself." It WO-Ls' therefore, no prophet to forete:l that the Of divided councils which is inherent in tt:ti" government will soon begin to have its 04' in a Cabinet which conta;ns Lord SALISBURY tk ene'the hand and Mr. JoszPll CHAMBERLAIN On COther. The very largeness of the majority will epihe. the effect of developing differences of 00. The of will feel that they are 13 with or without the Liberal Unionist of i4htell,” and they will insist upon the adoption 144 heir Own policy in preference to that which Will ,een paraded for election purposes. This '4ePules the Liberal Unionists in cruel dilemma. hoe: will either have to humbly and submissively pa,tl3t the position of mere "items " of the Tory tiY) Without any set- *ate existence of their own, eY Will have to throw in their lot more or less orsetvedly with the Opposition. In either case thr c'eroment will suffer discredit in the country theeligh a shameless falsification of its pledges,and qa,,"waY will be prepared for a renewed ascen- eY of Liberal principles.</p>
        <p>Tun results of toe contests in T*o Results. Newton and Northwich divisions have been widely different. In 14;41 ot the untiring efforts of Mr. MOON and a number of active supporters Newton is as in fact more Tory, than ever. The majority 14;4ainst the Liheralswhich was well under a thous- -144 in 1892, is now fifteen hundred. The Tories 14 tei: We believe,touched their highest water mark i.et,N'ewton division at this election. They have () benefit of the reaction which. has set in all pit-1' the country, in addition to the heavy poll of 4t,eteal voters to which Mr. MOON alluded in his Rpodell On Monday night. There seems also to be agrr °und for the statement that the miners Ilv„e'llurnerous in one part of the constituency Lib- 'leen either indifferent or hostile to the ill Zia' eat's° on this occasion, just as they were Ince division where Mr. S. Woods tniale,T.3/41 beaten. They have, it is said, been Gev,„ by the unscrupulous cry that a Liberal 'rernent is responsible for bad trade. If this 144 the miners are likely to have a rude etlitlg• They will doubtless soon find that 6re(211 the one hand a Tory administration does t°4 : eel4sarilY mean a demand for a single extra 14Qa e°a), on the oth been hand a party has the tin Power which is bitterly opposed o all 4110 Of the miners and other workmen for Prort,,eerhours of labour and other legislative im- ti°ttian3;e4ts• The result of the Northwich iiic- 41 J Pleasing contrast with that of Newton. ,T, _ CuIttNNER has not only maintained the'rea,"o" ne:secured in 1892, butEhas actually Vat.Q in- hie majority by almost ;four hundred carry at. a time like this a seat in ,teapaire with more than sixteen hundred votes iS a remarkable performance and shows 1114e1), R„til,c'st unmistlkeable manner how corn- r-e,a'r J. T. BRUNNER has won the confidence -Di6et of the people amongst whom he has 4)(1 -arg Pro ortion of a singularly active tleefal life. P</p>
