        <p>THE volume of 175 pages of he which , contains a statement 1151,1.- pi, receipts and expenditure of the 't4;cll)7 °f Warrington for the year endingon 45vei 2411 Cannot be said to be as readable as a 14(41 ' hilt htiii it contains a good deal of infor,ma. Narelthich is interesting to ratepayers. It siovre N nr l °lleo 853 clearly and satisfactorily how the 411 y a.' c/e'----that is the money which is so cheer- Illihr tici handsomely provided by these same rateey- iti,-3. We do not propose enter upon a f"wr Zit of the „Penditure, but a reference tooa ths a h-ihe itcriss may not be without interest. On ,„N1111,14?et ei deputations, for instance' a .P.,°_ 'N4,117 neen said at one time or another, but the Nyittees which indulge in " daputating av irl cikeelared that the information they have.tl ~,tll're th" this means has been worth infini e y 410 -'4ll the 81 ilf4 acrte money spent. The assurances of I t'tk. en and councillors on that point mus 't‘lri el til :II 'll't final ; as to the expenses, set in ses, they are 4b rl P'ain figures in the financial statement. f.'4 ' erneterY Committee e 3 b has spent a modest tit the School Attendance Committee c° th l''fiently not able to rnana4e the deputation L'ilea: last year for less than £6 15s. tlid li t,418, howevar, shrink into insignificance li.sll4'n'" tB4 6d. for " expanses of deputation to 17shl ft '4° iqunicipal Corporations Association Ch N''-r)Ve i'Cilillefil rrinient Board, &amp;v...." The thirst for It‘ge ea,," en sanitar subjects seems to be a pro- -4(1 I„.°till 4 -t-4e e bf deputPtions. The health department, 4tlkB 11 t04,411e )sPent i ss. 6d. in sinding a deputa- -4i National ')-1 Health Congress at London, in sending deputations to Preston 1. -141°1. The night-soil department chs-</p>
        <p>bursed £l5 19s. 3d., the Longford manure depart- ment £l9 18s. 7d., and the intercepting depots de- partment £9 15s. for similar purposes. According to some people the benefits which Warrington have hitherto derived from the Ship Canal are ex- tremely vague. On the other hand, there is no vagueness about the expense to which Warring- ton has been put by the Ship Canal According to the borough accounts £l6 18s. 2d. was expended at the opening of the Canal, and at the opening of the Walton Lock a further £2l 10s. 3d. went "bang " like the Scotchman's " saxpence." Eleven pounds have gone in "taking levels of the river,' but these amounts all appear small when com- pared with the £B4 for "professional services re Ship Canal, Ltc.," and the £9l ss. 7d. which is entered under the heading of the Parliamentary expenses department for similar services. Several Church of England clergymen and their clerks are credited in these Corporation accounts hr fairly' substantial amounts for burial fees at the Come-i tery. The ,Rector and his clerk take the lion's 1 share-254 16s. 2d.; the Vicar of St. Paul's com- ing next with £24 10s. 2d., and the Vicar of St. Barnabas last with £8 us. What has been the total cost of pulling down the old wall and putting up the new gates in front of the Town Hall? That is a question which has been asked several times but never very definitely answered. But, according to figures given under the heading of Bank Park department, which we presume include all the items, the total cost of this improvement is £6OB 18s. 9d., of which £2OO was for fixing the gates. The fire department has lately come in for a good deal of criticism and it will be interesting to know that its total cost to the town is £462 13s. 5d., but out of this sum only £94 lls. 11d, goes to the firemen as wages.</p>
      </div>
