      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>BY OUR RUNCORN CORRESPONDENT.</p>
        <p>At last the election is over, and the Liberal vic- tory is as decisive as any of Sir John Brunner's supporters could have possibly wished for. Many would have been glad had the contest been a little earlier, for the prolonged excitement has not been altogether conducive to good feeling all round. The election was keenly contested,and it would not be overstepping the mark to say that each candidate has delivered forty speeches during the cam- paign.</p>
        <p>The poll was not quite so heavy as at the pre- vious election. In 1892,the number of electors who went to the poll was 9,905, whereas on the preseni, occasion the total number was 9,774, or 131 less. But notwithstanding this Sir John Brunner has obtained 126 more votes than he did at the election in 1892, whilst his opponent, Mr. Ward, has failed to poll as many votes as his pre' ,• or, Mr. White- ley did in 1892, there beiug s less against his name. It was the confident prAiction of the Tories that if Mr. Ward did not win, he would at least greatly reduce the majority, and it was pre- dicted again and again that Sir John Brunner's majority would not run above three figures. Be has, however, not only more than held his own, but increased his majority to 1,638. How are the Tory prophets fallen</p>
        <p>The question will be asked, how has this splendid victory of the Liberals been brought about? To those who have watched the contest from beginning .to end there could not be a shadow of doubt that the organisation of the Liberals was vastly superior to that of their opponents, and for this, Mr. William Handley must receive most of the honour. Thcir arrangements were exceedingly complete and the sub-agent of each district had at his command a band of voluntary workers who, throughout the contest, have displayed a great amount of energy and ability. To speak of Runcorn alone, Mr. John Clark at Runcorn North, and Mr. A. Riley and Mr. W. Carroll at Runcorn South, are deserving of all credit, and the same must be said of the assistants. On the day of the poll the Liberal Committee rooms presented as busy an appearance as any bee hive and were in striking contrast to the rather dismal and lifeless appearance which hung about the com- mittee rooms of the opposite party.</p>
        <p>The very active part which has been taken in the contest by Mr. Philip Speakman, J.P., C.C., has been exceedingly gratifying to a large number of Liberals. tie presided at the meeting when Sir John Brunner was again adopted as the Liberal candidate, and has also been the chairman at the Liberal meetings in Runcorn. lie has worked hard in other ways and his assistance has helped in no small degree to the glorious consummation of Wednesday.</p>
        <p>The victory is the more noteworthy inasmuch as it'is the only one for the Liberals throughout Cheshire, and Sir John Brunner goes back to Parlia- ment as the only Liberal member from the cheese county. Uncomforting as this must be to a great many Liberals, it is at least one grain of comfort, that the wave of Toryism in the country has not only been kept out of the Northwich division, but that Liberalism. has gained ground. Buoyed up with the Liberal reverses at Crewe and Stockport the Unionists were in hopes of sweeping the board in Cheshire, but Northwich will be a bitter pill for them to swallow. Personal considerations have, no doubt, as the Unionists allege, entered into the con- test, and many may have voted for Sir John Brun- ner because he is Sir John Brunner, but the victory is a glorious one for the Liberal party, all the same.</p>
        <p>Now that the election is concluded, and perhaps for a number of years Co come, we can again, settle down to the ordinary affairs of life and converse with our friends without entering into discussion as to the respective merits of Brunner or Ward, or speculating as to whether the electors will accept the motto "for—Ward " or otherwise. If bitter feeling has beencausedduring the strife all anmosi- ties should now be forgiven and forgotton. Let us bury the hatchet.</p>
        <p>Mr. Ward, the Unionist candidate, may be par- doned a little irregularity and excitement during the trying times he has just passed through, but there was one thing he said in his lively effusion at the meeting on Saturday, which showed that in his case at all events, coming events cast their shadows before. In this speech Mr. Ward has been indulging in a wholesale destruction of paper to wit hand- bills of the opposite party), and in reference to one similarly treated he said " /Jere is something which your member has to do with." Judging from this sentence it would seem that the foreshadowing of defeat had been so strong upon the orator that he was unconsciously betrayed into a prophecy which is highly creditable as prophecies go.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>ACCIDENT ON ELECTION Day..</head>
        <p>ACCIDENT ON ELECTION Day..</p>
        <p>A lad, 13 years of age, named Thomas McDonald, of 4, Friar-street Runcorn, had his shoulder bone broken, on Wednesday. Re was amongst the crowd which gathered around Sir John Brunner's carriage during his visit to Runcorn on the election day. After Sir John had addressed the crowd assembled at the Market placc the horses were unyoked from the carriage and a number of stalwart men dragged it along Bridge-street. It was during these proceedings that the lad in question was knocked down and run over. Dr. McKenzie attended to the lad's injuries.—A lad named Kennedy of Fryer- street was also pushed under the carriage wheels, but his injuries were found to he very slight. LOCAL WINNERB AT PHRISTON SHOW.</p>
        <p>We ;;;I;1- that Sir Gilbert Greenali, -Bart., of Walton Hall, Warrington,lias carried off a consider- able number of prizes at the show of the Royal Lancashire Agricultural Society which is being held this week at Prt.k ton. The prizes include a first for Channel Islanfl bull ; a first and third for Channel Island cow: a second for hunter, mare or gelding, qualified to carry 15 stone with hounds; a first tor hunter, mare or gelding qualified to carry 12 stone with hounds; a first in the hacks, cobs, and ponies class, for mare or gelding, 14 aud not.ex- eeeding 15 hands (to be ridden in the ring) ; a second for cob, mare Or gelding, 12-2 and under 14 hands, and a first for pony mare or gelding (to be ridden in the ring by boys under 15 years of age). Mr. S. B. Chadwick, of Crofton Lodge, Runcorn, has also been successful in winning a third pr:ze for a shire brood mare with foal at foot. SEWING MACHINES, Hand £2 18s. 6d.NTresdlel24 4s. with handsome vMnut cover.—J. and W. DUTTON, 16 and 18. Sankey-t.. Warrington —Advt.</p>
        <p>BRUNSWICK STREET WESLRYAN SUNDAY SCHOOL. The annual treat to the scholars and teachers attend• inv this school was held on Thursday afternoon. About 500 scholars proceeded by the 1.15 p.m. train to Frod- sham, and thence walked to Overton Hill. Amongst those present were the these superintendents. Messrs. John Simpson, T. Simister, and E. Taylor. Rain kept off until 3 30, when it began to fall heavily and stopped all out-door games. They proceeded to the sheds at Mersey View, and disported themselves until tea was served. A vote of thanks was afterwards passed to Mr. Simnson for his kindnesa in providing the treat, on the motion of the Rev. W. George. Home was safely reached about nine o'clock.</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER. SATURPAY, JULY 27 1895 RUNCORN AND WIDNES. NORTHWIC a DIVISION ANNIVERSARY SERVICES AT WESTON. ELECTION. Mr. James Sadler, of Batterley Hill. preached at the anniversary services in connection with the Primitive Methodist Chapel, Weston, on Sunday. Special hymns were sung. Mr.J. G.Smith presided at the harmonium. Good congregations assembled at both services, and the collections,which were in aid of the Trust Fund,realised £2 Bs. 6d. BALTON-ROAD FOOTBRIUGE, RUNCORN. Miss HOLSEfield, of Runcorn laid the feundation stone of the proposed footbridge over the Bridgewater Canal near the Crane,Halton-road,on Monday afternoon. The erection of the bridge, which has been strongly advo- cated by Mr. Walter Collier, is a part of the agreement between the Manchester Ship Canal Company and the Runcorn Urban Council. It will satisfy a long-felt want, especially to workmen, who are at present are obliged to take a circuitous route in order to reach the places where they are employed. RESCUE FROM DROWNING BY A RUNCORN BOY. On Thursday afternoon of last week, a youth named Thomas Newton of the Holloway, Runcorn, was bathing in the Ship Canal near to the pumping engiee, when he was seized with cramp. Another youth, named Plant, of Penketh-lane, went to his assistance, and was bring- ing him to the side when Newton began to struggle, and pulled his rescuer under the water. Seeing their pre- carious position, Samuel Minshull, aged 14 years, of 80, Greenway-road, plunged into the water, and being able to swim, succeeded in dragging the two lads to the side, when they were pulled out of the water by bystanders. Minshull, it may be added, has previously been instru- mental in saving two other lives. MASONIC PICNIC. On Thursday, the annual installation picnic of the Ellesmere Lodge, Runcorn, of Freemasons, took place. About 70 persons proceeded in two reserved composite coaches, by the 9.10 a.m. train to Rhyl. Conveyances were in readiness at Rhyl station to drive the party to the Westminster Hotel,where dinner was served, Dinner over, a drive was participated into Abergele, St. George, through Kennel Palk, to Glascoed. Permission was obtained to visit the Park. On returning to Rhyl, tea was served at the Westminster Hotel. After a ram- ble on the sands. through the town, the homeward journey was commenced. Runcorn being reached shortly after ten o'clock. DEATH OF A SHIP CANAL ENGINEER. Many will regret to hear ot the death of Mr. Harold William Abernethy, M.lnst.C.E., who was engaged in the construction of the Manchester Ship Canal on the Runcorn section, and during that time resided at Frod- sham. Shortly after completing the work he became ill, and in accordance with the orders of his medical attend- ant he went Out to South Africa. He was returning home on the Union steamship Scot, when on the 9th inst. he died and was buried at sea on the following morning. He was only 37 years of age. He was the son of Mr. James Abernethy, past president Inst.C.E. of Whiteness, Broadstairs, and 11, Prince of Wales- terrace, London. During his residence in Frods- ham he endeared himself to a large circle of friends, and was appoieted Worshipful Master of Cholmondeley Lodge of Freemasons (Frodsham). A RUNCORN ENGINEER AND MIS COMMISSION. Atthe Runcorn Couuty Court on Tuesday Henry Branch, engineer, Greenway-rd., sought to recover £4 ss. commission on sale of portable engine and bailer, from Harry Parkeman, timber merchant, Halton-rd., Runcorn, Mr. Burton appeared for elaintiff and Mr. Allen represented defendant. For the former it was stated that Parkeman asked Branch to obtain him a customer for any of his machinery and he would allow him a commission of five per cent. On June 29th he found him a customer in the person of Mr. Bugbird, for an engine and boiler, and they were purchased for £B5 which sum had been paid over. He now claimed for.the commission. Plaintiff having given evidence in support of the above, he acknowledged in cross-examination that he alga acted as agent for the purchaser. Mr. Allen said that his client denied that he ever employed Mr. Branch and that there was no commission ever offered, offered, and that if so it was not at the figure stated. His client would absolutely deny employing plaintiff, and further show that he did not try to sell the engine to Mr. Abel when he wanted to purchase it, but ran down the price. Therefore he did not act as an agent would. Plaintlff had received a promise of something from Mr. Bugbird, and Mr. Branch had no right to com- mission for both parties.—Flis Honour gave judgment for the defendant. THE SAD DROWNING FATALITY AT RUNCORN. An inquest was held at the Runcorn Police Station on Monday afternoon by Mr. J. E. Worsley, coroner, con- cerning the death of a lad named Joseph Sidley Dun- babin who was drowned in the Manchester Ship Canal OR the Thursday of the previous week. The first wit- nem; called was Joseph Sidley, who stated that he lived at Crescent Row. He was grandfather to the deceased and had brought him up. Deceased left the house about 1.30, on Thursday afternoon. He did not see him alive again.—George Alfred Rinsey, a lad residing with his parents at 80, Loch-street, said he went with the deceased to the banks of the Ship Canal on the date named. The deceased was fishing near the pumping station, and witness was standing near to him. Deceased was standing on a stone near to the water. He did not see the deceased fall in, but he hued a splash. Witness tried to save him by suspending his (witness's) legs towards him, but he could not reach. Three other boys who saw the deceased fall into the water went for assist- ance. A man came but before he could undress, the deceased sank.—Walter Harvey, 36, English Row, said he saw the deceased fall into the canal, Rinsey tried to reach him with his legs but could not. When the man came, the deceased sank for the last timo, and the man did not enter the water.—Mr. W. Shaw, missionary of the Runcorn branch of the Mersey Mission to Seamen gave evidence as to recovering the body, on Friday night by means of his grappling apparatus. There were two cuts on the face but these seemed to have been done by the hooks.—The Coroner after summing up the evidence said it was a very sad case for a boy to be lost in that way. He was sure they all sympathised with the grand- father who seemed to have kept him, educated him,and brought him up, in the loss he had sustained. A ver- dict that the deceased was accidentally drowned was returned. The foreman of the jury said they wished to congratulate Mr. Shaw, upon his excellent grappling apparatus, and to thank both him and Mr. Byfield for their ser vices.</p>
        <p>Whatever may in future be said of the North wich Division election of 1895, it will be long remembered as one of the keenest and stiffest " fights" that have occurred in the constituency. From one end to the other, both candidates have pursued their candidature with remarkable vigour. There have been speeches innumer- able, and the electors have been provided with a mass of political literature. The Liberal candidate's</p>
        <p>FINAL WORDS to the Runcorn electors, prior to the poll, were said at a crowded and most encouraging meeting in his support on Monday night. Not a vacant seat in the com- modious Public Hall was to be seen, and on the platform beside the Chairman (Mr. P. Speakman, J.P., C.C.), there were Lady Brunner, the Misses Taylor,Mr. Griffiths, M.P. for An4l-slea ; the Rays. J. Cole (Gars. ton), J. Stafford and T. Bramall ; Messrs. Dominic Daly (London), J. Flandley, J.P., S. Taylor, J.P., C.C., J. R. Firth, A. R. Norman, T. Handley, J.P., R. H.Posnett.T. Lea, D. Bisbrown, W. Handley, G. H. Kirkham, Joseph Bridgewater, M. L. Davis' E. Taylor, M. Sweetman, W. H. Chadwick, J. Hayes, B. Crosby, B. Kniveton, James Lea, and P. Phillips. It was not until nine o'clock that ISir John Brunner arrived from Winnington, where he had been addressing another meeting. His entry on the platform was the signal for an outburst of cheering and the singing of a political song. Mr. P. Speakman, who has taken a most active part in the contest, opened the meeting with a few well chosen remarks. From beginning to end he has urged the desirability of conducting the contest without per- sonalities, and on Monday he said he was pleased that it had been so well conducted. Fin contended it was a fight not for men but for principies—(applause)--and although at the present time there was gloom over their party, they knew from history that those troubles now and again took place ; but they had the satisfaction of knowing that out of the last 100 years the Liberals had governed the country for something like 75 years. (Applause.)—The customary vote of confidence was pro- posed by Mr. B. Kniveton, and seconded by Mr. R. H. Posnett, both local men, who made excellent speeches. Mr. Daly, of London, Mr. Griffiths. M.P., and the Rev. T. Braman supported the resolution.</p>
        <p>SIR JOHN 13RUBNER then rose to speak and had an en- thusiastic reception. He devoted the greater part of his speech to contradicting a number of statements which had been put forward. Some innocent remarks of his made at Weston Point in 1887 which were intended to convey that it would be a pleasure to him to see the watermen who were at Weston Point, late on a Satur- day, :tile to get home on Sunday had been interpreted to mean that he wanted the navigation open for trade on Sundays. He said they might take it from him that he desired Sunday to be a day of rest for the workpeople of this country as much as they did themselves. Another silly statement to which he gave an emphatic denial was that a man engaged at Messrs. Brunner's works had been discharged because he was 50 years of age. From enquiries made, Sir John Brunner said that the firm had within the last seven months taken on 60 men over 50 years of age. In referring to the contention of his opponents that Mr. Ward would improve the salt trade, whilst in the neighbourhood of Runcorn he would improve the alkali trade,he said it was like set- ting a fox to watch the chickens to put a Salt Union to watch the alkali trade. Sir John Brunner also found it necessary to give a second denial to another absurd statement made by his opponents that he stopped is. 6d. a week from h 4 workmen whilst he gave £5O weekly to Runcorn during the recent distress. Be characterised it as an absolute lie.</p>
        <p>A splendid meeting terminated with a few earnest words from Mr. Griffiths, M.P., who urged his hearers to return their old member.</p>
        <p>Another mass meeting of Liberals was held in Devon- shire-square, Runcorn, on Tuesday evening, and was characterised by a great deal of enthusiasm. lbe Rev. J. Simpson conducted the meeting, and the other speakers were Mr. D. Daley (London), Mr. B. Kniveton, Mr. J. R. Frith and Mr. W. Stan way. Sir John Brun- ner was at 1\ orthwich where a great Liberal demonstra- tion was held.</p>
        <p>MR. WARD the Unionist candidate addressed a meeting of suppor- ters in the Primitive Methodist Schoolroom, Greenway- r ad, on Saturday aftertwon. Mr. Lightburn occupied the chair, and addresses were delivered by the candid- ate and Mr. Wilcox, M.P. A lively Unionist meeting was held on Tuesday evening in the Public Hall. The meeting was not so well attended as that of the Liberals on the previous night, and a good number of those pre- sent were Liberals. Mr. Ward was quite in a tantram at the provocation he said he had received. He com- plained that the provocation had been intensed by the importation of one who was almost beneath contempt. A man who would come down and give the lie direct on a subject he knew nothing whatever about was a mean, contemptible cur. (Hisses and applause.) There was no other name for him. He was quite prepared, if that person was amongst the audience, to meet him any- where he liked and prove him to be a contemptible, scandalous liar. (Shouts of " Let us have politics.") "Yes, you shall have politics, my friend," retorted the candidate, "but here (holding up a handbill) is some- thing which yous member has to do with, and which I care this much for" (tearing the handbill into fragments and dashing the pieces upon the platform, amid hisses and applause.) Continuing, Mr. Ward said he bad lived too long in that district, and his character was estab- lished before Sir John Brunner was known to take any notice of it, and he did not care how much his opponents threw at him ;he was not going to follow suit. Mr. Ward then exhibited a handbill issued by the Trades Union calling upon the electors to vote for Sir John Brunner, and said it was issued before they had even waited upon him (Mr. Ward), and he cared nothing for the handbill (tearing it up and throwing it under the table amid hisses and cheers.) He procec led to speak of the increase in the export of salt from Runcorn and referred to the meassres the Unionists were prepared to carry out. The Hon. R. A. Ward, M.P., of Crewe, Mr. Pretty and Mr. Hughes (London), also spoke and were frciiuently interrupted.</p>
        <p>- PREVIOUS ELECTIONS. There have been four elections previous to the present one since the constituency was formed at the redistribu- tion of seats,and Sir T. T. Brunner has fought the battle of the Liberals on each occasion. In 1885 he was op• posed by Mr. W. H. Verdin, the nominee of the Conser- vative party and the result was as follows : Brunner 5,023, Verdin 3,995, majority 1,028. In the following year, Mr. Robert Verdin came forward as a Liberal Unionist, ard he was successful in wresting the seat from Mr. Brunner who was defeated by 458 votes ; the figures being Verdin 4,416, Brunner 3,958. It will be noticed that on this occasion there were many absten- tions and this was the cause of the defeat. In August , 1887, Mr. R. Verdin, M.P., suddenly died, and thus for the third time within two years the constituency was called upon to elect a member of Parliament. Mr. Brunner again came forward on behalf of the Liberal cause and Lord Henry Grosvenor, son of the the, of Westminster, was the nominee of the Conservatives and Unionists. Mr. Brunner was this time successful in re- gaining the seat for the Liberals, the result of the elec- tion being a complete reversal of the previous verdict. In 1892 on the Conservative party resigning office,it was in vain that the Northwicla Conservatives tried to get a local gentleman to attempt the task of unseating Mr. Brunner. At length an outsider was found in the per- son of Mr.GeorgeWhiteley,a cotton spinner of Blackburn and now a representative in Parliament far Stockport, land he fought the constituency in their interest. The result of the voting was—Brunner, 5.580 : Whiteley, r 4,325; a majority for Mr. Brunner of 1,255. On the present occasion the Conservative and Unionist party were successful in inducing Mr. Thomas Ward, ft local man, and a director of the Salt Union to become their candidate. Be has fought gallantly for them and his supporters have all along been confident that if they did not win they would at least decrease Mr. Brunner's majority on the last occasion.</p>
        <p>THE POLLING took place on Wednesday. The number of electors on the register is 11,788, and the polling commenced at 8 am, in 16 polling districts. Mr. William Handley was the agent for Sir John Brunner, and Mr. C. Hughes for Mr. Ward. A sub-agent was appointed by elch party in every district. Mr. John Clark was stationed at the Liberal committee room in Runcoin north and Mr. A. Riley and Mr. Carrall in Runcorn south. For the Union- ists, Mr. J. W. Lightburn was the sub-agent in Runcorn north, and Mr. A. Lightburn in Runcorn south. Each candidate had a host of workers and numerous convey- ances had been lent. Those who lent them to the Liberals for use in the Runcorn district were—Mr. P. Speakman, J.P.. C.C., Caotain Rodgers, Captain Rowlands, Messrs. T. Rigby, .J.F. (Frodsham Bridge), Mecullock (Liver pool), R. H. Posnett, John Richardson, T. Handley, J.P., A. R. Norman. Joseph Jeffs (Helsby), S. Woodward (Kingsley), S. Harvey. T. Lea, and J. W. Temperlev. The following supplied conveyances to the opposite party—Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Co Dr. Steele, Messrs. O. Ha zleb urst,J. P., C.Reynnlds,J.P. , W. Mortimer, J.P., A, Bates, S. Chadwick, H. Houghton, J. W.Light- burn, R. Abel, W. Hankinson, and Pakin. There was a great display of party colours, the yellow and blue of the Liberal party predominating. The greatest interest Was manifested in the contest at Runcorn. It is the largest town in the division,and it has a voting strength of nearly one-third of she whole division. In Runcorn North there are 2,143 names on the register, and in Runcorn South 866. For the convenience of voters there were seven polling booths. Of these five were at the Market Hall and two were at the Beard School in Victoria-road. As the vehicles were driven rapidly to and fro they were cheered and hooted. In the neigh- bourhood of the polling booths where the respective parties had their committee rooms, all was bustle and animation. Mr. Ward was early on the scene, and visited the Committee rooms. He afterwards departed by train for Northwich. In the afternoon</p>
        <p>SIR JOHN AND LADY BRUNNSR arrived in Runcorn by train,and were driven in an men barouche through the streets. Mr. P Speakman, J.P., C.C., accompanied the candidate and his wife. Is an (men carriage immediately behind them rode Mr. William Handley and Mr. J. W. Deakin, of Northwich. Ls the party proceeded along the streets they were ac-</p>
        <p>corded a most flattering reception. The Runcorn South Committee room was first visited, and here Sir John Brunner busied himself in seeing that the organisation was in good order, and in taking voters to the poll. He then proceeded amidst cheers to the residence of Mr. P. Speakman, where tea was partaken of. Again entering the carriage a visit was paid to the Runcorn North Com- mittee raom, and here a large crowd assembled who gave Sir J. Brunner a most hearty reception. Despite the rain which was falling at the time, he mounted the Market Hall steps and addressed a few words of encouragement to the crowd of workers and supporters. He thanked them for their reception and the efforts they had already put forth. He assured them that at the other stations they were working hard on his behalf with every prospect of success. Amid loud and continued cheering the horses were unharnessed from the carriage and Sir John and Lady Brunner were drawn along Bridge-street prior to their departure for Northwich. Polling became brisk after tea and there were large crowds in the vicinity of the two polling places. The partisans showed their feelings by cheering and hooting. There were no disturbances, however, throughout the day. Superintendent Atherton had taken the precaution to draft in an extra force of fifty , constables but their services were very little required. There was not much difficulty in dealing with the traffic and only one trap accident was reported. After the polls closed at eight o'clock a procession was formed near the Market Place and men, women and boys marched to the Liberal Club singing " We fought and won before, boys, and so we can again." The poll was not so heavy as could have been wished for,which was perhaps owing to the rain which fell heavily for several hours before the close of the poll. The result was received in Runcorn about half-past twelve, and watt as follows :</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>RIOT AT BANK -QUAY STATION.</head>
        <p>A RIOT AT BANK -QUAY STATION.</p>
        <p>"CLEANING OUT" A REFRESHMENT ROOM.</p>
        <p>Mr. Fenna, solicitor, for the London and North-Western Railway, appeared at the Warrington Borough Police Court on Wednesday morning to prosecute in two cases of persona travelling on the railway without ticket. Messrs. John Fairclough, J. Geddes, and J. H. Fleming were the magistrates. The first defendant, John Quinn, a powerful-looking young man was charged with riding on the London and North-Western Railway between Birmingham and Warrington without having paid his fare, on Tuesday, and with attempting to defraud the company of the same; he was further charged with obstructing the officer while executing his duty by assaulting him on the same date.—Mr. Fenna said the case was not an ordinary one. It arose out of a most disgraceful proceeding at Bank Quay Station on Tuesday -morning and he had not heard of suck disgraceful con- duct in the whole of his life. There was an excursion train from Birmingham to Aintree Races on Tuesday and it was full, tickets being collected at Bank I Quay Station. On arrival here one of the porters went to a compartment in which there I were seven passengers seated. He examined seven tickets and was just going away when his suspicions were aroused by the way the men were holding their legs, and stooping down, and he saw defendant's feet protruding from under the seat. Re informed the platform inspec- tor and they returned to the compartment and the de- fendant was then getting from under the seat, and an- other man gave him a return half-ticket which defendant tried to pass off as his own. This was refused and the unruly conduct commenced ; the inspector and porter Welt) hustled about and those in the carriage would not let hien get near the door. A crowd of some 150 or 200 disorderly excursionists gathered round the door and tried to prevent the man being apprehended. A large number of the men rushed into the refreshment room smashed all the glass ware on the counter, struck the waitress in the chest, and eventually swept the bottles of liquor from the shelves. Defendant took part in a I violent way in preventing Turner, the officer, from executing his duty, and the scene became in the end a complete riot and other assistance had to be got before the man was apprehended. Mr. Fenna added that there had been card and dice playing in the compartment, and also that in consequence of the roughness of the excursionists several of the regular passengers had bookcd separately. He pressed for an exemplary Penalty as the magistrates unfortunately could only in- flict fines. _ _</p>
        <p>James Whittaker, platform porter, gave corroborative evidence and stated that defendant aided the others in the carriage in their obstruction by pushing from behind. He (witness) was kicked repeatedly. John Turner, platform inspector, stated that he was struck in the chest and kicked when attempting to enter the carriage. Several men offered to pay defend- ant's fare hut he himself made no offer. The train was delayed 15 to 20 minutes before defendant was given into custody. The magistrates fined defendant 40s. and casts or one month's imprisonment with hard labour in default. . _ . .</p>
        <p>George Fair and Anthony Sherlock, Stockport youths, were fined Sc. including costs or 7 days each for travel- ling on the London and North-Western Railway from Frodsham to Warrington without fares on Tuesday.— Mr. Fennfs also prosecuted in this case.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>J. T. BRUNNER (L) 5,706 T. WARD (C) 4,068 --- Liberal Majority</head>
        <p>J. T. BRUNNER (L) 5,706 T. WARD (C) 4,068 --- Liberal Majority</p>
        <p>SIR JOHN F. BRUNNER AT RUNCORN. -TRIUMPHAL PROGRESS.</p>
        <p>' In Runcorn it was expected that the result of the elec- tion would become known about twelve o'clock, hut before that time a large crowd gathered in the precincts of the Liberal Club anxiously waiting for the tidings. They wiled away the time in lustily singing the election r songs which had been composed by Mr. Handler. Shortly before half-past twelve the telegram with the result, arrived. The figures having been read out, a scene of unbounded enthusiasm followed. The tid- ings of great joy were received with a shout 1 of triumph which must have announced to the whole town that the result , was far from being a disappointment to the Liberals. About nine o'clock the successful candidate arrived at the railway station. He was met there by a great crowd whose en- thusiasm knew no bounds as he stepped into the carri- age which was awaiting him. He was accompanied by Lady Brunner, and Mr. Philip Speakman, LP., C.C. A triumphal procession was formed, the brass band struck up a lively tune, and with the Unicorn Jack floatiel proudly in the forefront amid a number of torchlights the mass of humanity wended its way slowly along. Low- land's-road to Devonshire Square,singing election songs, while the spectitors at every available win- dow en route cheered lustily. It was a re- ception worthy of the occasion and one that rill net soon be forgotten. On arrival in the Square,Sir John addressed the great assemblage of people. Be said : I really cannot express my heartfelt feelings to the men and women I see around me to-night. May God grant that I may do my duty to you. (Cheers.) Never man had such a noble set of friends as I have. I can but pro- mise that I will try to be true to you. (Cheers.) Run- corn has from the beginning of this contest intended to win—(cheers)—and when Runcorn makes up its mind to win a battle, the battle is won. (Cheers.) I wanted my friend, Mr. Speakman—(cheers)—and Mr. Bratt, who had seen Nurthwich to-day, to see the whole of the constituency, and I am very glad to be able to bring Mr. Speakman back with me to-night, and to tell you that Northwich, Middlewich, and Winsford, are just as true to the Radical cause at this election as even Runcorn has been. (Cheers.) I have heard that in days gone by, that Winsford has not been able to speak its opinion.but I think to-day they have conquered as you have. Let us therefore have a kind thought in our heart for these places—Northwich, Middlewich, and Winsford—and remember that each and all alike have good men who have done the cause of Liberalism gallant service. (Cheers.) When shall we have a fiztit again? (Cheers and " never.") lam bound to tell you my conviction that the Liberals in this constituency have given the Tories such a lesson as they are not likely to forget in this century. (Cheers.) Will you accept from me and my wife our fervent and warmest heart-felt thanks (cheers).—The carriage was then driven along Regent-street, and Church-stree', and thence to Halton. A short stoppage was made here, and the carriage then proceeded to Sir John Brunner's residence at Winnington. For some time after his departure from Runcorn, a number of 1) r)ces- sionists paraded the streets of the town.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>AT THE GLADSTONE CLUB.</head>
        <p>AT THE GLADSTONE CLUB.</p>
        <p>After the declaration of the poll, the newly-elected member, followed by a large crowd c f supporters, pro- ceeded to the Gladstone Club, and, addressing an en- thusiastk gathering from the balcony, said they had at last made an end of every show of Toryism in the North- with Division of Cheshire. He wanted them to let him retain their friendship ; he wanted nothing better than to win and retain the friendship of all the Tory !salty of that constituency, because he desired their help as well as the help of the Liberals, for he was now the member of the Liberal party for the whole county of Chester. (Cheers). With the help of both Liberals and Tories, he desired anel expected to fulfil his duty towards them io the fature as he had done in the past. (Cheer,). Five thousand seven hundred and six Liberals hod 'anted for him that day, and he wished he had 5,706 good right hands to shake with them all (cheers.) He wanted them to realise that wherever Liberals were gathered that day and at that host they were cheering at the result of the North tvich election. All property tn INnrthwich. (Cheers.)—Sir John Brunner afterwards afl- dresaed his supporters within the club, and said that their victory at the close of a disastrous general election would he insniring, ftfigniriting, and cheering to the whole of the Liberals of the United Kingdom. He was told that he was to be the member of the cot stituency until the next century. (Laughter.) They must not be two sanguine about Viet. Ths Tories would not re- main in office so long, for he was satisfied that they would show themselves so utterly unworthy of the posi- tion they now occupied that before the twentieth century arrived they would be down in the dust (cheers. Sir John Brunner then left the club, and entered hie carriage. The horses were quickly unharneseed, and hundreds of willihg hands dragged the vehicle and its successful occupant through the town and on to Sir John's residence, Winnington Old Hall. The procession, numbering seine 5,000, was headed by a braes band, and the cheering all along the route was deafening. The whole population turned out and enthusias- tically waved hats, banners, and all sorts of Liberal emblems. On arrival at the hall, Sir John Brunner said :—My friends and neighbours, there is no miatrike about it that this has bean a victory for the " Chimic " (cheers), and I hope , the good rule, the good feeling and the good I manners that have obtained at the " Chimic " will he followed by all the people of this constituency—(cheers) —that the employers will think as well of their men,and that the men will think as well of their employers, all over this constituency. (Loud cheers.) But mind,in my opinion, it is necessary for the employers to deserve well of their men—(cheers)—and then the men will deserve welt of their employers. (Renewed cheering.) I am satisfied about it that it Is because there has been such a thorough good feeling, such kindly consideration on both sides in good old Winnin,gton that we have won this fight. (Cheers.) CONGRATULATIONS.</p>
        <p>During the afternoon Sir John Brunner received shoals of telegrams congratulating him upon his victory. Amongst them waq one from the Reform Club, London, which read :—" Members astonished and delighted at your enormous majority." The Liverpool Reform Club wired :—" Hearty congratulations to the one bright spot in Cheshire. Members of the Reform Club de- lighted." The National Liberal Club, the United King- dom Alliance, the Liverpool Junior Reform Club, and a great many other political associations also telegraphed their felicitations. THE DEFEATED CANDIDATE AND THE RUNCORN</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>VOTE.</head>
        <p>VOTE.</p>
        <p>Addressing his supporters at the Constitutional Club, INorthwich, after the declaration of the poll, Mr. Ward, the defeated candidate, in accounting for his defeat,re- ferred to the dead weight of the large firm of Brunner, Mlnl and Co., which he said was good enough for over 1,000 votes against any man. In referring to Runcorn, he said he found there that the better class of artizana, and generally speaking all the carpenters, joiners, ko., were almost universally in favour of the Unionist side, but there was the great mass of ordinary laboureii who had been employed at the chemical works, and the ordinary labourers who had been receiving relief—and probably they ought not to say a word against such men—which formed the dead weight to hang round the neck of the Unionist party, and which had pulled them down. (Choerq.) It was almost impossible to fight against this dead weight lof labourers, who chiefly assembled round the chemical works. Take that away, and Unioni,rn would have won in a canter, and he had not the least hesitation in saying that in all the portions of the district where thay had I not these odds to contend against they bad won. (Cheers.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>LIBERAL DEMONSTRATION AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>LIBERAL DEMONSTRATION AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The state of the weather en Saturday was not favour- able to an open air demonstration, but it did not deter the Thineorn Liberals from holding one, wt,ich was in every way a success. After parailinz the town, headed by a brass band, they proceeded to Devon shire squa^e, where a mass meeting was held. Over one thousand persons were present, and although there were one or</p>
        <p>two showers of rain during the proceedings, the meeting was continued without interruption. Amongst those</p>
        <p>present on the furry were the president,Mr.Philip Speak- man, LP., C.C., Sir John Brunner, Bart., Messrs. T. Handley, J.P., J. McFlugh,Billam (president Watermen's Federation), J. Callie (Liverpool), A. R. Norman, J. D. Bishro.wn, E. Gatley, B. Crosby, J. Hayes. Revs. J. Stafford and T. Bramall.—Mr. McHugh referred to a paper, Mr. Ward, the Conservative candidate, read be- fore a Debating Society at Northwich,in which he spoke against Trades' Unions. He advised his hearers to vote for Brunner on the following Wednesday.—Mr. Billarn then addressed the meeting, speaking on Local Veto.— Sir John T. Brunner, who on rising met with a most enthusiastic reception, said he was asked a few days ago at Halton "who was this Mester Ward ?" (Laughter.) He did not think that anyone had asked Mr. Ward who he (Sir John Brunner) was. (" No, no.") Let him say a few words in support of what Mr. Billam had said. He was an advocate of Local Veto. (Applause.) He was so bec Awe he wanted to put the power in their hands, in which he contended it ought to rest. He had been striving to put more power and more power in their hands. (Applause.) The people had never had any power put into their hands but what they had used it wisely and well for the benefit of their fellow men. (Applause.) He was satisfied of this that when they (the people) got the power of the control of the liquor traffic they would use it so wisely and kindly that after twelve months they would thank God for it. (Applause) They knew him to be a good employer of labour (Applause.) He thanked God that he had never had soldiers in his works. (Applause.)lllFle started to build the work. at Winnington in 1873,and he deter- mined to be friendly with the men's trades' unions. The very first men they got in their works were sent from the Manchester Trades' Union. (Applause.) They asked for men who would do credit to the union and credit to</p>
        <p>t hem, and from that day to the present they had had peace in their works. They had never had a quarrel, never had a strike or a lockout. (Applause.) It was the result, pure and simple, of their having made up their minds, that they would have careful reason and conciliation between employer and employe. If they voted for him on Wednesday it would show that they were in sympathy with good employers. They (Messrs. Brunner Mond and Company) were not simply content to have friendly relations with the trades' unions but they deciderl to give a week's holiday to every steady man and a week's pay. (Applause.) The result had been that they never missed the loss of the week's wages. The prospect of getting a holiday made the men steady, and kept them to their work. Ten years ago only 43 per cent., of the workmen gained the holiday but there was now 92 per cent.—(applause)—who were steady enough to get a holiday. That meant that the work done by steady men was good work, and he recommended employers to follow their example in this matter. (Applause.) Six years ago they decided to have three shifts instead of two. The men used to work seven days a week and 12 hours a day. That, he contended, was a dog's life and not a man's. (Hear, hear.) When they decided to have three shifts of eight hours each, they thought it would be necessary to reduce the wages ten per cent. This they did, but at the end of 12 months they found the i ,plan so satisfactory that they gave the Imen ten per c:ant, back again. (Applause.) They, therefore, paid the same wages for eight hours work as 12. The men worked so much more cheerfully, so much more willingly and efficiently that it did not cost the firm a copper more. (Applause.) If they voted for him they would be voting for an eight hour working day instead of twelve. (Applause.) They would declare to every employer that it was their duty to follow the example which had been set them in that matter. In conclusion he hoped that his name would not only be on top of the ballot paper but at the top of I the poll. (Applause.) Two questions having been handed in, Sir John Brunner in answer to the query " Why did the Liberal Government give the Duke of Saxe Coburg the sum of £lO,OOO a year ?" said the duke mi7ht hoe ot e. but Mr. Gladstone persuaded him tn - 'Sir John Brunner) would have thought better of him if he had taken none. (Applause.) He had known people declare that the Monarchy cost us a lot of money. He asked them not to be persuaded in that belief ; the cost of the Queen and the Royal , family was as a drop in the bucket as compared with Ithe cost of the House of Lords (Applause.) In answer Ito the question " Why did the Liberal Government pen- sion the Speaker of the House of Commons while they refused to give the unemployed any assistance ?" he said in the first place, the Liberal Governmnit did not refuse to give the unemployed any assistance, and in the second place, the Speaker was the first commoner of the country. He was the head of the re- presentative house, and if such a man did his work well he should be honoured. Proceeding, he said he understood Mr. Ward had asked them to establish the policy of the prevention of pauper alien immigration. But he wished them to remember that the number of poor people who came into this country to stay was very small,but the number who passed through to go to America and elsewhere was large. This country had long had the reputa- tion of being a place of refuge for the politically op- pressed. He asked them as Englishmen to keep up that reputation. This he would say, that if any people came here of whom it was quite clear that they were grAng to be a charge on the poor rate and unable to sup- port themselves, he would exclude them with a strong hand. (Applause.) Since he became a candidate for Parliamentary honours he had been confronted with the question as to whether it was right that a son, earning small wages, should be compelled to return to the Guardians the amount that body gave in relief to his father or mother. Hitherto, he acknowledged, he had said yes, hut he had come to the conclusion that if they took money from a man and his family when young, they prevented him from saving for himself for hi i own old age. He believed that old neoph shctild be treated kindly and considerately, but he was in favour of the poor law being altered so that no man should be asked to return that which the Guardians had given to his parents.—Mr. Cattle afterwards addressed the meeting which concluded about five o'clock.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this Council was held at Dutton Work- house on Tuesd4y afternoon. Mr. .1. Mclntyre, J.P., occupied the chair, and there were also present :—Ad- miral Parry, Messrs. J. Knowles, J. Weir, T. Lewis, J. Stokes, T. Wright, C. Mounfield,W. Bradford,T. Darling- ton, J. M. Williams, T. Baxter, C.C., H. E. Heywood, J. B. Chadwick, J. Brundreth, S. Woodward, H. Talbot, and Mr. J. Ashton (clerk), Mr. G. Ashton (deputy clerk), Dr. Adams (medical officer), Mr. Diggle and Mr. Swinton (surveyors), and Mr. Farrington (nuisance in- spector).</p>
        <p>THE SPACE REQUIRED IN DAIRIES AND COWSHEDS.</p>
        <p>A letter was read from the Local Government Board adverting to the regulations proposed to be adopted by the Runcorn Rural Council exempting existing dairies and cowsheds from the operation from the clause 3 of the Dairies, Cowsheds, and Milkshops order of 1885. It appeared to the Board that it was essential that the re- gulations should be complied with, with respect to dairies or cowsheds erected previous to the coming in- to) force of the regulations as well as to those erected subsequently. As the Board had already stated, they considered that the amount of cubic space required was less than should he required, They trusted, therefore, that the Rural District Council would proceed to adopt regulations without that suggested proviso. The Chair- man said the Act as it stood would never be carried out. —The Clerk stated that it had been pointed out before that the county could not comply with the regulations of the clause in question.—Mr. Weir said he did not think there were twenty shippons in the county that had the space required by the Act.—The Chairman said he would like it to be thoroughly understood, so that the county would know exactly the demands that they were requested to carry out. The space required in shippons was 450 cubic feet.—Mr. Stokes said be would like to know who made such regulations. They had been accustomed to deal with cattle all their lives, and they knew that such an amount of space wat not necessary.—The Chairman said there was no doubt that the Authorities bad veterinary inspectors to assist them in making the regulations.—Mr. Knowles said there were many a hundred shippons which were unfit to tie beasts up in.—The Chairman said he would not like to be one to enforce such a law to be carried out.—Mr. Woodward said he would move that the clause in ques- tion be eliminated from the bye-laws. If it was en- forced it would create a revolution in every village in their district. Three-quarters of the existing buildings would have to be altered. It was altogether impractic- able.—Mr. Bax N-r said he did not think there were any shippons with 450 cubic feet of space.—Mr. Woodward said if there wys a possibility of getting the clause ex- empted by pressing it home to the Local Government Board he would withdraw his resolution.—The Chair- man thought they might do a little more squeezing.— Mr. Williams moved that they reply to the Local Government Board, stating that under ex- isting circumstances the Council could not see their way to adont the regulation that was praptsed, inasmuch as it would cause a revo- lution in the matter of cowsheds throughout the county. They were anxious to adopt the Acts without the recom- mendation in question, but if it was persisted in, it would end in their dropping the Act.—Mr. Heywood seconded the proposition.— The Chairman said he thought it would be found there were very few cow- sheds with 450 feet space. If they adopted the clause they would require new shippons from one end of the county to the other.—Mr. Woodward said he could find some Government shippons which were not in accord- ance with that regulation.—Mr. Stokes thought it should be known that they had sent back the regula- tions to the Local Government Board twice before.—The motion was passed unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>MORE COMPLAINTS AGAINST THE SHIP CANAL.</head>
        <p>MORE COMPLAINTS AGAINST THE SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>. Mr. Willliam B. Cook, clerk to the Thelwall Parish Council. wrote stating that he was instructed to draw the Council's attention to the foul condition of the water in the Manchester Ship Canal, and the unhealthy and nox,ous stench which emanated therefrom.—Dr. Adams said he understood that Stockton Heath was also taking action with rPgard to the same matter.—Mr.Baxter said the Cheshire County Council had had several cemplaints about it.—The Clerk thought it was a matter for the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <p>5 , County Council to deal with.—Mr. Weir said the stench ; was not half so bad since the rain we had had lately.— , It was deci led that the Clerk send the communication to the Manchester Ship Canal Company. A DANGEROUS ROAD AT FRODSHAM. A communication was read from Mr. C. D. Parkinson, of Overy Bank, Frodsham, drawing the attention of the Council to the very narrow and somewhat dangerous road between Five Crosses smithy and the bottom of his garden. As the length of road was very short he would give sufficient land to make a good one, provided tbe Council would undertake to erect a fence to his approval. —A small committee was appointed Co see Mr. Parkinson as to the fence he required, and to report to the next meeting of the Council. THE SWING BRIDGE AT \TALTON. Mr. Swinton reported that the committee appointed at the last meeting of the Council to report on the alleged turning of the Stag Inn swing bridge, Walton Inferior, by the Manchester Ship Canal Company, against the public, in order to allow ballast trains to pass by the bridge, was called to meet on Wednesday, the 17th inst. Only Mr. Weir and himself attended. They considered the case a gross encroachment on the rights of the trav- elling public by the Ship Canal Company turning the bridge for the railroad traffic, and suggested that Vie Council request them to discontinue interfering with the public right at once.—Mr. Weir said he had made enquiries and had learned that a trap was stopped on one occasion in order that the bridge might be turned for a ballast train to pass.—Mr. Knowles said he had been un,able to learn that anyone had been stopped in cons( quenee of the bridge being turned for the purpose men- tioned.—lt was decided that Mr. Swinton's report should be sent to the Manchester Ship Canal Company. THE FLOODED ROAD AT THELWALL. Mr. Swinton, surveyor, reported that with reference to the road under the railway and canal bridges at Half- acre-lane, Thelwall, Mr. Burgess, the late road surveyor suggested to him that the Highway Authority, the Canal Company, and the Railway Company ought to share in the responsibility. Since opening the sewer he had met Mr. Wiswall,engineer for the Canal Company, and asked him to provide and fix three trapped iron gullies, which he would recommend his company to do. The report was adopted.</p>
        <p>MEDICAL OFFICER'S REPORT. Dr. Adams reported that another case of scarlet fever had occurred at Pepper-street, Appleton. Four cases of scarlet fever had occarred at Weston Point, two in Sandy-lane, and two in Post Office Row. The case re- ported previously at M-ore had terminated f atal y. He further repr.rted that th.&gt; condition of the privies and ashpits at Weston Point were both insanitary and dis. graceful and constituted in his opinion a nuisance, dangerous and injurious to health.—Tnis report was adopted.</p>
        <p>WESTON WATER SUPPLY. Mr. Farrington reporting on the water supply of Wes- ton, stated that he visited the township on the 16th inst. and found that the water was turned off for a time for the purpose of fixing a new valve in the mains. He went over the portion where there was an insufficient water supply, along with an employe of the Runcorn Urban District Council, and the information obtained from him fully confirmed the surmise contained in his last report that the intermitency and insufficiency arose from the service pipes being too few in number, and in some of them ths connections with the mains might possibly tiave become defective, in fact this had been found to he so in one case which was pointed out to him. Also the main in the lower road might have become too small either by corrosion or by having to supply a great many more houses which had been erected in and about the road since it was laid. He would suggest that the main should be opened and examined by and at the cost of the Runcorn Urban Council. The conneotions of the service pipes should also be examined. Ihe cost should be paid by the owners of the various properties. Mere service pipes should be put in.—The Imp actor was informed to serve notices where he found an insufficient supply. NOTICE OF MOTION. Mr. Woodward gave notice that at the next meeting he wAuld move tnat the Cheshire County Council be asked to take over the main roids leading from Runcorn to Northwich, and from Acton Bridge to Stretton. This concluded the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
