      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>WIFE KICKING OUTRAGE. At the Warrington Borough PDlice Court on Tuesday morning, before Dr. T. S. Smith (chairman), and Mr. S. Wright, Joseph Birchall, Antrobus-St., was charge on a warrant with assaulting and beating his wife on the 21st July.—Mrs. Birchall, the complainant, said that on Sunday night she was in a neighbour's house as defend- ant had turned her out of her own, when be rushed in and seized her by the hair and kicked her tinder the ear (pointing to a lacerated wound there) and also thumped her, causing the bruises on her face.—The neighbour in whose house she was gave corroborative evidence, which was supported by another woman.—P.C. Clark stated that on Sunday night at 11.20 some little • girls informed hint that a man was beating his wife down by the "gorse and Jockey." He went there and saw the woman bleeding badly from the ear, and she told him who had done it. Witness went and saw pri- soner, who was under the influence of drink and very excited. Defendant had been up for a similar offence in 1885.—The magistrates bound him over in the sum of £5 and himself in a surety of £lO to keep the peace for six months or go to gaol for a month with hard labour. Detendant's wife applied for a separation order, which, however, was not granted,while the sister of defendant who appeared to have had some intention of paying his fine told him he would have to go to gaol for the month.</p>
        <p>MINOR CASES. WARRINGTON BOROUGH COURT. FRlDAY.—Before Alderman Harrison (chairman), and Mr. S. Chandley. ANOTHER DELAY.—Nothwithstanding that objection was taken to 40 minutes delay last Friday awing to the non-appearance of magistrates, yesterday was scarcely any better, for Alderman Harrison was the only justice present at 10 o'clock, the remaining J.P. being a full half-hour late, and even then a magistrate appointed for another day had to be reqfisitioned. OLD BIRDS IN POSSESSION OF FINE FEATHERS. —Sarah Hickey and Eliza Woods, Percival's Fold, both well- known to the police, were charged with being found in possession of two new hats, two pairs of corsets, four aprons, two shawls, two skirts and one bodice, sup- posed to have been stolen on Wednesday.—P.C.'s F. Cooper and J. Cooper stated that they received infor- mation that two women had been seen in Mersey-st. in possession of a quantity of new clothes and that they had been seen with two men. Witnesses went and found the defendants in possession of the goods. They were brought down to the p Ale° station where they stated two men had given them a sovereign each and they bad bought the goods. Witnesses further stated that they found the goods under the bed at the lodging- house of defendants.—Defendants were remanded until Saturday for enquiries to be made. • Una BIRCH FOR TWO Boss.—Albert Melton,l3 Mill-street and John Chadwick, 4, Mill-street boys were charged with stealing from the back yard of George Brown, 18 Arplef-street,on Wednesday,4 tame and reclaimed doves —Mr. Hunt stated that the cemplainant was Mr. G. J. Brawn, cashier for Messrs. Clare and Ridgway, and re- siding at 18 Arpley-street. The doves, produced, value 3s. were seen safe about 7 30 and were missed at 11 30 Information was given to the police who subsequently found the doves at the shop of a dealer from whom they were traced to defendants who were brought face to face and admitted the theft.—Mr. Brown, the complainant, asked the bench to deal leniently Pith tne defendants as he thought they had given way to temptation.—The magistrates said they could not look over the offence but they were unwilling to send the lads to prison,and there- fore ordered the elder boy Malton to receive 12 strokes of the birch and Chadwick six. MIsIIEHAVINCI AppRENTICES.—A warrant was issued against Fanny Ragan for having left her work on the sth June and put her employer, Mrs. Mills, fustian cutter, to a loss of 55.—John Bolton obtained an order for the payment of 61. a week against Mary E. Mather. his apprentice, for leaving her work on the Ist July and putting him to a loss of ss. leloemee.—Before the Mayor (chairman), Alderma Harrison and Mr. F. Monl.s. WILTUL DAIdAOE.—MAXISILIM PENALTY.—Annie Smith, a violent character, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Guardian-st., on Saturday morning; she was also charged with wilfully breaking windows to the value of 55., the property of the Warrington Guardians. —Mr. B. Shearer, enquiry officer, prosecuted and said that the woman came to the Workhouse and asked for admission. She was drunk and was refused entrance but she would not go away. An officer was sent for and meanwhile the prisoner smashed nine panes of glass one after another. When the officer came considerable diffi- culty was experienced in getting her away. Mr. Shearer added that she had only just come out of gaol for a , similar offence, and she was a continual nuisance to I them. He pressed the case against her as he had done, in previous instances.—Coreoberative evidence having been given by Mr. Garnett, who was in the Workhouse office at the time of prisoner's appearance, the latter was sentenced to the maximum penalty—one month's hard labour on the first charge and two on the second. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—Margaret Leonard, no ad- dress, for this offence was sent to gaol for one month's hard labour as being incorrig- ible Patfick Baldwin, Arneld-yard, Hay- doci-st. •, Mary Hewitt, Union-St., Chester ; Margaret Griffin, Warhurton-st. ; Jane Johnson, Morris'-yard, Silver-st. ; Michael Kenny, 100 Pierpoint-se., were all fined 55. including costs ;Charles Groves 17 Glasshouse- row, for two offences was fined 51. in the first case and 54. and costs in the second ; Thomas Gresty, 60 Lord Nelsen-st erdered to pay 2a. 81. to the poor brx ; Jana Mather, 3 Riehy-st., was charged with being drunk en the 17th July in Buttermarket-et. After the case had been proved the Mayor enquired how it was she had been summoned after being taken home as she was not disorderly. Inspector Spinks explained that the woman had for some time been addicted to drink and had pawned her husband's clothing and things in the house. The magistrates agreed that this put another aspect on the case and adjourned it for a week as defendant had 'not appeared. SLEEPING OUT.—Marnret McLoughlin for sleeping , out in a shed without visible means on Sunday muruinn, I was fined 54. and costs. • Petra AND Toss.—Martin Dolan and Thomas Fogarty, Ben•y's-yard, were charged with playing pitch and toss in Erwood-st., on Sunday afternoon—P.C. Barlow de- posed to seeing 12 men " tossing "in the street named on Sunday afteroon. Fie came upon them unawares and apprehended the two defendants while in the act. —Each was fined ss. including costs. NEGLECT OF FAMILY.—John Ayres, 2, Berry's-yard, was chareeti en a warrant with having on the 11th June, unlawfully neglected his family whereby they be- came chargeable to the Warrington Union.—Mr. Shearer said that the prisoner's wife gild two children were ad- mitted to the Workhouse on the 11th June. Defendant was then in town and could have continued in regular employment if he had been so minded. Both he and his family bad spent most of the winter in the Workhouse. —The magistrates unhesitatingly committed him to gaol for one month with hard labour. ALLEGED WIFE ASSSULT.—Ed win James Gregory, 56, Horsemarket-st., a respectable man, was charged with assaulting and heating his wife Ahce,on the 20th July. —Mr. Percy Davies appeared for the defence and Mr. Fowler for the prosecution —After some discussion an agreement was come to between the solicdors that on condition defendant kept away from his wife for a fort- night an adjournment for that time he asked for.—Thie the magistrates consented to and the case was adjourned tor that period. 7</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
