      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <p>.k• ??'</p>
        <p>4'</p>
        <p>`,l y - r,„,/,</p>
        <p>WHERE TO GO FOR MY HOLI.DA.YS ? This is a question which is now occupying the atten- tion of hundreds of our readers. Now the various health resorts of the British Islands, as well as the Con- tinent, will be shortly flocked with visitors. Second only in importance to Where to go to ? If, indeed, second to that is its natural corollary : How shall I de- rive the most benefit from my holiday ? Now, any exp rienced medical man will tell you that a course of some suitable tonic medicine taken during the change will double the beneficial effects of the holi- day. The appetite for food will be sharpened, the as- similating powers of the stomach will be increased, the nervous system will be strengthened, the blood will be purified, and the indispensable "tone " regained by the system, much more certainly and effectually by such a course of medicine taken during the holiday alone or by the medicine alone. Each helps the other, and the ex- perienced in these matters always take both together. Great care, however, mast be exercised in the choice of your tonic. Many so-called remedies are absolutely injurious. There, is, however, one which long and uni- versal experience proves to be absolutely trustworthy, that can do no harm to the most delicate constitution, and has practically in every instance where it has been tried, even proved to be most beneficial, and, indeed, the, most suitable tonic medicine for all such cases. This is the well-known preparation known as G wilym Evans' Quinine Bitters. THE VEGETABLE TONIC. WHAT ARE ITS EFFECTS? 1. It assists and promotes Digestion. 2. It strengthens the Nerves and Muscles. 3. It Cleanses and Purifies the Blood. 4. It Enlivens the Spirits. 5. It removes all Obstructions and Impurities from the human body. 8. It gives Tone to the whole system. Sold in Bottles at Is. lid., 2s. 9d., and 4s. 6d. each. Beware of Imitations! -See the name of " Gwilym Evans "on Label, Stamp, and Bottle. Sole Proprietors : QUININE BITTERS MANUFACTURING CO., LIMITED. LLANELLY, SOUTH WALES.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <p>MISCILLANEOII9.—James Frangletoa, 6, Gandy' Y Terrace, was fined ss. including costs for keeping a dog without a license on the 7th July.—Patrick McCormick, 37, Orford-street.was dismissed with a caution for being drunk whilst in charge of a horse and cab en the 17th July.—A similar method of procedure was adopted with reference to Peter Dutton (13), 35, Beswick-street, charged with throwing stones in Collin-street to the d In- ger of passengers on Sunday night.—James Smith, 11, General-street, was mulcted in the sum of 13. for havitrz his chimney on fire on the 14th July.—Samuel Allen, 3, Lythgoes-lane, was fined 2s. 6i. for not having proper control over the horse and cart he was in charge of on the 10th July. Tris MEANING or TTIE WORD "Fuemus."—Mary Ann Hignett, wife of Charles, Antrobus, was charged with furiously driving a horse and trap in Market-street, to the danger of passengers on the 6th July.—Mr. Bailey, stall-holder in the market, stated that the saw the defendant on the date named driving in the Market-place rather rapidly considering the cr.,wd. —Another witness deposed that defendant was not driving at a furious rate but he saw her knock a man down.—A workman deposed that he was the man , knocked down. He was standing at the corner when , the wheel ef the trap caught him.—The magistrates then discussed at length the question of the word "furiously" in the charge. The defendant had not been driving furiously and therefore did not coma under that head.— The Chief Constable explained that it was the only word that could be used according to the law.—The Bench then corsidering that.it was defendant's first offence dis- t' missed the case.</p>
        <p>T-13;;;AiTlieio.re Dr. Starkey T. Smith (chairman), and Mr. S. Wright. A SCENE IN LOYELY-LANE.—Albert Fownall, a middle- aged and apparently respectable man, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Lovely-lane at 1 o'clock on Tuesday morning.—From the evidence adduced i appeared that defendant had been living apart from his wife for two years but on the night in question had re- turned and being refused admission he threatened to smash the door in. All the neighbourhood was aroused, the windows of the neighbouring houses being occupied by the lightly-clad forms of those whose slumber had I been disturbed by the uproar.—When arrested by the police defendant was drunk. His wife and step- daughter were afraid of him. The magistrates imposed a penalty of 53. and costs:or in default seven days' hard labour. TEURSDAY.—Before•Mr. S. Chandley (chairman), and</p>
        <p>Alderman Sutton:</p>
        <p>OccastoaLtr, Lies—Ns—s-.::P—e-rm-is-slo-n- was given to Mr. Massey to sell on the Football field on the first Monday in August, on the occasion of the sports. AN UNGRATEFUL LODGER.—Peter Reynolds was charged with stealing from the dwellisg-house of John Hickey, 8, Heath-lane, on June 17th, c man's jacket and vest, and a pair of lace-up boots, valued 123. The Chief Constable stated that the prisoner was formerly a groom of Mr. Ohmy, circus proprietor, with whom he came from Oldham to Warrington. About June 3rd he made the acquaintance of John Hickey and his family, and went to lodge there. On June 17th, John Hickey came downstairs in the morning, and then saw defen- dant asleep on the sofa. At about eight o'clock Mrs. Hickey came downstairs, and defendant had then gone. Her husband's coat, vest, and boots were also missing. The matter was reported to the police, and on Wednes- day night, this week, Detective Monaghan was passing the theatre in Scotland-road and saw the prisoner com- ing down from the gallery. When he saw Monaghan, the prisoner turned back, but the former went after him and arrested him. He was wearing the clothes at the time of the capture.—Mrs. Hickey said that she had given the prisoner food and lodgings, and cleaned for him during the fortnight that he had been at her house. —Detective Monaghan said that he had made inquiries at Oldham, and found that the prisoner had been a well-known character there.—Mr. Chandler said that the conduct of the prisoner showed that he was most ungratzful, and the bench would order his detention in gaol for one month with bard labour. SLEEPING OUT—Sarah Hankinson was sent to gaol for 14 days for sleeping out iu Monks' rope walk on Wednesday night, without visible means of subsistence. P.C. Meacoek proved the case. Mr. Monks who ap- peared in court, said that he frequently had his looks smashed. Jane McFeat was discharged with a daution, for sleeping ins railway carriage in Arpley siding on Wednesday. P.C.'s Meacock and Forster proved the case, and Detective Barratt said that there were fre- quently complaints of people sleeping in carriages at nights. Sernavay.—Before Messrs. John Burgess and S. Chandley. .</p>
        <p>AN UNSIISTAINDED CHARGE—Sarah Hickey and Eliza Woods were brought up on remand from tbe pre- vious day, charged with having been found in possession of two hats, two aprons, two neck-shawls, two pairs of corsets, two skirts, and one bodice, on the 18'h instant. —The Chief Constable went into an explan ition of the case in much the same way as he did on the !previous; day, and remarked that be had received no additional evidence. He did not therefore, ask for a further re- mand.—The prisoners stated that they had had some money given them by two horse dealers on the day of the horse fair,and they had purchased the articles.—The Chief Constable remarked that such was the case, and the articles were purchased from a respectable draper's shop. —The prisoners were discharged, there being no evidence against them. DESERTION.—Ed ward Jones was charged with being a deserter from from the 4th Dragoon Guards, stationed at Shorncliffe in the month of Septemher.—Prisoner admitted the offence, and was remanded to await an escort.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS</head>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS</p>
        <p>Mom:lLL—Before Mr. H. R.vnolds (chairman), and Mr.</p>
        <p>G. L. Davies.</p>
        <p>A QUARREL AT WESTON.—Mary Jane People, of Wes- tot, was summoned for having on the 18'h July, as- saulted James Holland, and a cross-summons for an as- sault against Holland was issued by People. Mr. Allen appeared on behalf of Holland and Mr. Burton repre- sented People. —Mr. Allen stated that Holland was 70 years nf age, and was unable to work. He lived at Five Houses and attended to a small garden in front of his house. Mrs. People's hens very often strayed into his garden and were a source of annoyance to him. On the date named he threw a stone at one of the hens he saw in his garden. Mrs. People came to him and struck him a severe blow over the left eye causing blood to flow. The man was insensib!e for a short time, but when he recovered he saw Mrs. People carrying away his spade. He went after her and she hit him with the spade. With regard to the cross-summons his client de- nied having assaulted her. Evidence in support of this statement was given by Mr. and Mrs. Holland, the latter stating that Mrs. Peoplf?s son admitted that his mother struck first. —For the defence, Mr. Burton con- tended that Holland was the aggressor, he having struck Mrs. People first when she went to see about the hen. She denied that she ever struck him on the face, but she admitted having got hold of him and pushed him. She took the spade from him so that he could not strike her with it. There was no necessity for him to follow her to her house. He had to he carried off the premises. After evidence by Mrs. Pee ple, and her son, and Mrs. Clark, the Chairman said the evidence was so contra- dictory that it was impossible for them to see who began the quarrel. They would be both bound over in the sum of £5 to keep the pearl, for six months. Each side wonld have to nay their own costs. • PROFANlTY.—Elizabeth Stokes, a boatwoman, Chol- mnndeley-place, was summoned for having on the 7th July at Runcorn, used obscene and profane language in Stone-street. —P.C. Wylie and Insnector Hicke gave evi- dence in support of the charge.—Eli Knowles, 10, Stone- street and Elizabeth Twigg denied that Any had language was used by the defendant—A fine of 53. and costs was imposed. DRUNKENNESS.—Abraham Kilgrass and John Kilgrass were both charged with being drunk and disorderly in Heath-road. Runcorn. on the 13th July.—P.C. Morris gave evidence in support of the charge. The case against the firstmameo defendant was dism:acw-1. but John Kilgrass was fled sa, and costs.—Annie Philbin, Shaw-street. was charged with being drunk and dis- orderly in Shaw-street the previous Saturday.--Inspector Hicks who proved the case deposed that the prisoner I was rinsing a great disturbance in Shaw.street. She was dr3ok and was fighting those who came around her. She was shouting about voting for Home Rule for Ire- land and Mr. Brunner. A fine of Si. and 74. Bd. costs was imposed or in default seven days' imprisonment.— She went to prison. FURIOUS DR/VING.—John Duckett, fishmonger, Run- corn, was summoned for furiously driving a horse and cart in Bridge-street on the 18th inst.—P.C. Butler stated that at 3.50 p.m. on the date named he was on duty in Bridge-street. The defendant, who was in charge of fish cart went at a reasonable pace when passing wit- ness, but afterwards urged it on until it was going frma between eight or twelve miles an hour. It was a wonder that he did not run over someone.—Defendant denied that he had a whip in his possessinn, and said the animal was unable to go at the speed mentioned.—T. Macdonald a lad, stated that he was riding with the defendant at the time mentioned and the pony was not running fast at all. The defendant had no whip.—A fine of ss. including costs was imposed.</p>
        <p>Everybody wonders how the Pearson Pottery Commny tfanley. Can sell 100 pieces of crockery for one guinea '2l 11) Get a post card at once, and send for li,t. A beauti rut dinner service, a china tea serviee, ince. t wins, disheq !co: &amp;C. Everytody pleasurably surprised. All for one guinea. If you wish to save money, write tothe PF.ARSON Forma Co., Hanley, for particulars.—Advgl</p>
