      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT</p>
        <p>COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of this Council was held on Wed- nesday when the chair was occupied by the Rev. G. F. Roberts, and there were also present, Messrs. W. Hough- ton, J. Warburton, J. Houghton, T. Halton, T. Mather, J. W. Knowles, S. Sutton (clerk), F. Kerfoot (nuisance inspector), and Dr. Sephton (medical officer.) BALANCE IN BAND. The CLERK reported that the balance in hand was £l7 3s. 5d., upon the Surtonwood Sanitary account, £1,700 153. ; and upon the Riaton Cemetery Loan account, £BO 7s. Bd.</p>
        <p>MEDICAL OFFICER'S REPORT. Dr. SEPHTON reported that the births during the past month had been 24, deaths 12. the death rate being 13 1 per I,ooo,against 14,the average of the last three months and 10.3, the corresponding month last year. The death rate from zymotic disease was nil, against 9, the aver- age of the last three months, and 8 the corresponding month pf last year. There had been one case of scarlet fader reported during the month. There had been an epidemic of measles in Croft since the last meeting of the Board, and about 50 children had been attacked, but no case had terminated fatally. The school had been closed and the epidemic was now declining.—The report was adopted on the motion af Mr. William Houghton. SURVEYOR'S REPORT. Mr. OWEN reported that since the last meeting, the following inspections had been made :—Mr.Warburton's house, Great Sankey ; Mr. Dumbill's houses, and Mr. H. Hughe's houses, Penketh. The following plans were recommended to be approved:—TheMa.rchioness of Headfort, farmhouse for Mr. Hudson at Rixton, Bridge Inn. Burtonwood, alterations ; Mr. Albert Payne, six houses, Liverpool-road, Little Sankey ; Mr. H. Ezard, two hOHSeS, Padgate. The contractor was proceeding satisfactorily with the work, and he had issued a certi- ficate for a further payment of £l5O, making a total of £950. The notice as to the final completions of the streets at Liverpool•road, Little Sankey, were posted on the 9th July and after allowing a month for objections the streets would be handed over to the Highway Board as highways, repairable by the inhabitants at large. Several of the owners of property had not yet paid their respective accounts due for them, amounting to £lB5 gs. Bd.—The report was adopted unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>A. MIDNIGHT RUN ROUND CHESHIRE.</head>
        <p>A. MIDNIGHT RUN ROUND CHESHIRE.</p>
        <p>Two ardent Warrington politicians, desiring first hand information as to the result of the Altrincham election, cycled thither on Monday evening. Ar- riving there, they sallied forth to the scene of action, ani were considerably entertained and amused by the exciting scene outside the Working- men's Couser vative Club. A crowd of roughs were trying to force their way into the rooms, and broken windows and broken heads were the order of the day, or to be more correct—the night. A timely charge by the gallant police routed the bel- ligerents, and the sound of wooden truncheons on wooden heads made merry music on the midnight air. The poll was declared at 11.15, and our friends forced themselves into the Conservative Club.which was a scene of the wildest excitement. Women screamed and strong men howled. On the appear- ance of the successful candidate the uproar was deafening. One of our friends threw a lighted cracker through the window which considerably added to the din below. After the Commotion had somewhat subsided, the two A.P.'s remounted their . iron steeds and turned their faces homeward. A r slight sprinkling of rain had freshened the roads and brought out the fragrance of the woods—En- joying a pleasant chat, while bowling along the road, they did not notice the distance, and after going about 8 or 9 miles, without passing through ) Lymm, it gradually dawned upon them that they ) mast have missed the way.A light in a wayside inn, attracted their attention and a knock at the door elicited the information from the sleepy landlord ) that it was 1 o'clock, and time for all respectable people to be in bed. He also informed the belated I cyclists that they were at "The Smoker" at Plumb- lay, a few miles from Northwich, and directed ; them to retrace their steps three or four miles " to I Mere Corner, where the four cross roads meet and then take the turn to the left," and" Good Night." ; They did as directed and after spinning along for some Gime, came to a junction of roads, and remem- bering the advice of Boniface, turned to the left. Their luck, however, did not seem to be in the ) ascepdant, for after riding a few miles, the road narrowing meanwhile almost to a path, they met a waggoner, who told them they were in Budworth and had again missed their way. There was nothing for it but to turn back, and after showering a few blessings in the direction of the landlord at Plumbley for his somewhat ambiguous advice, they eventually arrived at Mere corner and found themselves on the familiar linutsford-road from whence all was" plain sailing." Notwithstanding the various mistakes of route, the ride was a most enjoyable one. The still cool- ness of the night air, and the exquisite scents from every flower garden made the ride a perfect luxury, and our friends did not at all regret their blunders as they rode through the quiet streets of Warring- ton, in the dusk that heralds the break of day at about half-past two o'clock, and they are promis- ing themselves a similar treat in the near future.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS The monthly meeting of this Board was held at the Workhouse on Wednesday morning, when the chair was occupied by Mr. William Houghton. There were also present, Mr. Jenner Fust (Local Government Board In- spector), Mrs. Clay, Revs. G. F. Roberts, J. H. Riddette, and T. R. Matthews, Messrs. A. J. Jolley, J. Biltinge, J. Clarke, A. Lang, T. B. Malkin, J. Warburton, J. W. Knowles, T. Halton, T. Mather, G. Pilling, S. Davies, A. Jolley, Dr. Patterson, W. Hickman; J. C. Sutton (clerk), S. Sutton (deputy clerk), C. H. Ward (master), and H. M. Mines (superintendent). HOUSE COMMITTEE.—MR. MOSELEY'S VISIT. At a meeting of the above committee the following report was read from Mr. J. R. Moseley, poor law in- spector of schools :—"July 6. The children did as well as could be expected for their tender years, and the short time many bad been in the workhouse. They answered the questions, on the whole, readily." ' The minutes were confirmed, on the motion of Mr. CLARKE, seconded by Mr. A. J. JOLLEY. SCHOOLS COMMITTEE.—REPORT OF THE LADIES' COMMITTEE. At a meeting of the above committee, held on the 9th July, the committee had before them the two selected candidates and their wives for the poste of industrial trainer and mother of home, and it was decided to recommend John Todd and his wife Mary for the appointment. The report of the Ladies' Committee was read to the Board by the Superintendent of the schools, sad referred principally to the provision of clothes, and it expressed the opiaion that the necessary work could be accomplished without additional aid being called in. The report was adopted with the exception of one recom- mendation in connection with a machine for making stockings.—Mr. Davies in moving the adoption of the minutes of the Schools Committee, after paying a com- pliment to the Ladies' Committee for this work, said that the Building and Repairs Committee had been summoned to Padgate on Tuesday, the previous week in order to prepare the report to be presented at the meeting of the Schools Committee the previous day. To their astonishment the members of the Build- ing and Repairs Committee did not attend, and no re- port was received from them. The Schools Committee were disappointed, and a little annoyed at the apparent slight cast upon them. He had some difficulty in pre- venting a vote of censure • being passed upon the com- mittee. By their neglect several things would have to be held over for a month for the confirmation of the Board, which he thought rather unfortunate. He thought the time had come when some of the corn- mittees might be dispensed with. The committees at Padgate schools and the wOrkhouse had always been able to superintend and make any alterations they thought necesFary. The Building; and Repairs Com- mittee were of very little use, and the time had come when it should be considered whether it was desirable to abolish it.—Mr. KNOWLES seconded the adoption of the minutes.—Mr. HALTON said that he, as a member of the Building and Repairs Committee was present at the meeting referred to, at great inconvenience to himself. He thought that too much had been said with reference to that committee—too much chaff and childish things— and he was wishful to withdraw his name From it. He thought that if there was a Building and Repairs Corn- mittee, they ought not to be treated like children, but should be supported in their work which was not very pleasant.—Mr. HICKMAN said he had stayed away from the meeting on principle. The committee did not care about votes of censure. He then proceeded to show how the committee were treated at a previous meet- ing of the Padgate committee, to which they had been summoned by Mr. Mines by letter. He contended that such treatment was most disrespectful, and quite uncalled for.—The minutes of the School Committee 11 were then passed unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>POSITION OF -LITTLE SANKEY.</head>
        <p>THE POSITION OF -LITTLE SANKEY.</p>
        <p>Mr. MALKIN said that he considered it unfair that Little Sankey should be deprived of a representative on the Board, and he moved that the Clerk be instructed to communicate with the Local Government Board with a view to the retention of Mr. Thompson as guardian for Little Sankey, now proposed to be merged in the St. Austin's ward.—Mr. Flicwmat4 seconded.—The Crams said that the matter was more for the Corporation 01 Warrington to deal with than for the Board of Guar- dians. Thr Board could however make this recommen- dation.—Mr. KNOWLES moved that the Clerk do not write to the Local Government Board. He said that it would he far better to take things as they hal them. He thought that Warrington was sufficiently ably repre- sented at present —Mr. J. WARBURTON seconded, but the Chairman declared that he was too late. Mr. Mal- kin's motion was then put to the meeting but was lost by nine votes to eight. —The Cryptic advised Mr. Malkin to endeavour to influence the Warrington Corporation to move in the matter. If he could do that, he would pro- bably carry out his purpose.—Mr. Malkin said that it was very unfair that Warrington should have its repre- sentation reduced.—The subject then dropped. MR. lENNER FUST'S OBSERVATIONS. Mr. JENNER Fcrsr then addressed the Board. He said that he called their attention at his last visit to the need of further assistance to the matron, to the necessity for increase in the nursing staff, and to the need of better accommodation for the sick. He was glad to see that the question of assistance to the matron had been dealt with. The question of the nursing staff still stood over, and with regard to the accommodation for the sick, he saw that they had passed a resolution in favour of hav- ing anew infirmary, and he hoped that that would be put into action as speedily as passible. Mr. rust then proceeded to criticise various departments. With regard to paupers, he said that he noticed that in Warrington, there was one pauper to 50 of the population, while the average of the last five years was one in 59. He then proceeded to make observations upon the pauper ques- tion in general.—The CHAIRMAN promised to give Mr. Fast's recommendations close consideration and thanked him for his presence there that morning. The Board then rose. CROFT CHURCH CHOIR TRIP.</p>
        <p>The memli-e-r-s-oi"ati;76r;f7-a'u-r-c-t-i- -choir and Sunday school teachers, accompanied by the Rector, the Rev. H. I. Wadeson, Mrs. Wadeson, members Of the congregatien and friends, to the number of about 60, had their annual trip, on Sat- urday the place selected this year being Southport. They left by the 9 a.m. train from Culcheth station, and arrived at South- port at 10.45. They then visited the pier, promen- ade, &amp;c., and otherwise enjoyed themselves until noon, when they partook of dinner. Afterwards some of the visitors paid a visit to the residence of Mr. Reginald Owen (formerly proprietor of the General Elliott Hotel, Croft), whilst others went to the Botanical Gardens. Churchtown, and Birldale Park, returning to Southport for tea. The excur- sionists left Southport by the seven o'clock train, and arrived at Padgate station in good time.</p>
        <p>NO LES ON GAM', S AND</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>CRICKET.</p>
        <p>The match between Warrington and Northern came to a definite issue on Saturday, and in this respect, the game differed from their previous three matches when neither side won. Warrington had a very good team for the occasion, including besides the regular players-- S. M. Crosfield, who was accorded a hearty reception on the first occasion upon which he has appeared he re a Warrington " gate" this season—and also W. G. Druc, the captain of Cambridge University, who has in first- class cricket a batting average of 44. W. Mortimer, of Cambridge University, was also on the team.</p>
        <p>With such a team as this, there would no doubt have been a fine attendance, but the state of the weather kept a large number away. The match itself resulted in a very easy win for Warrington, and but for the last wicket of the Northerners, it would have been still more decisive. The last wicket put on almost as many rues as all the rest put together. J. J. and S. M. Crosfield were in fiae form with the ball, the former especially, and it w s, therefore,not deemed necessary to separate them during the Northern innings.</p>
        <p>The Warrington batsmen did not commence very well for Mortimer, Smith, and Piomer were all out for 30 J. J. Crosfield and W. G. Druce however began to lay about them to some tune, and before they were separ- ated, Warrington had won. Druce's innings were very enjoyable, and it was very tantalising that as soon as he appeared to be well set, and scoring at a rapid pace that he should be caught just on the boundary, J. J. Cros- field was also not a stone wailer by any means, and it is rather singular that he and Druce who made the longest stand of the match, should both make the same total- -26. The only other stand anything of mention was be- tween Bates and G. Clarke,but as the Northerners were beaten, it was of very little consequence. The fielding of Warrington was very clean, and smart, and they fully deserved their win.</p>
        <p>In the averages this week it will be noticed that R- Smith has kept the third position, but with a slightly I reduced average. It is a little curious how near the two pros. Clark and Bates are, there being only a decimal point between them. J. J. Crosfield and R. Smith have both completed their 200 runs, and George Clarke has also passed his first 100. Callon ought to be the nsxt, and only for a bit of bad judgment at the match on S it- urday, he might have accomplished the feat then. There are now five who have passed the hundred, which considering that more than half the season is passed, is not a very large number. The batting powers of the team evidently require a little improvement, and it is satisfactory to note that this department has improved slightly of late.</p>
        <p>The following is the list of the averages for the present week:— times</p>
        <p>Innings not out runs average. H Hind J I Crostleld R. Smith Lieu. Plamer W Mortimer Clark (A) Estes /3 England W H Podmore . 8 I Callon G Clarke W Wordsworth . 6 E t. onnor</p>
        <p>The second team avenged a defeat which they had sushined earlier in the season by defeating Northern 2nd at Waterloo on Saturday. They did this quite un- expectedly, for they were minus four regular players, and had to pick up two substitutes in Liverpool. The Northern had an exceptionally strong team,but notwith• standing this fact, they were beaten by 73 to 57.</p>
        <p>Buckley-t. made short work of Whitecross, for where as the former scored 63, the latter could only make 10 betwee'n them, and were thus ignominiously beaten.</p>
        <p>The Y.M.C.A. team could not play because of the rain on Saturday, but the second team took down Grappen- hall, not one of the latter getting into double figures, while Sanders and White for the Y.M.C.A.,just equalled the Grappenhall score.</p>
        <p>A large number journeyed to Manchester during the first three days of the present week to witness the match between Lancashire and Gloucestershire at Old Trafford, for there was then an opportunity of seeing the two champion cricketers, W. G. Grace and A. C. MacLaren. Both these players received great ovations on making their appearance on the ground, and it is to be hoped that MacLaren will be able to give more of his time to Lancashire cricket than be has done this year. By the way, it is said that although the last month, he has been playing cricket at the Harrow school, he has never been out yet.</p>
        <p>There is a great difference in size of the rival captriins of Lancashire and Gloucestershire, and also a great difference in their play. Grace is no doubt a very fine cricketer, but it is pleasanter to watch MacLaren. The latter has such a fine style, that one always feel sorry when he has to retire to the pavilion.</p>
        <p>The Gloucester team have two excellent bowlers in Jessop and Townsend, especially the former, and these two rivalled Briggs and Mold. It is a treat to watch the erstwhile Australian bowler, Ferris, bowl, and the manner in which he acted almost sent the crowd into hysterics from merriment. The modus operandi is as follows. Re runs some yards towards the wickets, and just before sending the ball, he lifts up his foot as high as he can lift it, he then throws both handsl up. puts his head on one side, and finally sends the MI, with his left hand. He used to be a terror to the batsrum. but he is not se now. They appear to be growing accus tomed to his antics.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>ME ACCIDENT TO THE "STANLEY'</head>
        <p>ME ACCIDENT TO THE "STANLEY'</p>
        <p>FORCE."</p>
        <p>ADJOURNED INQUEST.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday the county coroner (Mr. Samuel Brig- house) continued the adjourned investigation into the death of Edward Corlett, aged thirty-eight, one of the victims of the collision in the Mersey between the steamers Beta and the Stanley Force which left Weston Point on the 4th inst. Mr. Collingwood Hope appeared for the owners of the Beta, andralr. Dickinson (Hill, Dickinson, and Co.) represented the owners of the Stanley Force. William •Jones was the first witness called, and, ex- amined by Mr. Hope, stated that he was the master of, the steam dredger Beta. On Thursday, the 4th inst. they left Runcorn and went down the Ship Canal. They dropped their dredging outside the river,and after doing that they turned up the river again. They were then carryint two side lights, a masthead light, and a stern light. About ten minutes past twelve midnight they were abreast of Bromborough Pool, and were proceeding about eight knots. The tide was about half ebb, and was running from about two and a half to three knots. Witness was on the bridge, the mate was forward on the lock-out, and a man named Howard was at the wheel. At this time they saw a red light coming down the channel, and when near the buoy the approaching vessel starboarded its helm, and gave witeess his green light. Immediately afterwards witness saw hi red light come up again about 250 yards off. The stranger blew his whistle once, and witness answered with two blasts, and after that stopped the engines and ordered them full speed astern. A minute or two later the col- lision occurred, and they struck the other vesel just be- fore the beam on the port side. The result was that the other vessel sank in five minutes. Witness there- upon stopped the starboard engines, and went full speed ahead, and got a boat out. They stopped about for -three-quarters of an hour, and nicked up five of the other vessel's crew, which turned out to be the Stan- ley Force. Cross-examined by Mr. Dickinson : When witness saw the Stanley Force's red light it was hl duty to keep out of the latter's way. Witness however, kept on his course. He was sure that the Stanley Force showed her green light. The only reason he could advance f 3r her doing this was he thought that she was going todrop her pilot at New Ferry. He afterwards spoke to the Stauley Force's pilot, and asked him what he star-beaded for,hut he made no reply. The Stanley Force blew her whistle once, and witness replied with two blasts. He was sure it was two—not one long blast. After he whistled he ordered the engines to be reversed, but did not alter his helm, which was steady. If he had ported he would have run down a shooner, which was lying some abort distance off, and this was the reason he did not port. He blamed the Stanley Force for star-boarding. If she had kept on her course or reversed her engines at the same time as witness reversed his all would have been well. Richard !damn, marine pilot, examined by Mr. Dickin- son, said he joined the Stanley Force at Weston Point, about ten o'clock at night on the 4th inst., and pro- ceeded with the vessel down the Ship Canal, arriving at Eastham Locks about ten minutes past midnight. It was a dark night. When they first saw the hopper she was about a mile off, and was bearing on the port bow about a point and a half. Witness was heading about north. They did not alter their course until they left the gas buoys, when they changed to port. The next order was " Steady." They were then heading for the Dingle Point, to get to the right side of the channel to clear the schooner, and get on to the east side. After they had ported, they gave one blast of the whistle. The other vessel then ported about 511 points. About three minutes later the Beta put her holm bard-a-port. She was then about fifty-five yards away. The collision followed, and the Stanley Force was struck on the port side. It was untrue that thoy etarbnarded before they got to:the gas buoy,nor coul I t be captain of the Beta possibly have seen their eon</p>
        <p>gross-examined by Mr. Hope : They only got one long b 144 froin the hopper, not two short oaes as repre- sented by the captain. The Coroner p Anted out to the jury that the evidence of the two witnesses was quite sufficient to show that deceased c4me by his death accidently. There was no suggestion of criminal negligence, and they night leave the learned gentlemen concerned with the case to thrash the evidence out more thoroughly in another court. The jury agreed with the coroner, and returned a ver- dlct of "Accidental death."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
