      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>?Learns to the Obitor.</head>
        <p>?Learns to the Obitor.</p>
        <p>BALLOT AND BARTER.</p>
        <p>Sir,—NOW that the election is over will you kindly insert two letters received from our respected member and the Liberal candidate orr the sullect of absolute freedom of action by electors at election time. Your readers, I am sure, will be interested in this great question, and vast nunabers would welcome some measure to present organized bands hunting up and driving men to the polls like so many sheep. The in- evitable results often end in falsehood and the encour- agement of deceit. The futility of canvassing is shown by results of canvassing books when totalled up on the eve of an election, giving such glowing accounts of fictitious promises, calculated to carry any candidate pell-naell into St. Stephens. The only consolation to the defeated one is that he has been utterly deceived by false friends. Thousands of canvassers could testify to answers such as—" I have got the ballot, and shall not tell you who I shall vote for" ; or, "Yes ; put my name down, I'm all right for Mr. So and So." To the opposite party :—" Yes ; put it down. Your man shall have my vote. " Result : cabs, canvassers, candidates all after such men, who, being disgusted, don't vote at all, or dodge clown some back street. Men who value their freedom, and franchise have ample opportunity to make up their minds, by the profuse display of addresses, speeches, placards, leaflets, Sm., Szc. Listen to the opinions of two gentlemen, who com- mands the respect of every elector in the borough. St. Austins, Warrington, July 11, 1895. Dear sir,— I have to acknowledge your letter at the meeting at St. Luke's schoolroom this evening. I took the opportunity of replying to it as to your question about canvassing. I said that if anyone wcrild propound to the House of Commons a scheme, which would absolutely forbid can, warming, be would have my sincere sympathy. At the same time,l have too great faith in Englishmen,whether workingfmen or not, to believe that canvessing pre- vents them exercising their free choice.—l am, yours faithfully,RormaT PIERPOINT. To Mr John Moorfield." Mr. Percival B. Scott's Committee Room,Liberal Club, July 8,1895. Dear sir,—Mr. Scott desires. me to say, that he is in favour of preserving the absolute secrecy of the ballot, in the interests of working men and all other electors. The other matters mentioned in your letter will, if time permit, be carefully considered.—Faithfully, H. ROBERTS." Hoping that others will take up this matter.—l am yours faithfully,</p>
        <p>JOHN MOORFIELD. 44, Parker-street, V4arrington, July 19, 1895.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>'WARRINGTON CLERGY DAUGHTERS'</head>
        <p>'WARRINGTON CLERGY DAUGHTERS'</p>
        <p>SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>"BREAKING UP" CONCERT.</p>
        <p>-------- - On Thursday morning the annual "breaking up" concert of the St. Elphin's Clergy Daughters' School, Warrington, was given at the school in the presence of the Right Rev. the Local Bishop of Chester (Bishop Jayne), and the Revs. Canon Willis, Canon Penrhyn, C. Sullivan, El. Stapleton Cotton, Harry, and Miss M. J. Beal (principal of the school). An admirable concert programme had been arranged and the skill of each and every performer was marked while the audience testified their appreciation fre- quently. The following is a list of the items submitted and the respective youne, ',dies who were responsible for their production :—Double piano trio : nrst 0.113, the Misses D. Clark,E. Cox,and M. Rawson; second piano : Misses K. Coghlan, E. Jones,and K.Phillips ; piano solo, " Stindchen " (Hiller), Miss E. Cox; duet, two pianos, " Sonatine " (Florida), the Misses B. Cotton and C. Hedley ; violin and piano duet, " Concertante " (Siegfried Jacoby), the Misses Harris and E. Reynolds; piano solo," Romance" and "The Mill" (Schumann and Jensen), Miss H. Giles ; quartette, piano (Mosz- kowski), first piano, Misses E. Cox and B. Cotton,second piano, Misses H. Giles and W. Harry. This completed the first part of the prcgramme and during the interval the prizes were presented. The second half of the programme commenced with a charmingly varied cantata entitled "The Little Mermaid," and which the the chorus of scholars rendered with effect. The solo parts taken by the Misses Newerharn, Gordon, Taylor, Giles, and Clarke were also commendable. The remain- ing items were :—Duet (two pianos), "Rondo" (Gurlitt), piano 1., Miss L. Willington, piano 11., Miss Ridgway ; violin solo, " Tarantella" (Papini), I. Harris ; piano solo " Tarantelle " (S. Clark). G. Jones ; piano duet, "Bolero" (Gurlitt), Misses M. Cotton and M. Lane. The prizes were distributed by the Bishop, each lady reci- pient being greeted with applause. The follow- ing is a list of those whose prowess in the different branches had gained them a prize : Foam PEUZES.—Form 6, Mary Sullivan ; Form 5, Zoe D'Ombrain ; Form 4. Beatrice Cotton ; Form 3, Laura Willington ; Form 2. Gwendoline Phillips and Gladys Clark ; Form 1, Muriel Cotton. SCRIPTURE PRIZES.—Form 6, Winifred Harry • Form aura 5, Ethel Cox ; Form 4, Bertha Healey ; Form 3 L Willington ; Form 2, Gladys Clark ; Form 1. ay Jones. MATHEMATICS—Constance Phillips. LANGUAGES Senior, Mary Sullivan ; languages junior, Beatrice Cotton. ENGLISH.—Zke D'Ombrain. Music Senior, Ethel Cox ; Music junior, Beatrice Cotton (both Hamilton memorial prizes). DRAWING Senicr, Isabel Harris drawing, junior,, Eva Knowles. .</p>
        <p>CONDUCT PRIZES.—Good Example, Mary Sullivan, given by the Rev. T. W. Cook (vice-principal of Chester College) ; perfect, Mary Sullivan • good conduct, Laura Willington ; needlework senior, Constance Cox; junior needlework, Evangeline Fish; garden, Janie Johnstone and Dorothy Clark. GOOD CONDUCT CERTIFICATES.—Form 6, Isabel Harris; form 5, Effie Jones and Mabel Norris; form 4 (upper), Ethel Salkeld ; form 4 (lower), AmyNalkeld ; form 3, Constance Cox ; form 2, Gladys Clark ; form 1, Mary JOIMNS. • Rover, DRAWING SOCIETY.-23 passes and 8 honours. Also two full certificates (the first gained), Emily Mary Sullivan and Margaret Isabel Harris. The Rev. CANON WILLIS, after the presentation of the prizes to the above students, read a letter of apology far non-attendance from the Examiner of the School, (Mr. J. Worsley Cunliffe), in which he stated that his former good impression of the school is fully con- firmed and he trusts they will enjoy the well- earned rest. The writer also added that be would send his report in about two days. The rev, gentleman him- self said that the Doctor would bear him out that the physicial condition of the school was very satisfactory, and he (the speaker) could vouch for it morally and spiritually. The Right Rev. Bishop of Chester. who was applauded on rising, said with referen-e to the question of remov- ing the schools it was a burning question, but so far as he could remember the sehool here had been on the , whole remarkable for the health of its inmates. They would lose by leaving, many pleasant and more than pleasant associations, which was a thing to be regarded when *hey had to break with the past. Be was sure it would also be no small loss to move from under Canon Willis's fostering care. (Annlanse.) They all knew how devoted his attention was to the deepest and best wel- fare of the school and how well he was supported by others. Canon Penrhyn was also a constant and ancient friend of theirs. Within the school everything was done to promote the best interests of those in education there and he had had many testimonials from various quarter- ters which (rave him eood reason to know its teaching value. He notice with pleasure that the diocese of Cheshire was well represented in the prize list, but he trusted that other dioceses would not be discouraged by finding how prominent they were. The speaker con- eluded by wishing health and God-speed to the school wherever established alai-, a bright and happy career to these who were being educated there.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>PENKETFI.</head>
        <p>PENKETFI.</p>
        <p>MERSEY GOLF Crzn.—The tie for the silver run pre- sented by the Flonnrary President (Uon. T. W Loh, Wag phveri off the other day, Mr. G. A. beating Mr. F. W. Gilbert five holes up and three to play.</p>
        <p>CALT. or Write for' petr of To TAYLOR'S 6s. 114. or Bs. 6o TADIRS' or if e. +l.'s SEWN' Eh-SOT.; Or SHOEA, past free.-53 Manehester.street,Liverpool.—AdJt.</p>
        <p>SEQUEL TO A TRAP ACCIDENT.</p>
        <p>ACTION AGAINST A FRODSHAM PUBLICAN.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn Ommty Court on Tuesday, before His Honour Judge Wynne Ffoulkes, an action was heard in which John Henry Davies, grocer, Five Crosses, Froth sham was the plaintiff, and William Youd, landlord of the Bear's Paw Hotel, Frodsham, defendant. The claim was for 16s. for damage toe trap. Mr. Grier, barrister, instructed by Mr. Burton appeared for the plaintiff; and Mr. Pugh, barrister, Liverpool, instructed by Mr. Linaker, represented the defendant. Alt. Youid made a counter claim for £1 23. 6d. damage done to his harness, Sm. Mr. Grier said the claim was for a small sum but the parties considered the matter of some imminence as the present action might settle ether questions. On Wednesday, May 811, plaintiff was driving about 9.45 p.m. towards Frodsham. When getting over Frodsham Bridge he saw the defendant's waggonette, driven by his man coming in the opposite direction. Plaintiff was accompanied by his sister, who was sit- ting beside him. The approaching vehicle was com- ing at a fair pace but it was somewhat nearer the wrong side than it should have been. The bridge was one that it was desirable for vehicles to keep well to their own sides. Seeing how defendant's man was driv- ing Mr. Davies called out to him but he seemed to pay no attention and a collision happened. As a coasequence of the contact Mrs. Davies was thrown on the pavement and was subsequently found lying on the proper side of the roadway. Plaintiff after having given his horse in charge of another man returned to the place, and after seeing to his sister went and inspected the spot and saw that the waggonette was well over on the wrong side of the road. Another vehicle coming from Frodsham it was found could not pass on its right side and the driver's attention being drawn to this fact it was placed more on one side- He suggested that the cause of the accident was defendant's man driving on his wrong side; that he was recklessly driving, and inattentive to what wag</p>
        <p>going on. Plaintiff gave evidence at considerable length in proof of the above statenaent. The force of the collision was sufficient to make an indentation into the nave of the right wheel to the extent of 4 inches square. His sister fell out with her baby on the left side of the road—on the pavement. Mrs. Davies was seriously injured and was taken into the cafe close by, and subsequently was removed home in the waggonette. Be had paid 163. for the repairs to his trap.—By Mr. Pugh witness said the night was fairly light. There were no lamps attached either to his or defendant's vehicle. They did not re- quire them. Be thought the waggonette would be coming at the rate of from 10 to 12 miles an hour. He (Mr. Davies) was not travelling at more than 5 miles an hour. fie was certain defendant's man was on the wrong side of the road. When they were 50 yards apart he anticipated danger and when 10 yards apart he saw a collision was inevitable. He had written a letter to Mr. Youd dated the day after the collision in which he said he was unfortunate enough to meet his waggonette in charge of an intoxicated driver. His trap was con- siderably damaged. He (plaintiff) was on his own side close to the curb-stone. His sister had broken her collar bone. The driver of the waggonette was thrown off his seat by the collision.—By Mr. Grier : Only a portion of the letter written had been read for it went on to speak of an accident about six weeks previously at Kingsley. In his opinion the driver was in the condi- tion as described in his letter.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Helen Davies, of Five Crosses, gave corrobora- tive evidence. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Joseph Caldwell, labourer, Frodsham Bridge, said he could not swear that the driver of the wazgonette was drunk. He thought he might have been stunned by the fall. Ile was bleeding from his head. It was a dark night. He had no recollection of having made a state- ment to plaintiff's solicitor,that he had noticed any marks upon the kerbstone. He did not notice any. He did not make a statement to Mr. Burton or his clerk that Plevin was intoxicated.</p>
        <p>John Catteral, butcher, Frodsham Bridge,spoke to go- ing to the scene after hearing the smash, when he saw Mrs. Davies lying across the parapet. On the left hand side of the waggonette was Pleyin who had received a severe cut on the head and was bleeding freely. He looked very dazed by the fall.—By Mr. Pugh : The ni4hz was -fairly dark, he did not see any signs of drink about Plevin. He asked him where his cap was and they looked for it but could not find it.</p>
        <p>This concluded the plaintiff's case. Mr. Pugh for the defence said that was a case of the usual character. There were contradictory statements and the witnesses for the plaintiff were in important de- tails opposed to each other. His Honour did not think so. Of caurse there was no positive evidence as to drunkenness. He thought there ' was a prima facia case which required answering. Mr. Pugh then intimiated that he could call the driver who would say that he was on his right side that he fell off on the left hand side of the road, that he was on the parapet, that he lost his cap, and that after looking for it they came to the conclusion it had gone over the wall. Where was the negligence ? The plaintiff had said that , when he saw danger of the accident he pulled to the near side to try as far as he could to avoid it, but his sister said they came in the same position all the length of the bridge. Proceeding be showed that on the part of the plaintiff it was tried to make out that the driver of the waggonette was intoxicated and on that account the accident happened. That was a serious allegation to make, and the object in making it was obvious. But the last witness produced stated that he saw no signs of drink upon him. Mr. Grier said he did not wish to make a point of the man being drunk. Mr. Pugh said the charge had been made in the letter quoted and therefore he thought if the statement was launched haphazard and without consideration. It went to show that the plaintiff would colour his evidence to support his claim. The cause of the collision was in- explicable unless the man was drunk or closed, his eyes as to where he was going. His Honour said that so far as the plaintiff's evidence went there was nothing to show that Plevin was intoxi- cated.</p>
        <p>Mr. Grier said it was extremely probable that the man might have had more drink on such a special occasion than he had en ordinary days. Us would not, however, suggest that he was quite drunk. Thomas Plevin, in the employ of Mr. Youd, said he had been with him for eight years. He was driving Mr.and Mrs. Taylor to Sutton Bridge. He was going at a quiet trot when the accident hHppenecl. He was on his right side, and when he was thrown off the box he landed on the parapet.—By Mr. Grier : He had not been in more than one accident previously and on that occasion his master did not pay compensation. Evidence was also given by Walter Taylor, farm bailiff, Frodsham Bridge, William Shore, grocer, Prod- sham Bridge, and George Herbert Yonge, of 19, Wilson Patten-street, Warrington, on behalf of defendant's case.</p>
        <p>- William Youd also spoke with reference to the counter claim and said that a saddler had stated that the cost to repair the damage done would be £1 23. 81. Hi g Honour in summing up said there appeared to be a certain amount of mystery as to the cause of the colli- sion, but to his mind it was perfectly clear that Mrs: Davies fell on the parapet on the left hand sido,and that therefore her brother must be on his correct side. There was also evidence to show that the defendant's man was going at a tremendous speed from the damage that resulted. The waggonette must have been almost in the middle of the road to cllide with plaintiff's trap. He must on the evidence ceime to the conclusion that the defendant was responsible, and he therefore gave a verdict in favour of Mr. Davies, both on the claim and counter claim.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>F ROD SH AM.</head>
        <p>F ROD SH AM.</p>
        <p>THE PAROCHIAL COMMITTEE.—At the monthly meeting of the Frodsham Parish Council, acting as the Parochial Committer, held in the Frodsham Court-room, on Friday evening, Mr. H. E. Heywood, presiding, the Inspector reported that in accordance with instructions given at the last meeting he had issued notices calling upon cer- tain persons to provide a proper supply of water for their respective properties, but such had not been done. It was resolved that tenders be obtained for doing the work, and that if need be the Inspector anply to the justices for permission to enter upon the premises for the purpose. The Inspector was anthorized to take two samples of water from the running spout at Frodsham Bridge and submit them to the county analyst.—On the motion of Mr. Williams. seconded by Mr. Barrow, it was resolved that the generous offer of Mr. Heywood to paint the fountain and reservoir at the bide be accepted.—The Surveyor submitted a plan of Mr. Kydd's proposed jam factory. After consideration the plan was referred to a small committee to ascertain the p)sition of the sewers.—The Clerk reported the receipt of a letter intimating that the County Council were quite willing to provide a water cart for Frodsham on the committee finding the water, this being considered a concession to Frodsham.—Tha committee appointed to wait upon Mr. Thorne with regard to the "shoddy" nuisance reported that they had met that gentleman,and he had made arraagements to abolish this nuisance in the future, by allowing no waggons to remain in the sidings from Saturday to Monday. This was considered highly satisfactory.—The minutes were confirmed at the meeting of the Rural Council on Tuesday. VOLUNTEER CRURCH PARADE.—On '4undav morning the officers and members of the Frodsham I Company attended divine service at the Fradsham Parish Church. when the Rev. E. N. Simm, 8.A., Curate, preached. The officers present on this occasion were Colonel Ashton Captains Harrison, Smith and Thorneley, Lieutenants Burton, Oarratt, and Brundrit, with about 50 rank and file. The Volunteer Band was in attendance.- SUNDAY SCHOOL ANNIVERSARY—TWO sermons were !preached in the Primitive Methodist Chapel on Sunday by the Rev. P. O. Hirst, of Preston. In the afternoon an address was delivered by the Rev, gentleman,- when Mr. Whitley, of Stockton Heath presided. The choir and scholars rendered the hymns and anthems in a manner. which gave satisfaction to all. The offertories for the day amounted to £IB 7s. flid. —.-.-40--</p>
        <p>The rented hnumes of London annually bring their owners £37,400,000.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 27, 1895</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 27, 1895</p>
        <p>were each fined 23. 6. and costs.—James Anderson and John Brennan, of Salford, were charged with attempt- ing to pick pockets on Saturday night on Earlestown Station. Detective-Sergeant Goode, and Detective Kynaston of the Railway Company, gave evidence which went to show that they watched the prisoners for some time on Saturday on Earlestown Station. When the Warrington train came in prisoners got into a com- partment and attempted to pick some lathes' pockets. A few minutes later when the Manchester train came in they got behind a lady, and Brennan covered Anderson, while the latter put his hands into the lady's pocket. The lady caught Anderson's hand as he was taking it out of her pocket. Her husband made complaint and witness apprehended prisoners, while they were taking tickets, and gave them in charge of P.S. Clarke. Both pleaded not guilty, but the bench sentenced them to 14 days -each.—Cruelty to a horse.—Peter Lannigan of Widnes, was charged with being drunk in charge of a horse on Saturday, and also with unlawfully ill-treat- ing the animal.—Defendant was fined 21. 6cl. and costs for the drunkenness, but the magistrates after consulta- tion decided to summon the owner of the horse and re- manded the defendant until Saturday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>EARLESTO WN IN D NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTO WN IN D NEWTON.</p>
        <p>NEWTO. N CONGREGATIONAL CHURCH SCHOOLS.</p>
        <p>On Friday afternoon the annual field—d-a-i—ii.nd treat to the scholars attending the above Sunday School took place. Mustering at the School at 2.30, the children marched in procession, headed by the Reformatory school band, to a field off Ash- ton Lane, belonging to Mr. W. Longton. Here they enjoyed themselves in various ways, until about four o'clock,whon buns and milk were served. The remainder of the afternoon was spent in games and racing for useful prizes. On Saturday the senior scholars and teachers had their annual picnic to Frodsham. The weather was not ot the best but they managed to enjoy themselves on Overton Hills, dinner and tea being partaken of at Mersey View.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>FIRE AT NEWTON.</head>
        <p>FIRE AT NEWTON.</p>
        <p>About three o'clock on Tuesday afternoon, the thatched root of a barn,in Crow-lane,Newton,rented by Mr. John Hatton of 29, Fairclough-st. Earles- town, was discovered to be on fire. An alarm was raised and several of Lord Newton's workmen were quickly on the spot. They got a stand pipe and hose, and attached it to the main in Crow-lane. A good stream of water was played on the flames, which in about an hour were extinguished. The whole of the thatch had to be thrown off, and a lot lof damage was done to the straw in the barn by water. The fire is supposed to have been caused by a spark from the chimney of an adjoining house. Sergeant Clarke, Banks, and P.C. Green (135) rendered valuable assistance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>SHOCKING ACCIDENT AT NEWTON.</head>
        <p>SHOCKING ACCIDENT AT NEWTON.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening, between 11 and 12 o'clock after the declaration of the result of the election at Newton, a number of colliers living at Newton Common, were firing roburite cartridges. One of them named Richard Meyers, a collier employed at Collins Green colliery, and between 40 and 50 years of age was holding one in his hand, with the fuse alight, when it suddenly exploded, and shat- tered his right hand. Dr. Valentine was sent for, and he with Mr. Brown, chemist, hastened to the man's house, when they found the hand was en- tirely useless, and Dr. Valentine amputated it at the wrist. The man is in poor circumstances and hats a family of five children to support. • 1.0.G.T (EsinvEzatt Lon.) This Lodge had their usual weekly session on Tuesday, when they had a visit from the Temper- ance Mission Lodge of Warrington, and entertained them for the night with songs, duets, readings, re- citations, etc., which were given by Sisters Nurse, Isherwood, Hankinson, and Bros. Simister,Roberts, Taylor, Isherwood, Sparks, and others. Bro. J. Taylor afterwards proposed a vote of thanks to the Temperance Mission Lodge which was accepted and replied to on behalf of the Lodge by Bro. Roberts after which the Lodge closed in the usual was, re- freshments being provided for the visitors.—The juveniles of this lodge should have had another field day and treat on Saturday, in a field kindly lent by Mr. T. Sanderson, but the weather being un- favourable, they had the treat at the Co-operative Hall. Various games were enjoyed during the afternoon, buns, milk, &amp;c., being afterwards served out by Bro. A. Morris and Sister Hatton, assisted by Sisters Taylor,Cocmbs and Brown, and Bras. Taylor, H. Brown, Strong, Dean, and W. Brown; and they were dismissed later with nuts and sweets.</p>
        <p>DEGRADING THE QUEEN'S UNIFORM.-A WARNING.</p>
        <p>At the Earlestow-n Police Court on Friday,William James Latham, a coloured man, was charged that he did unlawfully wear Her Majesty's Uniform, not being authorised to do so. Prisoner, on hearing the charge read over, said that he bought the uniform. He was wearing a fatigue jacket, which evidently at one time belonged to the West Yorkshire. regi- ment, and it was plentifully decorated with sham medals. He had during the day been selling tips. —Sergeant Barnes said that at 4 o'clock he saw the prisoner dressed in a soldier's coat, in front of the grand stand at Newton Race Course. He was sur- rounded by a crowd. Ile followed him down the road and asked him by what authority he was wearing the coat. All that he could get from him was that he bought it. Witness told him he had no right to wear Her Majesty's uniform unless he had been in her service. Prisoner said he had been coming to Newton for 15 years with that coat on, and had never been charged before. He bought it in Richmond-row, Liverpool, and did not see how they could charge him. He had been in the RoN al Bermuda Militia, which he joined when he was 12 years old.— Inspector Morgan said he had known the man for 15 or 20 years He was a tipster go- ing about the lact3 CoorseB. and there was no real harm about him. The case was only brought to prevent Her Majesty's uniform being abused.—The Bench said it was a breach of law to wear that uniform. A new regulation came out this year,and they were prepared to deal leniently with the pri- soner, and let him off with a caution, if he was brought up again he would be liable to a fine of £lO.</p>
        <p>PROPOSED NEW SWMCMING BATH FOR EARLEmTOWN.</p>
        <p>Messrs. Fitzgerald Bros.,builders, Earlestown, are, we hear, about to erect a swimming bath on land fronting Back Market-street and close to Stanley- street. The plans have already been prepared by Mr. Percy Montagu Beesley, Bridge-street, Earles- town, and it is proposed to commence building at an early date. The building, which measures 60 feet by 45 feet, consists of a swimming bath 46 feet long by 31 feet wide, dressing rooms, with lava- tory accommo lation and attendant's room. The sides and bottom of the bath are to be lined with white glazed bricks. The building will be heated throughout on an improved principle.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>NEWTON RACES.</head>
        <p>NEWTON RACES.</p>
        <p>Unfortunately this year the annual race meeting at Newton fell upon inclement weather, and as a conse- quence the attendance of the outside public on Friday was not nearly so large as on previous occasions. Satur- day (the cup day) was even worse than Friday, but on that day tbere was an increased attendance. All the works in the district were closed, and although a large number of people journeyed by train to various places, a still larger number remained at home. Fortunately this year the event passed off without any serious mis- hap, the only accident which has come to our notice being to a little girl named Haynes, living in Legh-street, who was knocked down in Market-street on Friday afternoon by the horses attached to a waggonette, and her face was rather badly cut:.</p>
        <p>- There was rather a heavy list of offenders who came under the notice of the police, and they were dealt with at the Esrlestown Police Station, each evening after th- races. On Friday the magistrates were Colonel Wood and Mr. C. B. F. Born.n. Two men named Hunter and Johnson were charged with playing a game of chance with box and numbers on the race course. P.C. Duncan said they sold the numbers to the public at a shilling each, but a confederate always won. For this little pastime they were ordered to pay 20;. and costs or go to gaol for 14 days.— Thomas Ashcroft, of Go!borne, for amusing the gullible poblic with a box and dice, pleaded not guilty, although P,C.'s C .rlton and Foxeroft averred that they caught him in the act. The magistrates considered the case would be met, if he paid 20.. and costs to the County rate.—Walter Pigott, on the information of P.C. Carlton had also to make a similar contribution to the rates for a little diversion with box and numbers. Thomas Wilson for the same offence, had to pay 20s. and costs. Be sold numbers to the public to the extent of 23., the winner received ls. 61. and he got 61. for his trouble.—William Cairns an expert at the three card trick, wanted to gibe the magistrates an exhibition of his cleverness, in mys- tifyiag them, but they declined the offer, and ordered him to pay 203. and costs.—Thomas Walsh, for playing " under and over, had also to pay a similar sum for his release. . _ . . . _ _</p>
        <p>- On Saturday the magistrates were Messrs.Edmondson, Borron and Brown. Thomas Kennedy, who said he came from Manchester, was arraigned for playing the three card trick. P.C. 135 (Green) said he saw him playing with several others on Friday night in Back Bridge-st. His confederates got away. He gave several addresses and said he belonged to Liverpool. He was fined ss. and costs or seven days.—A man named Dougherty was charged with playing a game of chance with box and dice and was mulcted in 203. and costs. —John Williams, who said he came from Plank-lane, near Wigan, pleaded guilty to playing with box and dice. He said he was sorry that he had been caught. It was the first time, and if the magistrates would take pity on him it would be the. last. He was really sorry. The magistrates took a lenient view and let him off for 10s. and costs.—John McGrath,for a similar offence had to pay 203. and costs, because he was an old hand Ralph Rudd for heading pennies at is. a time was fined 203. and costs.—J. Rood was charged with attempting to steal a watch and chain of the value of £7, the pro- perty of James Garrity. Inspector Longworth said at two o'clock he heard a cry that a man had had his watch and chain stolen. He got through the crowd and into a tent where he saw the prosecutor and prisoner, the former saying that the latter, who had the watch in his possession, had stolen it. Prisoner denied that he , was the man, and he was remanded for a week. On Monday morning, before Colonel Wood ar.d Mr. C B. F. Horror), Henry Bailey was charged with tieing drunk and disorderly on Saturday night. P.C. Burns proved the case. Fined 23. 6.1. and cests.—James Ad- croft and George Woods, of Haslingden, were charged with playing pitch and toss on Haydock line. P.C.'s Green and Burns proved the case, and the defendans</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>LATE COLONEL IicCORQ UODALE.</head>
        <p>THE LATE COLONEL IicCORQ UODALE.</p>
        <p>THE FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>Amid great demonstrations of sorrow and respect the remains of the late Colonel McCorquodale, whose death took place on the 16ch inst. at Gadlys, his residence in Anglesey, were interred in Llansadwra churchyard, on Saturday, a funeral service being also held at the same hour at Newton-le-Willows. The remains were removed from Gadlys to the church 'early on Saturday morning by workmen on the estate, the bearers here, as at the subsequent and final service, being the gardeners at ,Gadlys, headed by Mr. Hughes, head gardener. The I coffin was of plain oak, and bore the simple inscription I —"George McCorquodale, taiirn May 10,1817 ; died July 16, 1895." On the removal of the body to the church a private service was held for the servants and family, at which the Rev. E. Evans, rector of Llaiasadwra, offi- ciated, assisted by the Rev. H. Sherlock, a son-in-law of the deceased gentleman. At one o'clock the public funeral service was held In Llansadwrn Church, conducted by the Revs. E. Evans and R. H. Williams, vicar of Llanfaethlu. The funeral was very largely attended,there being present, in addition to the relatives and friends of the deceased, representatives of the com- mercial undertakings he had founded, besides large numbers of his tenantry. The choristers of Bangor .Cathedral, under the leadership of Mr. Westlake Morgan, cathedral organist, were present at the church, and ren- dered the funeral hymns "Oa the Resurrection morn- ing." " Now the labourer's task is o'er," and" King of Love my Shepherd is." Among those present were the following :—Messrs. G. F. McCorquodale, A. C McCor- quodale, N. McCorquodale, H. McCorquodale and H. S.McCorquodale(sons),Mrs.McCorquodale,(widow),Mrs. Sherlock, Mrs. Greenshields, Mrs.Birley, Mrs.Blacklock, Mrs. Dugdeon, and Miss McCorquodale (daughters), Sir Charles E. Hamilton,Messrs. R. L.Greenshields, H. F. Birley, and J.H. Dudgeon (sons in-law), Messrs. A. T. Squarey, George Cox, R. Lockett, C. danderson, H. Blacklock, T. Comber, J. Gardiner, G. Patterson, H. Dudgeon, W. Black, P. Rathbone, Cross,William Treffos, Davies, Mills, Brocklebank, F. Lowry, Richardson, J. J. Cowan (Edinbuigh), R. Cowan (Valley-field), Charles Cropper (Kendal), Dr. Bickersteth (Brig-y-don), Messrs. R. R. Rathbone, Bulkeley-Price, C. R. Cox (Pdingarth), and W. T. Cross (Redhill). The following gentlemen were present as representing the business houses witb which the deceased was connected :—Messrs. D. Ailoss (London), Colonel F. J. Wood (Newton-le-Willows), Edward Totty and L. F. Hughes (Liverpool), and Mr. Hull )North Wales Paper Company). A large number of beautiful wreathe had been Sent by the following ladies and gentlemen :—Mr. G. F. McCorquodale, Mrs. Arthur, Lady Hamilton, Mrs. Hast- ings, Mrs. Blacklock, Mrs. Hume Dudgeon' from old servants Rossway, Mrs. Blacklock (Southpert) ; the secretary, counting-house, Cardington-st. ; Mr. and Mrs. Henry Dudgeon, Belgrave Hall ; servants, Rossway Gardens ; Mr. and Mrs. Culross, Mr. and Mrs. A. C. McCorquodale, Mr. and Mrs. Greenshields, Mary, Lady Vivian, " Little Children," Greenshields,Mr. and Mrs. J. Smith (Brookside, Newton-le-Willows), Mr. and Mrs. Sherlock, Mr. James Tinsley (Dareabury Hall), workers at Newton-le-Willows, Mr. and Mrs. F. I. Wood, Lieutenant-Colonel J. L. Wood and officers of the 6th V.B. The King's (Liverpool Regiment), the servants at. Gadlys and The Willows, Mr. and Mrs. Chadwick (Flaulfre, Anglesey), Messrs. McCorquodale's employes, Miss Molly McCorquodale (Wolverton), Con- stance Burton; the employes, Coleman-st., London, E.C.; the workmen at Glasgow. the workpeople at Card- ington-st., Mrs. Stone, the workmen of the North Wales Paper Company, the manager of the North Wales Paper Company, Mr. and Mrs. G. E. Pearson, the Rev. R. H. Williams, Mr. J. Gibson, Sir Myles and Lads, Fenton,the Leeds ernploses, the Gadlvs and Willows households and stables; the Hon. Claude Vivian, the Rev. H. Monk, Mr. and Mrs. William C. Jones, Mr. Fiugh Wagg, Mr. and Mrs. Patton M'Doticall, Mr. and Mrs. Comber, Mr. and Mrs. Gillespie, Sir Edmund and 'Lady Henderson Mrs. Horton, gardeners at The Willows, Mr. and Mrs. Henderson, Blacklock, MEMORIAL SERVICE AT NEWTON.</p>
        <p>On Saturday afternoon, about the time when the me- morial was taking place, at Llansadwrn, a short me- morial service was held at St. Peter's Church, Newton,a good congregation attending. Amongst those present were the Hon. T. W. and Mrs. Lngh, Messrs. C. B. F. Borron. J.P., W. Mitchell, sr., W. H. Arnott, J.P., A. Evans, Nuttall (L.eds), Rev. R. Smith, Dr. Elandyside, office staff, foremen and employes of the N.wton works, inmates ot the Liverpool Farm School Reformatory, in charge of Mr. Digweed, and others. The service was most impressive. As an opening voluntary"! know that my Redeemer liveth," was played on the organ by Mr. P. Fairclough, and then a portion of the funeral service was read by Rev. F. W. Jamstone, officiating minister. The hymns as appointed for the service at Llansadwrn church were sung, these being "On the resurrection morn," "Now the labourer's Cask is o'er," " The King of Life my Shepherd is." Psalm 90 was hanted, and as the congregation dispersed, Mr. Fairclough played the " Dead March in Saul. PULPIT REFERENCE.</p>
        <p>At the Parish Church of Newton-in-Makerfield, War- grave on Sunday morning, the Rev. J. Carson, M.A., preaching from Acts 13, v. 36, " David after he had served his own generation, by the will of God fell on sleep," referred to the late Colonel McCorquodale as fallen on sleep, after he had served his own generation. A great captain of industry, gifted by his Maker with rare ability for business, quick to see his opportunity,diUrnt in the pursuit of an honourable call_ ing•, such was their late neighbour. Frorri little things he had proceeded to greater, building up so great a business, and affording employment here and in several other places, to a multitude of both sexes young and old and all this whilst maintaining the most friendly rela- tions with all who served him, mindful of whose welfare, as well as of his own he continually sought after their further employment. All who knew this excellent man would doubtless testify, and he remained unharmed by his great prosperity, becoming neither proud, haughty, nor ostentatious, but always a kind and friendly person, winning the respect and esteem of those associated with him. In the midst of pressing business he gave up valu- able time to the discharge of public duty as a citizen and a magistrate, besides attending to the calls of religion and charity. Many, for instance, were his visits to the Boy's Reformatory, near his home, and of whose manag- ing Committee he was long an active member, whilst great was his kindly interest in its inmates,and he acted up to the severest rule of alms giving" Let not thy left hand know what thy right hand doeth, that thine alms may be in secret," as well as that great Christian pre- cept " with love serve one another." A special visit to the Rectory was gratefully remembered for the warm good wishes then expressed, when he (the preacher) entered upon his present position of responsibility, but the secret of such a career must have been the conscious- ness that it was the will of God, that he should during a lone life " serving his own generation," and not only those present, but also make future generations so far as human ken went, would be served by their late neigh- bour, because he diligently used such gifts of skill and industry as God committed to his trust. At the close of the service, the orrmnist sMr. E. Leyland) played the dead march, the congregation standing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>NEWTON DIVISION.</head>
        <p>NEWTON DIVISION.</p>
        <p>TH E ELECT f ON.</p>
        <p>The polling for a candidate to represent Newton Division took place on Monday. The various poll- ing booths, of which on this occasion there was a large number, owing to the voting being carried out in wards, opened precisely at 8 o'clock, in very inclement weather so far as Earlestown and New- ton art concerned. Consequently the polling was not lose any .naeans brisk, and the booths until well on into the Morning wore a deserted appearance. About. noon there was a decided improvement in the weather, and the workers were enabled to carry out their duties under much pleasanter circumstances. The various Osinmittee rooms were plentifully bespattered with election literature, the latest effusion being a con- tradiction from the secretaries of the comic football match given in aid of the late collier's strike of the Rev. E. W. W.Kaye's letter anent the relief funds. The Hon. T. W. Legh accompanied by Mrs. Legh was early astir, and proceeded on a tour of the division. Mr. Moon ac- companied by Mr. Rawlins appeared somewhat later and followed Mr. Legh in his peregrinatioas. There was a plentiful display of party colours, but ew- int: to the scattered position Of the booths,there was not- at any time anything approaching a crowd, nor excite- ment, and the election from beginninz to end was con- ducted in a most orderly and peaceful manner. Of course, it was felt on all hand that to reduce the Tory majority VMS a difficult task, altheuarh some hope was entertained that this might he accomplished. The result, ' however, showed that these hopes were illusory, and no matter whatever may be said to the contrary there can- not he the slightest doubt that so far as the colliers are concerned, the contest was fought on the trade quest thin. The colliers both Liberal and Tory admit tha- , this was the principle on which they voted,their idea be- I</p>
        <p>big that if the Tory was elected there would be an im- provement in trade, and anybody with an experience of colliers knows well that when they get an idea of this sort it is useless to try to convince them otherwise. At eight o'clock the poll closed,and the boxes were straight- way conveyed to the Town Hall at Newton, where the counting was conducted. A large number of people anxious to hear the result followed, and assembled in the open space in front of the Town Halt.l As time wore on the numbers increased, and by 10 o'clock there could not have been less than 2,000 or 3,000 people present, who amused themselves by altern- ately cheering and hooting the names of the candidates. Mr. Appleton, of Wigan, officiated as returning officer, and the counting of the votes was proceeded with as the boxes came in, a large staff of counters haviug been engaged for the purpose. Precisely at 1055, the press were admitted into the room, and the pcll was then de- clared to those present. Mr. Appleton said the number of ballot papers were 9,237, and out of these 25 were spoilt. He declared the numbers cast to be for Legh 5,358 Moon</p>
        <p>-- Majority At the announcement of the figures there was much cheering. Mr. Legh afterwards moved a vote of thanks to the returning officer, which Mr. Moon seconded. Mr. Appleton afterwards went to one of the Town Hall win- dows, and after order had been restored, announced the result which was received with cheering and hooting. Legh then took his place at the window, and ad- dressing tkose below said : Electors of the Newton Division. You have for the third time done me the honour of returning me as your representative, on this occasion by a largely increased majority (Cheers.) But while lam deeply gratified at the size of this majority, I cannot affect surprise at the result. (Cheers.) When- ever a Government sets itself to act in defiance of the ordinary aides of justice and commonsense, I anticipate that the electors of the Newton Division will always resent that conduct in a similar manner. The remark- able triumph which we have achieved is one of principle. but I also recognise the fact that it is also due to the admirable organisation which exists in this division, and to the assistance which the Unionist cause has received from all classes. (Cheers.) I must agai-I express the honour which I feel of being returned for the third time as your representative. I congratulate the electors of this division for the straightforward manner in which the election has been fought, and while again thanking them for their support, I trust that I shall do nothing in the future which is likely in anyway to forfeit their con- fidence. Once more do I venture to thank them for achieving a notable triumph on behalf of the Unionist cause. (Loud cheers.) Mr. Moon then stepped forward, and was received with vociferous cheering. He said : Liberals of Newton. I cannot but express my regret that we have been de- feated by such a large majority, but I feel none the less deeply grateful to all the kind friends who have given me such magnificent help and encouragement. I have never regretted accepting the candidature for the New- ton Division, and feel just as proud of that position at this moment as I ever did. (Cheers.) I shall always bear pleasant memories of this contest, and sincerely trust that Newton will yet give its adherence to the Liberal cause. It is in my opinion Liberal at heart, and the opinions of the Newton Liberals are swamped by out-voters who have very little stake in the division. Under a better system of registration, Newton will yet be won for the Liberal party, and may that be soon. (Cheers.)</p>
        <p>The vast crowd then began to move off, but the ap- proaches to the Town Hall remained besieged Isy a very large number of people, who were kept back by a staff of police under Superintendents Higginbottom and Bras- sington, and Inspector Morgan. As Mr. Moon made his exit, an attempt was made to shoulder him by his ad- mirers, hut the police succeeded in preventing it. When Mr. Legh made his appearance a like attempt was made, but in this case the police again foiled the rush, and Mr. Legh with his supporters got safely away to the gentle- man's club in Park-road. Here about a couple of hundred admirers surrounded the entrance and refused to go away. They amused themselves by giving three cheers and groans for almost everybody, and loudly called for a " three gallon cask," but the beverage was not forth- coming. Seeing that they would not be denied, Mr. Legh came oat, and delivered a short address, after which the crewd dispersed evidently satisfied. On the declaration of the poll a large quantity of fireworks were let off, and until nearly midnight rockets were being sent up. We might here state that 645 votes more were polled than in 1892.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>THANKING THE WORKERS.</head>
        <p>THANKING THE WORKERS.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday evening Mr. Legh paid a vi—sit to the Con- servative Club, the object being to thank the workers who had assisted in securing the large majority of 1,504, Mr. C. B. F. Borron, J.P., chairman of the Earlestewn Polling District, presided over a crowded gathering of workers and supporters of the Unionist cause. 0 wing to the serious illness of the steward of the club, Mr.J. Roscoe,by the express wish of the Chair- man and Mr. Legh there was no demonstration. Speeches of congratulation to Mr. Legh and the Union- ist of the Newton Division were made by Messrs. W. Mills, J'lllle3 Wright, William Southern, J. B. Oubridge, and others.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>MIDNIGHT ASSAULT ON A WAR:</head>
        <p>A MIDNIGHT ASSAULT ON A WAR:</p>
        <p>RINGTON PUBLICAN.</p>
        <p>A STRANGE CASE.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Borough Police Court, on Wednes- day, loft 4re Messrs. John Fairclough, J. Geddes and J.H. Fleming, Thomas Beswick and Herbert Cork, both of Buttermarket-st., were charged in custody, having been aporp.hended on a warrant, with having assaulted Thomas Jackson, the licensee of the Rose Inn, Dial-st. There was a cross-summons by Cork against Jackson for assault. Mr. Percy Davies appeared for Jackson, and Mr. Browne defended.</p>
        <p>In opening his case Mr. Davies said the complainant was also employed as a glassmaker by Messrs. Robinson. On Tuesday morning at 12.40 he was going to his work, and when passing the "Old Fox "Inn, Buttermarket.st. he noticed two men standing by one of the windows of the two shops. He turned round and one of the men,the tall one (Cork, manager for the Express Billposting Co.) shouted "Thou had better come back and see what we are after." Complainant returned a few steps and Cork then deliberately struck him with his fist on the side of his face. Complainant attempted to defend him- self when the smaller man (Beswick) rushed in and Cork struck him another blow in the face which felled him to the ground. Beswick then kicked him both on the back of his head—causing a deep cut—and on the bridge of the nose which was also damaged. The black eye which was so conspicuous, complainant was not sure hew it was done, but he had no disfigurement before the attack. Every time he at- tempted to get up the little man pummelled him while Cork held his hat and did not touch complainant while on the floor. The latter shouted at the top of his voice, " Police !" police !" and he was heard by Mr. Walsh, landlord of the" Crown and Sceptre" and his wife. They went to the scene of the cries and found complain- ant lyimg on the floor. Mr. Walsh remarked to Beswick "Don't be such a coward, he is old enough to be your father." Mr. Wainwright, hairdresser, also came upon the scene. These were the facts of Mr. Davies' case and he hoped theh Wnrsbips would visit the offence with a very severe punishment indeed. Mr. Davies added'that his client did not know either of defendants and had net spoken to them before, so there was no provoca- tion.</p>
        <p>Thomas Jackson, complainant, in corroborating his solicitor, said that defendants never gave him a chance to defend himself.</p>
        <p>By Mr, Browne: He never made use of filthy lan- guage and insinuations as he passed defendants. He did not lift his fit and strike Cork on the mouth,neither did be know whether he scratched his cheek. He did not strike Beswiek.</p>
        <p>Mr. Thomas Walsh stated that in consequence of the noise on the night in question be and his wife went out and saw two men rolling about in the street. They were Beswick and Jackson and the former was on top pummelling the latter. Jackson was quite helpless at the time but afterwards managed to get to his feet covered with blood. He was explaining the matter to witness when Beswiek rushed at him again and knocked him on his back in the street without any provocation. Witness than took eff his jacket and protected Mr. Jack- son. He had a slight idea that he had something to do with Beswick's black-eye which was attributed to Jackson.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Walsh and Mr. Wain wright gave similar evi- dence, the latter adding that Jackson cried out " Is these Englishmen." Detective Sergeant Monahan deposed that he arrested the two prisoners on Tuesday morning when Beswick remarked "Jackson brought it en, himself," while Cork said "I stood in Carter's doorway out the way." P.C. Clark deposed that he heard the disturbance from Bridge-street, and on going up saw the two prisoners and Jackson. Jackson made a complainant in the hear- ina of prisoners,Cork heing afterwards found by witness in the recess of Carter's doorway. Mr. Browne for the defence contended that the com- plainant's statement was not common sense or at all probable. For provocation he Said that Jackson turned round and looked suspiciously at the prisoners on the night in question which drew from them the remark quoted. Jacksgm, he said, then used foul language and strii.lk the first biota. They denied that there was any kicking. He submitted that the men ought never to have been arrested on a warrant as they were respectable and it was a case of six of one and half-dozen of the other. He applied for a dismissal. Cork and Beswick corroborated. The Chairman said they were of the opinion that the disturbance commenced through a misunderstanding and as far as Cork was concerned he would be dismissed and also tie cross-summons. Beswick would be fined 103. and costs or 14 days' hard labour.</p>
