      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-07-27_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>PARR'S BANKING COMPANY AND ALLIANCE BANK, LIMITED.</head>
        <p>PARR'S BANKING COMPANY AND ALLIANCE BANK, LIMITED.</p>
        <p>MEETING AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>LORD PLAYFAIR ON THE STATE CF TRADE.</p>
        <p>An extraordinary general meeting of the shareholders of the Parr's Banking Co., and Alliance Bank, Limited. was held at the bank premises, Winwick-st., on Thurs- day at noon. The chair was occupied by Lord Playteir, and there were also present Sir Thomas G. Frost,Messrs. Cecil F. Parr' deputy chairman,T. H. Dixon, John Dun, N. if. Eckersley, A. Hewlett, H. W. Meade-King, P. Stubs, and A. Turner (directors), also Messrs. James Robinson, J. Highfield, Dr. White,Rowland Young, J. H. ' Keane, H. Milling, W. Owen, J. C. Stead, J. Hutchins, H. Davies C. J. Whitley, J. C. Parr, A. G. Gray, W. Silcock, j. A. MacGregor, H. Dean, E. Connor, Dr. Bower, Amos Hanson, R. Pierpoint,AL P., Jahn Murray, David Garnett, and John Hartley. Alter the notice convening the meeting had been read by Mr. Jenne DON, Tue CHAIRMAN rose to move the adoption of the report which we published in our last issue. Be said that he assumed they would take the report as read as they had it in their hands. He would ask them to allow him to express his personal gratification at meeting the coun- try shareholders. It was a great pleasure to see their late chairman (Mr. Tarner) back again,looking so young and vigorous after his severe illness. (Applause.) It was also a great pleasure considering the name of the bank, to see present one of their past chairmen, Mr. J. C. Parr—(applause),—who was always ready to give counsel and aid to the bank when his advice was asked. He has also great pleasure in seeing sitting by his side, Mr. Cecil Parr, their deputy cnair- man, who was chairman of all the commit- tees, and had shown great activity and energy in every affair connected with the bank. It was only the modesty of Mr. Cecil Parr which pre- vented him being chairman in his (Lord Playfair'e) stead. Mr. Parr had without his knowledge proposed and carried him (the speaker) as chairman, in his absence and it was because he thought an older man should take the plaeo of chairman a the bank, and it was only in pre-eminence in age that he (Lord Play fair) could really claim the right to occupy that position, and no in respect of experience, knowledge or ability. He would express his gratification at seeing his friends on that occasion. He was expressing the great regret and sorrow in which they would all join when he mentioned that since their last meeting they had lost one ot their directors, Colonel McCorquodale, who since 1878 had been a member of the Board. He was always a zealous and active director, and he was sure they would all mourn the death and their loss on that occasion. (Hear, hear.) To come to the business of the meeting, he had no doubt that considering the state of trade and the de- pressed condition of the money market, they would have had some misgivings as to the dividends this year. The rates of interest were very low and it was sometimes difficult for the directors and managers to invest money put at their disposal with sufficient profit for the bank. But although it had been difficult sometimes to deal with the money, they had been able to maintain their divi- dend. The branch banks had as usual been active .and careful in the discharge of their duties, and thel London banks were becoming better known to the general London public, and there was a steady growth of the resources put at their disposal. He would give them the figures as to the increase in the resources placed at their disposal in the form of current deposit accounts and circular notes since 1892, when the head quarters of the bank migrated to Lend in. In 1892, the current deposit accounts, and circular notes amouated to £11,524,643, and in 1895 to £14,807,754, or during three years, there had been increased resources of £3,283,111. (Applause.) As to the future, whether there were indications of a better time or not,he would like to give some facts so that they would form their own judgment. During the past few years there had been a remarkable falling of the money which was used for new industrial undertakings, new manufae- tories, new mines, new railways, new banks. In 1890, there were used in this company, £143,000,000 sterling. In 1891 it fell to £10h,000,000, in 1892, it fell to £51,000,000, and they came last to 1893, when there were £49,000,000 invested. That was the turning point of badness, and he 1894, it began to go up , again, for in that year there were invested £92,000,000 and for the first half year of 1895, the growth of public confidence had been so considerable that it had risen to £55,000,000, and therefore they might confidently ex- pect that this year, they would at least be in the posi- of 1891, In some cases, trade had shown greatly in- creased activity such as the woollen industries and building trades. These had shown largely increased activity during the past year. There were many other trades that were rather living in hope than in realised results. On the whole there was greater employment, and apparently the tendency was to steadily increetsed employment. If they took the figures of the trades unions, they would find that at this time last year, they had 6.3 per cent, of their members out of employment. /n January of this year, the. had 5.6 per cent, only, so that gradually the employment was becoming better. They might congratulate themselves in Lancashire that the cotton trade was largely improved, although it was not progressing as rapidly as they could wish. What were their grounds of confidence that they would have a better time ? Perhaps they would allow him to say that he was very intimately acquainted with the United States. Over 18 years he hal spent in the United States,and he was acquainted with all the leading manu- facturers, the great capitalists, the great bankers and great statesmen. He had always watched very closely what was the state of the United States, because he always found that whatever was happening there would influence here. He therefore watched the United States with great interest. The prospects there were very good at the present moment with regard to tre to. (Ap- plause.) The cotton crop was magnificent, and if it was not injured by the frosts in September, they might look forward with confidence to a valuable crap of cotton. He had discussed the question of trade recently with one of the greatest bankers of New York,and that gentle- man said that as far as he could judge, there appeared to bP a time of prosperity for the United States coming if they could only get rid of some of their—to use a Scotch term—" daft" views about currency—he was not speaking of bimetallism. If he were not ton sanguine of the future, their very able manager, Mr. Dun, and his associates in the management would have a less anxious time in the next half year than they bed had in the past. He hoped his prediction would be realised. (Apnlanse.) The fieuree in the report spoke for themselyee. In main- taining thcir dividend he considered it very satisfactory. They would notice that there was a little reduction in the amount of the investments. That Wag not from loss of trade, hut owing to the purchase of other concerns which it was necessary to join to them, and which would add to their prosperity. The branch banks were doing admirably; and he expressed his pleasure that the direc- tors could give such a good report. He moved that the report be received and adopted, and that a dividend of ' 19 per cent, per annum free of income tax he declared for the quarter ending 30'h June, 1895, payable on and after August lst,and a dividend at the same rate for the 18th September next payable on and after November Ist to shareholders whose names shall be registered on Oc- toher 17th. (Applause.) _ -</p>
        <p>Mr. CECIL PARR seconded the motion, and said that le was glad they had for their chairman one who had a world-wide reputation, rather than an ordinary person, even if his name was Parr. (Laughter.) The motion was carried unanimously. Mr. J. CHARLTON PARR said that it was again his privilege to move a resolution. It was a p'easore to him to meet again in the old headqnarters of the hank. (Applause.) Re Was sure that he expressed the feeling of every local shareholder when he off reel a hearty welcome to the chairman on his first snnesranee in what he might call the cradle of Parr's Banking Com- pany. (Applause.) Sunshine in this world was se'dom without its corresponding shadow, and he felt great sorrow at the absence of one who scarcely I ever omitted to join the meetings in that room. He alluded of course to the late Colonel McCorquodale whose death they all deeply deplored. In him the bank bad lost a wioe coun- cillnr, a staunch supporter, and a very good friend. With regard to the dividend, he was sure that consider- ing that there was so much keen competition, the fact that it had not diminished, showed that the position and prospects of the bank were very satisfactory. After providing for all doubtful debts and rebating current bills 5 per cent, though the bank rate was 2 per and the market rate considerably below, the directors were able to recommend a dividend of 19 per cent, per annum. In this they were deeply indebted to the gentlemen to whose good management and great attention they had this result. He therefore proposed that a hearty vote of thanks be accorded to the directors for their services during the past year. Mr. WILLIAM OWEN seconded, and it was passed unanimously. Mr. TURNER proposed a vote of thanks to the general manager and other officials for their services during the past year. Mr. R. PIERPOINT, M.P., seconded. He mid a tribute to the good management of the bank, and said that he would suggest that the name of the bank be altered to suit the convenience of those who had to write it. It was gratifying to hear the chairman speak with such confidence that the state of trade was improving. He suggested that there was another reason why there should be a return of confidence throughout the country, and that was the struggle which so many of them had just been going through. (Laughter.) That might be another item which caused confidence in this country, and perhaps the world at large. Lord PLAYFAIR said that he must add his testimony and his great admiration for the singular ability, experi- ence, energy, and activity of the general manager Mr. Dun. It was largely due to him that there was such a large dividend. There was one word he must Say. Re Was not going to talk politics, but he must tell Mr. Pier- Point that the change in prosperity, and the increase in investments came last year. (Laughter.) in 1893, the investments were £49,000.000 for new undertakings,,,i in 1894 they had pt to £92,000,000. That was a good thing and he as a man on the opposite ride appreciated it. (Laughter.) The vote of thanks was passed unanimously. Mr. Joins DUN thanked the directors on behalf of him</p>
        <p>self and his colleagues. He said that he was not going to proph.sy as to the future, because they never knew what might happen. While they could not prophesy, they coukl hope. Mr.Hurcanis moved a vote of thanks to the Chairman for his presence that day. He considered it an honour to the bank that they had such a distinguished gentle- man as Chairman. Mr. DEAN seconded the motion, which was Ca unanimously. Lord PLAYEAIR in reply expressed his pleasure they had such a beautiful bank in Warrington. He I that the increasing prosperity of Warrington make the bank too small for the requirements town. That was not the first time he had perf( public function in Warrington. A great many —in 1845—he was one nf the Commissioners to ing the sites of large towns throughout Engla counties given to him to report upon were 4 and Cheshire. He well remembered Warriu,' was a great pleasure to him to see the and to see the marked improvement in it. pressed the hope that the town of W. increase in prosperity that the bank for the business required. (Appla The meeting then terminated.</p>
